  "raw": {
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Expression-of-Interest---UQ-Temps-Administration-Opportunities_R-41821-4": {
      "title": "Expression of Interest - UQ Temps Administration Opportunities",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-06-12\nCasual\n\nUQ Temps: Connects skilled professionals with a variety of casual opportunities across University of Queensland Opportunities : Various casual opportunities Casual Hourly Rate: Minimum $51 - $67 per hour + super Locations: St Lucia Campus, Herston Campus, Dutton Park Campus Explore Casual Opportunities with UQ Temps Are you looking for flexible, short-term work where you can contribute your skills in a dynamic and collaborative environment? UQ Temps connects talented professionals with a diverse range of casual opportunities across the University of Queensland. These roles provide a unique opportunity to gain experience across various teams while building your professional network. We are currently seeking expressions of interest for a range of casual opportunities, including: General Administration Executive Assistant support Student and Academic Administration Research Administration Junior Web Development About This Opportunity Working as part of the UQ Temps casual talent pool, you will support teams across the University to deliver high-quality administrative and operational outcomes. These roles are varied and fast-paced, offering exposure to different functions and stakeholders across UQ’s vibrant community. Your responsibilities may vary depending on the role but can include: Managing calendars, coordinating meetings and arranging travel Supporting committees and meetings, including agenda preparation and minute-taking Maintaining administrative systems and managing competing priorities Conducting research and data analysis to support projects and reporting Liaising with internal and external stakeholders in a professional manner Assisting with website updates and development using Drupal (for relevant roles) About You We are looking for adaptable and motivated professionals who bring: Demonstrated experience in administration, business support or executive assistance Strong interpersonal and written communication skills High levels of discretion and professionalism when handling sensitive information Excellent organisational and problem-solving abilities A proactive mindset with the ability to thrive in fast-paced environments Flexibility and a willingness to support a range of teams and functions A sound understanding of administrative systems and processes Experience with Drupal or web development (for web-focused opportunities) About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world. Everyone here has a role to play. As a member of our professional staff cohort, you will be actively involved in working towards our vision of a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits such as: Access to exclusive internal opportunities Ability to quickly build organisational knowledge UQ benefits such as vaccination programs, EAP, fitness passport, and more Networking opportunities In addition, the following mandatory requirements apply: Work Rights:  You must maintain your own unrestricted work rights in Australia whilst you work for UQ. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.  Background Checks:  All final applicants for this position may be asked to consent to a criminal record check. Please note that people with criminal records are not automatically barred from applying for this position. Each application will be considered on its merits.    Questions?   For more information about this opportunity and application queries, please contact uqtemps@uq.edu.au  stating the job reference number in the subject line.   For more information about UQ Temps, please visit UQ Temps - Candidates . Want to Apply?   All applicants must upload the following documents in order for your application to be considered: Cover letter addressing the ‘About You’ section, including preferred working location and role.   Resume  Please note that you will be asked to add all documents into the one upload box labelled ‘resume’, which is step one of the application form. Our strength as an institution lies in our diverse colleagues. We're dedicated to  equity, diversity, and inclusion , fostering an environment that mirrors our wider community. We're committed to attracting, retaining, and promoting diverse talent, particularly from Australia’s First Nations Peoples. If you require an alternative method to submit your application due to accessibility needs or personal circumstances, please contact  talent@uq.edu.au .     Other Information    UQ is committed to a fair, equitable and inclusive selection process, which recognises that some applicants may face additional barriers and challenges which have impacted and/or continue to impact their career trajectory. Candidates who don’t meet all criteria are encouraged to apply and demonstrate their potential. The selection panel considers both potential and performance relative to opportunities when assessing suitability for the role.   Applications will be reviewed as received. Candidates may be interviewed prior to the job closing date. We encourage candidates to apply as soon as possible. The University of Queensland reserves the right to close this application process early."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/UQ-Indigenous-Graduate-Program---Identified--s105-_R-52069-2": {
      "title": "Indigenous Graduate Program - Identified (s105)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-01-13\nFull time\n\nOffice of the Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Indigenous Engagement (ODVCIE) These positions are only open to Australian Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander people. We have multiple full-time fixed-term graduate positions for up to two years with a salary of $83,776 (HEW 5) plus a generous super allowance of up to 17%. A fantastic opportunity for recent graduates! The UQ Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Graduate Program is an opportunity to accelerate your career and receive hands-on experience at a world-leading university. Based at our picturesque and conveniently located St Lucia campus Kickstart your career. Shape the future. Stay connected to community. Are you a current student about to graduate, or have you graduated within the past three years? The Indigenous Graduate Program is your pathway to professional growth, cultural empowerment, and meaningful impact. About This Opportunity  The Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Graduate Program accelerates careers by building on your bachelor’s degree with tailored practical skill development. This program is designed to equip you with the essential skills to succeed in your chosen career, providing a unique opportunity to leverage your potential into a fulfilling professional journey. Why Join? Tailored career development: Structured training, mentoring, and leadership pathways designed for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander graduates. Cultural safety and belonging: Work in an environment that values your identity, knowledge, and lived experience. Competitive salary & benefits: $83,776 (HEW 5) plus 17% superannuation, with flexible work arrangements. Community connection: Opportunities to contribute to UQ’s Indigenous Engagement Strategy and strengthen ties with community. If you’ve graduated within the last three years, this program is designed to help you take the next step in your career journey. Please click here to watch Edwin Dyson (graduate of the Vice-Chancellor's Indigenous Graduate Program) talk about his experience with the program . For further information, please click here to view the information booklet. Why Work at UQ? The University of Queensland is recognised as one of Australia’s leading institutions, offering staff access to world‑class facilities, flexible work arrangements, and a vibrant campus community. As part of UQ, you’ll benefit from professional development opportunities, mentoring, and a supportive environment that values diversity and inclusion. UQ’s Reconciliation Action Plan (RAP) and Indigenous Employment Strategy guide our commitment to creating culturally safe workplaces and increasing Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander representation across the university. By joining this program, you’ll be part of a community that actively works towards reconciliation, respects cultural knowledge, and supports Indigenous staff to thrive. About You  The University of Queensland considers that the filling of this position constitutes an equal opportunity measure under s105 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1991(Qld) and under section 8(1) of the Racial Discrimination Act 1975 (Cth). The positions are therefore only open to Australian Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander people. The successful candidate will be required to provide evidence to confirm that they are an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person. You may be required to complete background checks including, criminal history and education checks. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. Share With Your Community If this opportunity isn’t the right fit for you, we warmly encourage you to share it with your family, friends, and community networks. Helping spread the word ensures that more Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander graduates can access this pathway to professional growth and contribute to UQ’s vision for reconciliation and inclusion. About UQ At UQ, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people are supported to fill their cultural responsibilities, with access to 8 additional days (pro-rata) of paid Cultural Leave annually. Indigenous employees are encouraged to access the UQ Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Staff Network with a variety of significant events held throughout the year.  As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world.   Everyone here has a role to play. As a member of our professional staff cohort, you will be actively involved in working towards our vision of a better world. By supporting the academic endeavour across teaching, research, and the student life, you will have the opportunity to contribute to activities that have a lasting impact on our community.  Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available, such as:  26 weeks paid parental leave or 14 weeks paid primary caregiver leave, 17% superannuation contributions, 17.5% annual leave loading. Access to flexible working arrangements including hybrid working options, flexible start/finish times, purchased leave, and a condensed fortnight. Health and wellness discounts – fitness passport access, free yearly flu vaccinations, discounted health insurance, and access to our Employee Assistance Program for staff and their immediate family. Cheap parking, On campus childcare and Salary packaging options. Want to Apply?   All applicants must upload the following documents in order for your application to be considered: Cover letter addressing how your degree and area of interest align with your preferred career pathway at UQ.   Resume  Questions? Please reach out to the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Employment team on  indigenous_employment@uq.edu.au to discuss. Applications will be reviewed as they are received, so we encourage you to submit yours as soon as possible to begin the process. Job Reference Number - R-52069 UQ is committed to a fair, equitable and inclusive selection process, which recognises that some applicants may face additional barriers and challenges which have impacted and/or continue to impact their career trajectory. Candidates who don’t meet all criteria are encouraged to apply and demonstrate their potential. The selection panel considers both potential and performance relative to opportunities when assessing suitability for the role.  We know one of our strengths as an institution lies in our diverse colleagues. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion , fostering an environment that mirrors our wider community. We're committed to attracting, retaining, and promoting diverse talent. Reach out to talent@uq.edu.au for accessibility support or adjustments.  If you are a current employee (including casual staff and HDR scholars) or hold an unpaid/affiliate appointment, please login to your staff Workday account and visit the internal careers board to apply for this opportunity. Please do NOT apply via the external job board."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Casual-Security-Officers--St-Lucia-_R-61365-1": {
      "title": "Casual Security Officers (St Lucia)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-17\nCasual\n\nProperty and Facilities Casual rate of $46.47 per hour plus 12% Super Multiple Casual opportunities based at St Lucia Campus About This Opportunity As a Casual Security Officer, you will provide professional and responsive security services across UQ's St Lucia Campus. The St Lucia security team supports a large-scale 24/7 operation, with officers responding to incidents, conducting patrols, securing buildings, assisting staff, students and visitors, monitoring security systems, and supporting emergency responses through the University's Emergency Operations Centre. You will play an important role in maintaining a safe and welcoming campus environment while delivering high quality customer service and exercising sound judgement in a dynamic operational setting. Key responsibilities include: Responding to alarms, medical emergencies, fire emergencies and other incidents. Conducting campus patrols and securing University buildings and grounds. Providing access to authorised personnel. Monitoring security systems and supporting operations within the Emergency Operations Centre. Responding professionally to enquiries and concerns from staff, students and visitors. Escalating urgent or high-priority matters to the Security Supervisor. Supporting the enforcement of parking and traffic regulations. Conducting preliminary investigations as directed. Preparing clear, detailed and accurate incident reports. Performing other reasonable duties as directed by the security leadership team. About You You will be a calm, reliable and professional security officer who can confidently make decisions in a busy operational environment and provide exceptional service to a diverse university community. To be eligible, you must hold: A current Queensland Certificate II in Security Operations, including Security Officer Unarmed, Crowd Control, Bodyguard and Monitoring units. HLTAID011 Provide First Aid. HLTAID009 Provide Cardiopulmonary Resuscitation (CPR). A current Queensland manual open driver licence. You will also demonstrate: Well-developed knowledge of security methodologies and practices. Strong interpersonal and communication skills. Confidence using security monitoring systems and related technologies. The ability to prepare accurate reports and maintain appropriate records. Sound judgement and effective decision-making skills. The ability to work independently and as part of a 24/7 operational team. Professionalism, tact and restraint when managing challenging situations. Experience responding to emergency or operational incidents. A genuine commitment to providing a safe and welcoming campus environment. Important Training Requirement Successful applicants must be available to attend an initial five-week period of full-time training. Following completion of training, any work offered will be casual and based on operational requirements. The training requirement does not mean that ongoing full-time hours will be available. The successful candidate will be required to complete background checks including verification of their right to work in Australia and a criminal history check. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you'll have the opportunity to work alongside some of the brightest minds from around the world, contributing to a culture of excellence, collaboration and innovation. Working at UQ means joining an organisation committed to creating a better future through education, research and community impact. Employees also enjoy access to a range of benefits and rewards, including exclusive internal career opportunities and affordable parking options. Questions? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Mr Andrew Barling at a.barling@pf.uq.edu.au .  For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number in the subject line. Want to Apply? All applicants must upload the following document for their application to be considered: Resume Other Information UQ is committed to a fair, equitable and inclusive selection process, which recognises that some applicants may face additional barriers and challenges that have impacted, or continue to impact, their career trajectory. Candidates who do not meet all criteria are encouraged to apply and demonstrate their potential. The selection panel considers both potential and performance relative to opportunity when assessing suitability for the role. We know one of our strengths as an institution lies in our diverse colleagues. We're dedicated to equity, diversity and inclusion, fostering an environment that reflects our wider community. We're committed to attracting, retaining and promoting diverse talent. Reach out to talent@uq.edu.au  for accessibility support or adjustments. #LI-DNI"
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Casual-Research-Phlebotomists---Tackle-Concussion-Trial_R-65434-1": {
      "title": "Casual Research Phlebotomists – Tackle Concussion Trial",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-06-21\nCasual\n\nQueensland Brian Institute Multiple casual positions available at HEW Level 4 The hourly casual rate will range from $51.24 - $54.33 plus 12% Superannuation Administratively based at our St Lucia campus however travel will be required to schools and study sites across Brisbane About This Opportunity    We are seeking motivated and reliable Research Phlebotomists , with previous experience in the collection of bio-samples (blood, saliva, finger-prick), to support the UQ Concussion Study at our world-leading Queensland Brian Institute on a casual basis. The UQ Concussion Study is based on a partnership between World Rugby, Rugby Australia, the Great Public Schools Association (GPS) and focuses on developing a biomarker for concussion diagnosis and its recovery. The study will recruit participants from schools in South-East Queensland, and a multitude of data elements such as demographics, cognitive data, and bio-specimen samples, will be collected from participants at different timepoints. In this role, you will provide support to school staff and participants at participating schools. Key duties will include bio-sample collection including phlebotomy, record keeping, facilitating data collection processes at schools and providing support to the study sites when needed. These are casual positions and as such, work hours will vary from week to week depending on participant availability. Please note, due to the nature of the study, applicants will need to be able to work flexible out of hours and/or on Saturdays during the GPS rugby season (May to September). Applicants must also have access to their own reliable transport to travel to study sites. About You  Ideal candidates will be reliable, organised and able to work self-directed in a busy environment. You must be able to work with school staff and adolescent participants in a respectful and supportive manner, and you will be able to provide clear and easy-to-follow instructions regarding all study procedures to both school staff and participants/parents as required. In addition, you will possess: Certificate III in Pathology Collection (HLT37215) or an equivalent Australian recognised qualification. Previous training and experience in phlebotomy is essential. Conducting venepuncture on adolescents would be highly regarded. Training in all other study procedures will be provided. Excellent interpersonal skills and the ability to work autonomously i.e. flexibility to attend study sites at short notice, work autonomously and in small teams with a wide range of varied stakeholders. Strong organisational skills i.e. ability to keep organised records, and report self-directed, in timely manner. Proficient in the use of the Microsoft office suite products including Word, Excel and Outlook. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Prof Fatima Nasrallah at f.nasrallah@uq.edu.au or Dr Alex Kanon at a.kanon@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number ( R-65434 ) in the subject line.     When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns with the ‘About You’ selection criteria above. Other Information As a casual employee, and in line with Fair Work, please note that there is no commitment to ongoing work in this position.  Pre‑employment checks will include verification of the right to work in Australia (employer-sponsored work rights are not available for these appointments), mandatory immunisations and a Working with Children Blue Card. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.  We are dedicated to equity, diversity and inclusion and recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ. We encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We are happy to support accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Application reference number: R-65434 Please note applications will be reviewed as received. Candidates may be interviewed prior to the job closing date. We encourage candidates to apply as soon as possible. The University of Queensland reserves the right to close this application process early.   #LI-DNI"
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Gatton-Campus/Casual-Associate-Animal-Technician_R-60694": {
      "title": "Casual Associate Animal Technician",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-02-02\nCasual\n\nQueensland Animal Science Precinct (QASP) Join our casual pool for a range of associate animal technician opportunities   Competitive hourly rate of $44.67 , plus 11.5% superannuation Based at our Gatton Campus   About This Opportunity    We are seeking reliable and committed Associate Animal Technicians to join our casual pool, providing essential care and husbandry for research animals at the Queensland Animal Science Precinct (QASP). This role ensures the welfare of animals while supporting general facility operations, including the use of equipment, chemicals, and other materials. You'll contribute to maintenance tasks and the daily functionality of the QASP, working under routine or general supervision.    These casual roles are located at the Gatton Campus and may require some work outside of normal working hours, including participation on the weekend/public holiday roster.    Duties and Responsibilities Include:  Animal Husbandry   Provide care and welfare for livestock, ensuring clean housing and maintaining biosecurity.  Perform record-keeping and assist with research sampling and euthanasia as needed.  Operational Support   Prepare facilities, operate machinery, and ensure compliance with safety and regulatory standards.  Support daily operations, including waste management and effective team communication.  Research Support   Assist in research projects by performing routine procedures and providing experimental support.  Participate in rosters, including weekend/public holiday duties, as required.  About UQ's Gatton Campus Established over 125 years ago, UQ’s Gatton Campus has a long history – from starting out as a dedicated agricultural institution, to being a field hospital during WWII, and to joining the UQ community in 1990.     Everyone here has a role to play. As a member of our casual staff cohort, you will be actively involved in working towards our vision of a better world. By supporting the academic endeavour across teaching, research, and the student life, you’ll have the opportunity to contribute to activities that have a lasting impact on our community.      Join an organisation where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued, and a range of benefits and rewards are available, such as:   Free parking and intercampus bus    On campus childcare , medical clinic, and post office   Access to our $4.1 million UQ Sports facility    Volunteer in our community garden    About You    Essential:   Certificate III in Agriculture (or equivalent) and relevant work experience.  Possession of a C Class Licence and at least 2 years’ experience in livestock handling and animal husbandry.  Competence in livestock techniques (e.g., SC/IM injections, blood collection).  Experience operating plant and machinery (e.g., tractors, forklifts) and capacity to obtain required licences/permits.  Ability to safely operate and use hand tools and basic equipment required for animal husbandry, facility maintenance support, and routine technical tasks, in accordance with OH&S standards. Strong attention to detail, ability to work independently, and excellent team collaboration and communication skills.  Desirable:   Knowledge of OH&S, OGTR, DAWE regulatory requirements, and animal welfare codes.  Experience in a research environment or meat processing. The successful candidate may be required to complete a number of pre-employment checks, including: right to work in Australia and Medical Clearance.  You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.     Medical Clearance: Working with laboratory animals is an inherent requirement of the position. The appointment is therefore subject to, and conditional upon, satisfactory medical clearance(s), including disclosure of relevant medical history, to undertake such work without unreasonable risk to your health and safety. In addition, the Employee is required to fully comply with necessary ongoing health monitoring procedures and control measures associated with the position.    Vaccinations and Immunisation: It is a condition of employment for this role that if you are required now or in the future, to work or interact in Queensland Health clinical facility; or in an equivalent, clinical health facility; or health care role; or will be required to perform work tasks that put you at risk of exposure to vaccine-preventable disease you are required to be immunised against, and remain immunised against, certain vaccine-preventable diseases (VPDs) in accordance with the University’s Vaccinations and Immunisation Guidelines (PPL 2.60.08). The employee is required to provide evidence of immunisation against VPDs.  Questions?    For more information about this opportunity, please contact talent@uq.edu.au stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.     Want to Apply?    All applicants must upload the following documents in order for your application to be considered:  Cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section   Resume   Other Information    UQ is committed to a fair, equitable and inclusive selection process, which recognises that some applicants may face additional barriers and challenges which have impacted and/or continue to impact their career trajectory. Candidates who don’t meet all criteria are encouraged to apply and demonstrate their potential. The selection panel considers both potential and performance relative to opportunities when assessing suitability for the role.   We know one of our strengths as an institution lies in our diverse colleagues. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion , fostering an environment that mirrors our wider community. We're committed to attracting, retaining, and promoting diverse talent. Reach out to talent@uq.edu.au for accessibility support or adjustments.  Applications for this casual pool will be reviewed on an ongoing basis (R-60694). #LI-DNI"
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Herston-Campus/Casual-Dental-Assistants_R-52986": {
      "title": "Casual Dental Assistants",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-01-21\nCasual\n\nSchool of Dentistry Casual position with an hourly rate in the range of $43.79 to $48.20, plus superannuation of 11.5% (HEW Level 3) Based at the Oral Health Centre, Herston campus About This Opportunity The University of Queensland's School of Dentistry is inviting expressions of interest from qualified dental assistants who are interested in casual opportunities. As a Casual Dental Assistant, you will support students and UQ clinical staff at the School of Dentistry’s student-led Dental Clinic. Your responsibilities will include (but are not limited to): Supporting clinical operations to ensure a smooth, effective teaching and treatment environment. Set up, clean, sterilise, and store dental instruments and equipment in line with infection control standards. Maintain accurate clinic records and uphold professional presentation standards. Book, receive and prepare patients for treatment under the supervision of the Clinical Coordinator. About You We welcome applications from Dental Assistants who hold a Certificate III in Dental Assisting (or equivalent Dental Assisting Qualification) and have hands-on experience as a chairside Dental Assistant. A Certificate IV in Dental Assisting (Radiography stream) would be desirable. Experience with Chairside Assisting Minimum of 1–2 years’ experience assisting during general and/or specialty dental procedures, with strong skills in instrument handling, sterilisation, and patient preparation Excellent Interpersonal and Communication Skills Demonstrated ability to communicate clearly and compassionately with patients and team members Willingness to Learn and Grow Professionally Demonstrates a proactive attitude toward learning new skills, techniques, and technologies in a dynamic clinical environment. Open to ongoing training and development opportunities. The successful candidate may be required to complete background checks prior to commencement including the right to work in Australia, mandatory immunisations, and Blue Card check. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world. Everyone here has a role to play. As a member of our professional staff cohort, you will be actively involved in working towards our vision of a better world. By supporting the academic endeavour across teaching, research, and the student life, you’ll have the opportunity to contribute to activities that have a lasting impact on our community.  Questions? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Anna Barrett at a.barrett@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.    Want to Apply? We welcome applications from all individuals and are committed to an inclusive and accessible recruitment process. To be considered, please ensure you upload: Resume  Our strength as an institution lies in our diverse colleagues. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion , fostering an environment that mirrors our wider community. We're committed to attracting, retaining, and promoting diverse talent. If you require an alternative method to submit your application due to accessibility needs or personal circumstances, please contact talent@uq.edu.au . Other Information   As a casual employee, and in line with Fair Work , please note that there is no commitment to ongoing work in this position.  UQ is committed to a fair, equitable and inclusive selection process, which recognises that some applicants may face additional barriers and challenges which have impacted and/or continue to impact their career trajectory. Candidates who don’t meet all criteria are encouraged to apply and demonstrate their potential. The selection panel considers both potential and performance relative to opportunities when assessing suitability for the role. Application reference number R-52986. Please note applications will be reviewed as received. Candidates may be interviewed prior to the job closing date. We encourage candidates to apply as soon as possible. The University of Queensland reserves the right to close this application process early."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Herston-Campus/Casual-Academics---School-of-Dentistry_R-59295": {
      "title": "Casual Academics - School of Dentistry",
      "description": "Posted: 2025-12-03\nCasual\n\nAbout This Opportunity  The University of Queensland School of Dentistry is seeking expressions of interest from casual academics to be engaged on a casual basis. Casual Academics at the School of Dentistry play a significant part in the overall student experience and assist in the teaching and delivery of both undergraduate and postgraduate courses. Responsibilities as a Casual Academic may include (but are not limited to):   Preparing and delivering guest lecturers, workshops, seminars and tutorials. Undertaking marking of assessment tasks, as directed by course coordinators, with hours for marking to be confirmed by School Administration staff prior to commencing marking. Casual academic positions are based at both the Herston and St Lucia Campuses and successful appointees may be required to attend on campus. Our Ideal Candidate  The School welcomes applications from candidates who possess appropriate qualifications and experience within the dental industry. High-achieving postgraduate coursework and research higher degree students are also invited to apply. What We Can Offer  These are casual positions. As such, there is no firm advance commitment to ongoing or indefinite work. The casual hourly rate can be viewed here . Compliance  All Casual Academics are required to comply with legislation and University policy relevant to the duties undertaken, including but not exclusive to:  The University’s Code of Conduct .  Requirements of the Queensland occupational health and safety (OH&S) legislation and related OH&S responsibilities and procedures developed by the University or Institute/School.  The adoption of sustainable practices in all work activities and compliance with associated legislation and related University sustainability responsibilities and procedures .  Requirements of the Education Services  for Overseas Students Act 2000, the National Code 2007 and associated legislation, and related responsibilities and procedures developed by the University.  About the School of Dentistry The UQ School of Dentistry has a long and proud tradition of over 80 years of strength in dental education and research and attracts high achieving students nationally and internationally. UQ is a leading source of expertise in the local, national, and international dental community and has been a key contributor to achieving effective solutions to contemporary issues and challenges. The School has strong collaborations within and outside the dental profession. As part of the Faculty of Health, Medicine and Behavioural Sciences, the School of Dentistry offers the Bachelor of Dental Science (Honours) program and specialist post-graduate programs Doctor of Clinical Dentistry and the Doctor of Dental Medicine. The School of Dentistry also has a growing cohort of research higher degree students. Further details about the School’s teaching programs, research interests and other activities may be accessed on the School’s website at http://www.dentistry.uq.edu.au . Questions?  If you have any questions regarding this expression of interest, please contact the School of Dentistry, Acting Student and Academic Administration Manager, Mrs Anna Jackson at anna.jackson@uq.edu.au . Want To Apply?     All applicants must provide a resume to apply.  The School of Dentistry employs Casual Academics as required throughout the year. If you are a current employee of the University (including casual staff), or hold a HDR Scholarship with the University, please login to your staff Workday account and visit the internal careers board to apply for this opportunity. Please do NOT apply via the external job board."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Heron-Island-Research-Station/Research-Station-Support-Services-Assistant--Heron-Island----Identified--s25-_R-62194-3": {
      "title": "Research Station Support Services Assistant (Heron Island) - Identified (s25)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-07\nFull time\n\nFaculty of Science 2 x Full time permanent positions Base salary will be in the range $79,096.42- $83,863.22 + 17% Superannuation (HEW Level 4)  This position is open to Traditional Owners of Bailai, Gurang, Gooreng Gooreng or Taribelang Bunda Peoples – the Traditional Custodians of the land and sea Country encompassing Heron Island and surrounding areas. Based at Heron Island Research Station Acknowledgement of Country  The University of Queensland (UQ) acknowledges the Traditional Owners and their custodianship of the lands on which UQ operates. We pay our respects to their Ancestors and their descendants, who continue cultural and spiritual connections to Country. We recognise their valuable contributions to Australian and global society.  About This Opportunity    Join a collaborative and purpose-driven team at UQ’s Heron Island Research Station, where scientific excellence meets cultural stewardship on Country. This identified role supports the day-to-day operations of UQ’s research station while embedding Traditional Owner knowledge, values, and perspectives into environmental management and research activities. We are seeking two positions with complementary focus areas: One position with a stronger emphasis on maintenance and facility operations . One position with a stronger emphasis on administration, outreach, and research support services . Working as part of a collaborative team, both Research Station Support Services Assistant roles contribute to the overall daily operations of UQ’s research stations. This work may be supporting the delivery of specific services including scientific (e.g. laboratory and aquaria), boating, snorkelling and diving, administrative, general maintenance and other operational tasks and activities required to maintain the safe and effective functioning of the research station. Key responsibilities include: Assisting with operational services including administration, logistics, and facility upkeep across the station Supporting the preparation, use, and maintenance of scientific, boating, and diving equipment to enable research and teaching activities Contributing to culturally informed environmental management practices and promoting awareness of cultural heritage protocols Collaborating with staff, researchers, and visitors to ensure safe, efficient, and respectful station operations On-site accommodation will be provided by UQ while undertaking work on Heron Island . Take our virtual tour . For further information, please  click here  to view the information booklet.  About You    The University of Queensland considers that to perform this role, it is essential that the person is an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander person. It is therefore a genuine occupational requirement under section 25 of the  Anti-Discrimination Act 1991  (Qld). The successful candidate will be required to provide evidence to confirm that they are an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person.  Must be a Traditional Owner of the Bailai, Gurang, Gooreng Gooreng, or Taribelang Bunda Peoples – the Traditional Custodians of the land and sea Country encompassing Heron Island and surrounding areas. Connection to and knowledge of the cultural heritage, traditions, and values of the Traditional Owners of Heron Island and surrounding areas. Vocational training qualification (or ready ability to acquire) in science, business, or a trade with relevant experience, OR an equivalent combination of relevant experience, education, and training. Demonstrated ability to follow complex processes and systems, ideally where necessary for health and safety, animal ethics, and/or environmental compliance. Current holder of (or ready ability to attain and maintain) the necessary qualifications and experience for formal recognition in the operation of plant and heavy machinery. High level computer literacy skills including the ability to use, or learn to use, software applications associated with analytical instruments and business operations. Demonstrated high level of interpersonal skills, with a proven ability to develop and sustain strategic relationships, to work collaboratively and undertake effective consultations with client groups. About UQ  As part of the UQ community, you will work alongside bright minds from around the world, united by a shared commitment to creating a better world. Excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued, and staff are supported through a wide range of benefits, including up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% superannuation, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, salary packaging, and cultural leave.  UQ is committed to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples’ learning, discovery and engagement, and to playing a leading role in reconciliation in Australia. Guided by our Strategic Plan, Stretch Reconciliation Action Plan and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Employment Strategy, we work in partnership with communities to embed reconciliation across our operations. You will be supported to connect with vibrant staff networks, including the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Staff Network, and your cultural identity will be respected, valued and embraced.  Interested?  For more information about this opportunity, please contact Dr  Caitlin Lawson at  caitlin.lawson@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at  talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a  resume  and  cover letter  summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section.     Other Information    Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, criminal history checks and Blue Card checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   We're dedicated to  equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing  talent@uq.edu.au  or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close 21 September 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-62194).   Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for late September/early October."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Casual-Academics---Institute-of-Modern-Languages_R-69033": {
      "title": "Casual Academics - Institute of Modern Languages",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-04\nCasual\n\nAbout This Opportunity      The University of Queensland’s Institute of Modern Languages is seeking expressions of interest from casual academics to be engaged on a casual basis.   Casual Academics make a rich and important contribution to the Institutes ’ language teaching and community engagement endeavours through the provision of a range of academically focused services and activities. Casual Academic responsibilities may include (but are not limited to):    Teaching    Personalised Tutoring    Development of teaching materials   Exam Marking   Preparation of lesson plans   Report writing and other associated duties.   What We Can Offer      These are casual academic appointments. As such, there is no firm advance commitment to ongoing or indefinite work. The casual rate will vary depending on the type of work completed. Please view our various casual academic pay scales.     For further information about UQ’s benefits, please visit Why Work at UQ and review  The University of Queensland Enterprise Agreement 2021-2026 (Enterprise Agreement) .     Eligibility Qualifications   A Bachelor level degree or other qualification in teaching, education, TESOL, languages or linguistics . Or a certificate IV in Training and Assessment or equivalent.   Knowledge and Skills   Native speaker or native-like proficiency in the target language.   Ability to develop programs for specific language learning p urposes .   Ability to evaluate & assess student progress.   Knowledge of current practices in language teaching methodology .   Experience   General or Language teaching experience.   Personal qualities   Good communication and interpersonal skills.   Ability to adopt a flexible approach to teaching style in response to student needs   You must have work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.    We value diversity and inclusion, and actively encourage applications from those who bring diversity to the University. UQ’s Diversity and Inclusion webpage contains further information if you require additional support. Accessibility requirements and/or adjustments can be directed to casualrecruit@uq.edu.au .     Want to Apply?     All applicants will be asked to nominate their course and/or general subject area preference/s in the application form. In addition, all applicants must upload the following documents for an application to be considered:   A copy of your current CV/resume   A brief statement addressing:   experience in tutoring/facilitating /marking exams ; experience using online tools (Padlet, Zoom, Teams); knowledge/expertise in the target language.  Please note that you will be asked to add all documents into the one upload box during the application form. If you are a current employee of the University (including casual staff), or hold a HDR Scholarship with the University, please login to your staff Workday account and visit the internal careers board to apply for this opportunity. Please do NOT apply via the external job board.   Questions?    To discuss the role please contact the [ Pru Rolff ] via email [ p.rolff@uq.edu.au ]     For technical queries about your application, please contact casualrecruit@uq.edu.au stating the job reference number   in the subject line."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Head-of-School--School-of-Mechanical-and-Mining-Engineering_R-65984-2": {
      "title": "Head of School, School of Mechanical and Mining Engineering",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-17\nFull time\n\nSchool of Mechanical and Mining Engineering Full-time, 5-year fixed-term position at Academic Level E, with underlying continuing (permanent) Professorial position Based at our beautiful  St Lucia Campus   About This Opportunity Lead one of the world's most respected engineering schools and help shape the future of engineering education, research and industry impact. The School of Mechanical and Mining Engineering is entering an exciting new chapter. With a strong global reputation, significant student growth, world-leading research capability, and deep industry connections, the School is uniquely positioned to influence the future of mechanical, aerospace, biomedical, materials, mechatronic and mining engineering. As Head of School, you will inherit a thriving, collaborative and inclusive community and be empowered to drive its next phase of growth. This is a rare opportunity to build on an extraordinary legacy while shaping the ideas, partnerships and people that will solve tomorrow's most complex challenges. The School’s commitment to research excellence and real-world impact is reflected in the outstanding global standing of UQ's Mineral and Mining Engineering discipline, ranked 5th in the world in the 2026 QS World University Rankings by Subject . In this role, you will: Shape the strategic direction of one of Australia's leading engineering schools. Lead a community of internationally recognised researchers, educators, professional staff and industry collaborators. Drive research excellence across areas transforming the future, including advanced manufacturing, renewable energy, robotics, automation, biomedical engineering and mining innovation. Build bold interdisciplinary partnerships that translate discovery into meaningful impact. Strengthen engagement with industry, government and global partners to create new opportunities for growth, influence and collaboration. Elevate the School's global reputation while protecting the collegial, supportive culture that makes it distinctive. Guide strategic investment decisions across people, infrastructure, education and research. Leave a lasting legacy through leadership that creates impact well beyond the University. For more information about this opportunity, please view the Appointment Booklet. About You We are seeking a respected academic leader with an international reputation for excellence and a passion for shaping the future of research, education and industry impact. Combining intellectual leadership with strategic vision, you will bring the credibility to influence at the highest levels and the ability to unite people around a shared ambition. Our ideal candidate will demonstrate: An internationally recognised reputation for academic excellence , with the credibility, leadership capability and strategic vision to enhance the standing and influence of the School and Faculty. A proven track record of leading and developing high-performing teams , creating a culture of collaboration, innovation and excellence, and inspiring others to achieve ambitious goals in research, teaching and engagement. Exceptional stakeholder engagement, influencing and relationship-building skills , with the ability to forge productive partnerships across industry, government, professional bodies and the broader community. Strong strategic, operational and commercial acumen , including experience leading complex organisations and managing financial, human and physical resources to deliver sustainable growth and impact. A commitment to inclusive leadership and organisational stewardship , demonstrated through fostering equity, diversity and inclusion, supporting staff development and wellbeing, and navigating governance, policy and regulatory responsibilities with integrity. Most importantly, you are excited by the opportunity to lead a community with extraordinary potential and help define its next chapter. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. At the core of our teaching remains our students, and their experience with us sets a foundation for success far beyond graduation. UQ has made a commitment to making education opportunities available for all Queenslanders, regardless of personal, financial, or geographical barriers. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development. Interested? If you'd like to have a confidential discussion, please contact Caylee-Jade Eve, Principal Consultant, Executive Recruitment via executiverecruitment@uq.edu.au .  When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns with the 'About You' section in the attached position booklet . Other Information Appointment to this role is subject to the successful completion of relevant pre-employment checks, which will include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, and criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. Relocating from interstate or overseas? We may support you with obtaining employer-sponsored work rights and a relocation support package. You can find out more about life in  Australia’s Sunshine State here .  We're dedicated to equity, diversity and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We're also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Monday the 26th October at 11.00pm AEST ( R-65984 )."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Senior-Lecturer-in-Architectural-Technology_R-68599-1": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer in Architectural Technology",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-17\nFull time\n\nSchool of Architecture, Design and Planning – Faculty of EAIT Full-time (100%), fixed-term position for up to 2 years commencing January 2027 Base salary will be in the range $145,961.51 - $168,043.68 + 17% super (Academic Level C) Visa sponsorship may be available for this position Based at our St Lucia Campus About This Opportunity  We are seeking a passionate and experienced Senior Lecturer in Architectural Technology to join our vibrant academic community at the School of Architecture, Design and Planning (ADP) at our beautiful St Lucia campus. Shape the future of architecture and digital design at UQ and be part of a university consistently ranked in the world’s top 40. This exciting new role combines industry expertise with teaching, research and collaboration to inspire future design leaders and drive innovation. You will play a key role in advancing architectural technology, digital design, sustainability and AI across UQ's teaching and research programs. Collaborating with colleagues and industry, you will help equip graduates to tackle future built environment challenges. We welcome applicants who may prefer a different service fraction—please indicate your preferred arrangement in your cover letter. Flexible options, including job share, may be considered to accommodate the right candidate. If you are ready to make your mark in a world-class institution that invests in its people and their potential, we would love to hear from you. Key responsibilities will include:  Inspire through teaching – Shape the future of architecture with engaging, innovative courses, lectures and tutorials that connect theory to real-world practice, particularly in mainstreams of architectural technology and architectural science. This is expected to happen through leading design studios that connect deep architectural theory with computation, fabrication, performance and material experimentation. Lead impactful research – Build a strong research profile, publish in leading international journals, and secure funding to drive change across the built environment sector and in collaboration with other sectors/disciplines. Mentor future leaders – Guide and support Honours and Higher Degree by Research students to thrive as the next generation of architectural designers/scientists and design innovators. Engage and connect – Collaborate with colleagues, industry, government and the wider community while contributing to UQ’s vibrant academic culture. Drive technological innovation – Advance the School's strategic priorities in architectural technology by developing and integrating digital design methodologies, computational design, building performance analysis, digital fabrication, AI-assisted design, sustainable construction, and emerging technologies into teaching, research and industry engagement. In doing so, the successful candidate is expected to develop and critically evaluate bespoke AI and computational tools, rather than only the use of existing platforms. This is a teaching & research position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance . About You  A PhD in Architecture, Architectural Science, Architectural Technology, or a related discipline. An established profile and leadership in teaching and research in architecture, architectural technology or architectural science.  Evidence of reputation and track record of publications in reputed refereed journals and presenting at conferences.  Evidence of successfully seeking, obtaining and managing internal and external research funding.  Evidence of a high level of quality teaching at undergraduate, honours and postgraduate level across a variety of settings, including small and large groups/classrooms.  Experience in course coordination and contribution to the development and continuous innovation of new programs, curriculum design and course material in the discipline area.  Technologically proficient and demonstrated leadership in integrating relevant digital and technical methods into curriculum, including critique of bias, authorship, automation and contextual relevance. Technically skilled and experienced, with a strong understanding of construction materials, systems and processes and their impact on documentation and detailing. An evidenced record of supervision of Honours and Research Higher Degree students to successful completion.  Experience in meaningful internal service roles in conjunction with active contributions to external activities.  We particularly welcome applications from women, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people with disability, members of the LGBTQIA+ community, and others who may be underrepresented in this discipline. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development. The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Professor Ali Cheshmehzangi (Head of School) at a.chesh@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.  When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information   Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia and qualifications checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. Relocating from interstate or overseas? We may support you with obtaining employer-sponsored work rights and a relocation support package, however upfront costs will still be expected to be covered. You can find out more about life in Australia’s Sunshine State here . We are dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We are also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Sunday, 18 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68599)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Lecturer-in-Architectural-Design--T-R-_R-68595": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Architectural Design (T&R)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-17\nFull time\n\nSchool of Architecture, Design and Planning – Faculty of EAIT Full-time (100%), fixed-term position for up to 2 years commencing January 2027 Base salary will be in the range $119,462.94 - $141,545.09 + 17% super (Academic Level B) Visa sponsorship is not available for this position Based at our St Lucia Campus About This Opportunity  This is a great opportunity to join the School of Architecture, Design and Planning (ADP) as a Lecturer in Architectural Design , bringing expertise in Architectural Technology and Architectural Science. This newly created role offers the opportunity to shape the future of architectural design at a world top 40 university. Join a collaborative and supportive environment where teaching, research, and industry engagement thrive, with access to leading facilities and opportunities to inspire the next generation of design professionals. As the built environment evolves, this role will advance UQ’s teaching and research in architectural technology and design. You will contribute with your expertise in digital and computational design, BIM, sustainability, fabrication technologies, and AI, while collaborating across disciplines and industry to prepare graduates for future challenges in the built environment. We welcome applicants who may prefer a different service fraction—please indicate your preferred arrangement in your cover letter. Flexible options, including job share, may be considered to accommodate the right candidate. If you are ready to make your mark in a world-class institution that invests in its people and their potential, we would love to hear from you. Key responsibilities will include:  Inspire through teaching – Shape the future of architecture with engaging, innovative courses, lectures and tutorials that connect theory to real-world practice, particularly in mainstreams of architectural technology, architectural science, and tectonics. This helps connecting spatial theory directly with computational methods to ensure technology supports critical design thinking rather than a separate software stream. Lead impactful research – Build a strong research profile, publish in leading international journals, and secure funding to drive change across the built environment sector and in collaboration with other sectors/disciplines. Mentor future leaders – Guide and support Honours and Higher Degree by Research students to thrive as the next generation of architectural designers/scientists and design innovators. Engage and connect – Collaborate with colleagues, industry, government and the wider community while contributing to UQ’s vibrant academic culture. Drive technological innovation – Support the School's strategic priorities in architectural technology by integrating digital design methodologies, computational design, building performance analysis, architectural tectonics, AI-assisted design, sustainable construction, and emerging technologies into teaching, research and industry engagement. This also includes human-led critique and validation of AI outputs, including bias, authorship, copyright, automation and local contextual relevance. This is a teaching & research position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance . About You  A PhD in Architecture/Architectural Design, Architectural Science, Architectural Technology, or a related discipline. A growing profile in teaching and research in architecture, architectural technology or architectural science/tectonics.  A developing reputation and track record of publications in reputed refereed journals and presenting at conferences.  Evidence of successfully seeking, obtaining and managing internal and external research funding.  Evidence of a high level of quality teaching at undergraduate, honours and postgraduate level across a variety of settings, including small and large groups/classrooms.  Experience in course coordination and contribution to the development and continuous innovation of new programs, curriculum design and course material in the discipline area.  Evidence of capability in computational and generative design, digital fabrication and building-performance tools, including critical and ethical teaching practice. Technically skilled and experienced, with a strong understanding of construction materials, systems and processes and their impact on documentation and detailing A growing record of supervision of Honours and Research Higher Degree students to successful completion.  Experience in meaningful internal service roles in conjunction with active contributions to external activities.  We particularly welcome applications from women, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people with disability, members of the LGBTQIA+ community, and others who may be underrepresented in this discipline. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development. The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Professor Ali Cheshmehzangi (Head of School) at a.chesh@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.  When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information   Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia and qualifications checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We are dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We are also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Sunday, 18 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68595)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Professor-and-Head-of-School--School-of-Chemistry-and-Molecular-Biosciences_R-69191-2": {
      "title": "Professor and Head of School, School of Chemistry and Molecular Biosciences",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-23\nFull time\n\nFaculty of Science | School of Chemistry and Molecular Biosciences   Full-time (100%), fixed-term appointment for five years with an underlying continuing Teaching and Research appointment at Academic Level E  Based at our St Lucia Campus  About This Opportunity   The University of Queensland is seeking an outstanding academic leader to serve as Professor and Head of School, School of Chemistry and Molecular Biosciences (SCMB). This is a rare opportunity to lead one of Australia's most respected centres for education and discovery across the chemical, molecular and biological sciences. Reporting to the Executive Dean, Faculty of Science, you will provide visionary leadership that advances the School’s strategic direction, strengthens its global reputation, and inspires excellence across research, teaching, engagement and impact.  Home to world-class researchers, educators and professional staff, SCMB delivers outstanding undergraduate and postgraduate education while advancing research that addresses some of society’s most pressing challenges, including human health, biotechnology, sustainability, agriculture and the environment. As Head of School, you will join a collaborative, ambitious and future-focused community and work alongside talented colleagues, students, industry partners, government stakeholders and global collaborators to create meaningful change. You will foster a culture that is inclusive, innovative and supportive, empowering established and emerging leaders to thrive while driving exceptional outcomes for the School, Faculty and University.  For more information about this opportunity, please view the information pack .   About You   You are an outstanding academic leader with an international reputation for excellence in one or more disciplines represented within the School. Holding a PhD or equivalent qualification, you will bring a strong and active record of achievement in teaching, research, publications, competitive grant success and higher degree by research supervision. You will be recognised as a visionary and purposeful leader who combines scholarly distinction with the ability to inspire, influence and unite diverse communities around a shared goal.  You will have demonstrated success leading large and complex academic environments, with highly developed interpersonal, communication and people leadership skills. Your experience will include shaping positive organisational culture, building productive relationships across industry, government and the broader community, and managing finances, operations and human resources effectively. An inquisitive and collaborative mindset, coupled with a strong commitment to equity, diversity and inclusion, will enable you to lead with integrity while enhancing the School’s standing, supporting outstanding teaching and research outcomes, and fostering meaningful engagement locally, nationally and globally.  About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. At the core of our teaching remains our students, and their experience with us sets a foundation for success far beyond graduation. UQ has made a commitment to making education opportunities available for all Queenslanders, regardless of personal, financial, or geographical barriers. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development. Interested? If you’d like to have a confidential discussion, please contact Caitlin Savage (Principal Consultant, Executive Recruitment) via executiverecruitment@uq.edu.au . When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume, cover letter, and responses to the selection criteria. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. Relocating from interstate or overseas? We may support you with obtaining employer-sponsored work rights and a relocation support package. You can find out more about life in Australia’s Sunshine State here. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion. We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing executiverecruitment@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Sunday 25 October at 11.00pm AEST (R-69191)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow-in-Power-Systems-Stability-and-Oscillation-Localisation_R-69503-1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow in Power Systems Stability and Oscillation Localisation",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-23\nFull time\n\nFaculty of Engineering, Architecture and Information Technology / School of Electrical Engineering and Computer Science Full-time fixed-term position through to end of March 2029 Base salary will be in the range $85,372.89 - $113,659.72+ 17% super (Academic Level A) Based at our St Lucia Campus   About This Opportunity  Join a visionary and collaborative research team working at the forefront of Australia’s energy transition. As a Postdoctoral Research Fellow, you will contribute to the Grid Enhancement Technologies grants program funded by the Department of Climate Change, Energy, the Environment and Water for the project, “Enhancing Grid Stability: Oscillation Localisation and Visualisation”, through a partnership between  EPEC Engineering, the University of Queensland and Powerlink Queensland.   Working within the Power, Energy and Control Engineering discipline in the School of Electrical Engineering and Computer Science, you will help develop innovative solutions that improve the stability, visibility and resilience of Australia’s electricity network. Leveraging world-class facilities and a connected network of industry and research partners, you will play a meaningful role in accelerating secure renewable energy integration and supporting Australia’s net-zero ambitions.   Key responsibilities will include:  Research: Develop advanced oscillation source localisation techniques for inverter-based resource dominated power systems, including mode identification, source detection, dynamic event classification, hardware-in-the-loop validation, research publication, and grant-supported project delivery. Supervision and Researcher Development: Contribute to the supervision and development of Honours and Higher Degree Research students, support researcher capability development, and collaborate in the delivery of professional development initiatives. Citizenship and Service:  Foster productive partnerships with industry and government stakeholders, contribute to School and University initiatives, participate in service activities, and demonstrate behaviours aligned with UQ values. Industry Engagement: Work closely with EPEC Engineering, Powerlink Queensland, and other stakeholders to translate research outcomes into practical tools that strengthen network operations and renewable energy integration.   This is a research focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance .     About You  You are an emerging researcher who is passionate about solving complex energy challenges and creating meaningful impact through innovative power systems research. You will bring: Completion, or near completion, of a PhD in Electrical Engineering or a closely related discipline. Demonstrated expertise in power system dynamics, stability analysis, renewable energy integration, or related areas. Knowledge of data-driven analytics, machine learning, signal processing, or advanced modelling techniques relevant to power systems. Experience with real-time simulation platforms, hardware-in-the-loop testing, or power system validation methodologies. Evidence of publication in reputable peer-reviewed journals and presentation of research outcomes at conferences. An emerging research profile with the ability to contribute to collaborative, externally funded research projects. Strong interpersonal and communication skills with the ability to engage effectively with researchers, students, industry partners and government stakeholders. Demonstrated commitment to fostering an inclusive, respectful and collaborative research environment.   We particularly welcome applications from women, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people with disability, members of the LGBTQIA+ community, and others who may be underrepresented in this discipline.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.   The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Dr Feifei Bai ( f.bai@uq.edu.au ) . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.    When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia and qualification checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   Relocating from interstate or overseas? We may support you with obtaining employer-sponsored work rights and a relocation support package, however upfront costs will still be expected to be covered. You can find out more about life in Australia’s Sunshine State here .   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Thursday October 22nd 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69503). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for early November 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Scientific-Officer---Materials-Characterisation---Cleanroom-Operations_R-69206-1": {
      "title": "Scientific Officer – Materials Characterisation & Cleanroom Operations",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-23\nFull time\n\nAustralian National Fabrication Facility, Queensland Node (ANFF-Qld) , based at the Australian Institute for Bioengineering and Nanotechnology (AIBN) Full-time (100%), fixed-term position through to 30 June 2028. Base salary will be in the range $106,709.01 to $116,198.52 + 17% super (HEW Level 7). Based at our St Lucia Campus About This Opportunity  The Australian National Fabrication Facility, Queensland Node (ANFF-Qld) is seeking an experienced and collaborative Scientific Officer to strengthen its materials characterisation capability and support safe, reliable cleanroom operations. Working within a connected research infrastructure team, you will take technical ownership of specialist equipment, enable researchers and industry users to achieve high-quality outcomes, and contribute to an inclusive environment where expertise is shared and new capabilities are developed together.   Reporting to the ANFF-Qld Facility Manager, the position will have two primary areas of responsibility: Approximately 60% – Materials Characterisation Technical Ownership: Primary ownership and operation of assigned materials characterisation tools and systems, including user training, calibration, preventative maintenance, troubleshooting, technical support and capability development.   Approximately 40% – Cleanroom Operational Support: Supporting effective day-to-day cleanroom operations, including chemicals, PPE, consumables, inventory, chemical waste and maintaining a safe, clean and well-organised laboratory environment.   The position will work closely with ANFF-Qld technical staff, AIBN infrastructure teams, researchers, students and industry users   Key responsibilities will include:    Act as primary technical owner for assigned materials characterisation tools and systems, including GPC, FTIR and other relevant analytical or characterisation techniques, based on facility operational requirements. Manage the full operational lifecycle of assigned equipment, including user access, training and competency assessment, calibration, preventative maintenance, troubleshooting, service coordination, performance monitoring and equipment reliability. Provide specialist technical support and consultation to researchers and industry users, including sample preparation, measurement methodology, data quality and appropriate use of characterisation techniques. Develop, maintain and continuously improve SOPs, risk assessments, training materials, equipment records and quality documentation for assigned tools and technical processes. Contribute to the development, optimisation and implementation of new characterisation methodologies, processes and technical capabilities, and support knowledge transfer across the ANFF-Qld team. Support cleanroom operations by maintaining appropriate stocks of chemicals, PPE, laboratory materials and consumables, including purchasing, receiving, restocking, inventory monitoring and usage records through relevant UQ systems. Coordinate the safe storage, handling and disposal of cleanroom chemicals and chemical waste in accordance with UQ, AIBN and facility safety requirements and procedures. Maintain a high standard of cleanroom organisation and operational readiness, including PPE stations, consumable storage, chemical cabinets, sticky mats and other routine cleanroom requirements. Serve as primary, joint or backup technical owner for other relevant characterisation, imaging, metrology or micro/nanofabrication equipment where required or as assigned by Facility Manger. Contribute to RIMS administration, operational reporting, safety, quality and continuous improvement activities. Work collaboratively with ANFF-Qld and AIBN staff, researchers, students, industry clients and other stakeholders, and undertake other reasonable technical or operational duties consistent with the scope and work level of the position and ANFF-Qld operational requirements.   About You You will have:   A tertiary qualification in chemistry, materials science, physics, engineering or a closely related discipline, or an equivalent combination of relevant education, training and professional experience. Demonstrated hands-on experience working with materials characterisation, analytical or metrology equipment, preferably within a research infrastructure, and university, biomedical, semiconductor, advanced manufacturing or similar technical environment. Practical experience with one or more relevant characterisation techniques such as GPC, FTIR, spectroscopy, microscopy, surface characterisation or related materials analysis techniques. Demonstrated ability to take technical ownership of scientific equipment, including operation, calibration, preventative maintenance, troubleshooting, fault diagnosis and coordination with equipment suppliers or service providers. Experience providing technical training, user onboarding and competency assessment, with the ability to communicate equipment operation, scientific methods and safety requirements clearly to users with different levels of experience. Demonstrated experience working safely in a cleanroom, PC2 laboratory or controlled laboratory environment, with sound knowledge of laboratory safety, chemical handling, risk assessment and waste-management requirements. Experience managing laboratory or cleanroom chemicals, consumables, PPE and inventory, including purchasing, stock monitoring, storage and replenishment. Demonstrated ability to develop and maintain high-quality technical documentation, including SOPs, risk assessments, equipment records, maintenance documentation and training materials. Strong analytical and problem-solving capability, with the ability to independently identify equipment or process issues, determine appropriate actions and escalate technical or safety risks when required. Strong interpersonal, written and verbal communication skills, with a demonstrated ability to work collaboratively with technical colleagues, researchers, students, industry users and other internal and external stakeholders.   About the Australian National Fabrication Facility (ANFF-Qld) ANFF-Qld is a not-for-profit research infrastructure facility established under the National Collaborative Research Infrastructure Strategy (NCRIS). ANFF is a network of eight nodes distributed across Australia, supported by over $200 million in investment in equipment, infrastructure, and personnel.   The Queensland Node (ANFF-Qld) is a state-of-the-art fabrication facility specialising in microfluidics, organic electronics and optoelectronics, biomaterials, semiconductor, quantum and photonic technologies, novel materials, and advanced imaging and characterisation.   Its facilities are located within the Australian Institute for Bioengineering and Nanotechnology (AIBN) and the Centre for Organic Photonics & Electronics (COPE) at The University of Queensland, as well as the Queensland Microtechnology Facility (QMF) at Griffith University. ANFF-Qld also facilitates access to other ANFF sites and the broader national network across Australian universities.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.   Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Ethan Aung - anffq.manager@uq.edu.au   For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.     When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, background checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close 18 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69206). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for the week commencing 27 October 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Research-Fellow--ZeroPC_R-68319-1": {
      "title": "Research Fellow, ZeroPC",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-21\nFull time\n\nAustralian Institute for Bioengineering and Nanotechnology (AIBN) Full-time (100%), fixed-term position through to October 2028 Base salary will be in the range $106,294 to $113,660 + 17% super (Academic Level A) Based at our St Lucia Campus   About This Opportunity  This is an exciting opportunity to join an established, expert team to conduct innovative research on thermoelectric materials for low-grade waste heat recovery. Working on a project within the ARC Research Hub in Zero-Emission Power Generation for Carbon Neutrality, known as ZeroPC, you will collaborate with researchers and industry partners across UQ, QUT and HBIS Group.   A high level of knowledge, expertise and experience in thermoelectric materials and devices will be required for this project, as you will be expected to carry out experimental and computational studies on thermoelectric materials, as well as supporting the supervision and development of Undergraduate, Honours and Higher Degree Research students.   Key responsibilities will include:    Research: Produce high-quality research outputs and work within a cross-disciplinary team to fabricate, characterise and test thermoelectric materials, thermoelectric generators and integrated waste-heat recovery systems; Undertake density functional theory calculations and finite element analysis to guide the selection and development of thermoelectric materials and devices, while contributing to funding applications and research translation.   Supervision and Researcher Development: Support the effective supervision and development of undergraduate project, Honours and Higher Degree by Research students through feedback, coaching and collaborative research practice.   Citizenship and Service: Demonstrate UQ values, contribute to priority activities and internal service, and help develop productive relationships with industry, government, professional bodies and the wider community.   This is a research focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance .   About You    Completion or near completion of a PhD in the fields of chemistry, chemical engineering, materials engineering, physics or similar field; Demonstrated expertise in fabrication and characterisation of thermoelectric materials and thermoelectric generators; Demonstrate expertise in DFT calculations or or finite element analysis of thermoelectric materials and devices; Evidence of publications in reputed refereed journals and presenting at conferences. Evidence of contributions towards successfully obtaining external research funding; Ability to work well in a team and to contribute to development of a collaborative and supportive research environment. About ZeroPC ZeroPC is an ARC Hub and partnership between University and Industry aiming to develop sustainable zero-emission power generation technologies to transform gaseous waste from our energy and manufacturing sectors into valuable products and create scalable pathways to market for driving industry transformation. This Hub plans to harvest renewable energy from the environment by using zero-emission thermoelectric power generators and then store it in green and safer batteries for converting gaseous waste from sectors that cannot easily avoid emission into useful chemicals, which in turn realize carbon neutrality and negativity .   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.   The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Prof Debra Bernhardt - d.bernhardt@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.     When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close 18 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68319)"
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Gatton-Campus/Primary-Care-Veterinary-Clinician_R-69194-1": {
      "title": "Primary Care Veterinary Clinician",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-25\nFull time\n\nUQ VETS . Full-time, continuing position. Base salary will be in the range $106,709 - $116,198 + 17% super (HEW Level 7). Join our Gatton Campus community – take a virtual tour !   About This Opportunity We are seeking a motivated and energetic Veterinary Clinician to join the UQ VETS Small Animal Hospital at the UQ Gatton Veterinary Teaching Hospital. Working within a collaborative, inquisitive and purpose-driven clinical environment, this role supports the delivery of high-quality, relationship-centred veterinary care while contributing to the education and mentoring of the next generation of veterinarians and veterinary technologists. As part of a visionary and supportive team, you will be challenged, inspired and rewarded while helping create meaningful change for animals, clients, students and the veterinary profession.   Key responsibilities will include, but not limited to: Provide high-quality, relationship-centred clinical care to animal patients and their owners, independently managing both routine and complex veterinary cases. Participate in the management of primary care, urgent and emergency cases within the Small Animal Hospital. Actively contribute to clinical teaching, instruction and supervision of undergraduate veterinary science and veterinary technology students, as well as mentoring interns and junior clinicians. Support continuous improvement initiatives, clinical leadership, and effective day-to-day hospital operations in alignment with University and professional standards.   About UQ VETS A career at UQ VETS means making a difference to not only the lives of the people and animals in your care, but also the next generation of vets and vet technicians. At UQ VETS, we offer the opportunity to build a dynamic career through structured development programs and access to industry leaders.   Instead of solely concentrating on meeting caseload benchmarks or revenue targets, your priorities lie in creating an impact, establishing a lasting impression on the field, and conducting work that brings about positive changes in the lives of animals.   The Veterinary Teaching Hospital is an organisational unit within the School that includes all of the School’s outstanding clinical facilities. UQ VETS Equine Hospital is a largely referral-based hospital with advanced facilities and offers a 24-hour emergency medical and surgical service. UQ VETS Small Animal Hospital boasts the latest in veterinary medical and surgical diagnostic and treatment options for small animals, birds, exotic pets and wildlife, as well as a 7-day urgent and emergency care service. UQ staff also supervise clinical placements at the RSPCA’s Brisbane Animal Care Campus, Wacol.   In particular, the UQ VETS Small Animal Hospital values every animal and offers unrivalled medical, surgical and diagnostic services for dogs, cats, birds, reptiles, small mammals and fish at our world-class veterinary hospital in Gatton.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available, such as:   Free parking and intercampus bus. On campus childcare, medical clinic, and post office. Up to 26 weeks paid parental leave/paid primary care leave. 17% superannuation contributions + 17.5% annual leave loading. Health and wellness discounts: fitness passport access, free yearly flu vaccinations, discounted health insurance, and access to our Employee Assistance Program for staff and their immediate family. Career development opportunities: access to exclusive internal-only vacancies and our Study for Staff program. 25% discount on eligible UQ Vets services. Access to our $4.1 million UQ Sports facility.   About You You will bring professional expertise, enthusiasm for teaching, and a collaborative mindset to this role.   A Bachelor of Veterinary Science or equivalent qualification, registrable in Queensland. More than four years’ experience in general practice or emergency and critical care. Membership of ANZCVS or an equivalent qualification is desirable, with a demonstrated commitment to ongoing professional development. Demonstrated commitment to relationship-centred veterinary care and teamwork across clinical and teaching environments. Proven ability to manage complex clinical cases and provide mentoring and clinical leadership to junior staff. Strong commitment to teaching and training veterinary science and veterinary technology students. Sound knowledge of legislative, professional and workplace health and safety requirements relevant to veterinary practice in Queensland. Highly developed interpersonal, communication, teamwork and consultation skills, with the ability to represent the School to industry, government and the wider community.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Wendy Goodwin w.goodwin@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, background checks, mandatory immunisations. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion. We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Sunday 25 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69194)"
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/University-Centre---Toowoomba/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow_R-68718-1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-25\nFull time\n\nRural Clinical School Full-time, fixed-term position for 12 months Base salary will be in the range $85,372 - $113,659 + 17% super (Academic Level A)  Based at our Toowoomba Regional Clinical Unit    About This Opportunity    Join a collaborative and purpose-driven research team dedicated to improving the health and wellbeing of regional, rural and remote Australians! This exciting Postdoctoral Research Fellow – Rural Health opportunity will enable you to build your research profile while contributing to impactful projects that address some of the most pressing healthcare challenges facing rural communities. Working alongside leading researchers in a supportive, inclusive and innovative environment, you will play a key role in advancing high-quality mixed-methods research, fostering meaningful partnerships, and translating research outcomes into positive change for communities. At UQ, you will be challenged, inspired and rewarded while working with like-minded colleagues who share a vision of creating change together.  Key responsibilities will include:     Research: Lead and contribute to high-quality mixed-methods research projects, publish in reputable peer-reviewed journals, present research findings at conferences, collaborate on interdisciplinary research initiatives, support the development of competitive research funding applications, and contribute to research that improves the health and wellbeing of regional, rural and remote Australians. Supervision and Researcher Development: Support the supervision of Honours and Higher Degree by Research students, contribute to researcher development activities, provide mentoring and guidance to emerging researchers, foster a positive and collaborative research culture, and demonstrate personal effectiveness in supervision and research capacity building. Citizenship and Service: Develactivities and productive partnerships with internal and external stakeholders, contribute to service and engagement activities, provide support and mentorship to colleagues, participate in relevant committees and administrative activities, and uphold the values and strategic objectives of the University.  This is a  research focused  position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s  Criteria for Academic Performance .     About You    Completion or near completion of a PhD in medicine, public health, rural health, or a related health discipline. Demonstrated experience leading, coordinating and contributing to high-quality non-lab-based research, including mixed-methods research projects. An emerging research profile in rural health, supported by publications in peer-reviewed journals and conference presentations. Evidence of contributing to competitive research funding applications and collaborative research initiatives. Strong interpersonal and organisational skills, with the ability to work effectively in a fast-paced, collaborative and supportive team environment. Understanding of, or experience working within, a rural healthcare or rural health workforce research setting, together with a current Queensland driver's licence.    About Rural Clinical School Located within the UQ Medical School, the Rural Clinical School (RCS) is the largest rural clinical school hosted by a single university. It works to foster and sustain a skilled and dynamic medical workforce to meet the needs of rural, regional, and remote Queensland. The Rural Clinical School provides students with rigorous clinical training built upon hands-on experiences in hospitals and community-based practices. Above all, we give our students the unique opportunity to live and work in some of Queensland’s amazing rural, regional, and remote locations. Our classrooms are the public and private hospitals, specialist consulting rooms, general practices and community healthcare settings throughout rural, regional and remote Queensland. Our teachers are dedicated and passionate rural clinicians. Our students get the opportunity to learn in a range of clinical contexts across the public, private and community sectors. The greater  benefits  of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a  Group of Eight  university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process.     Interested?   For more information about this opportunity, please contact Dr Bushra Nasir | Senior Research Fellow at b.nasir@uq.edu.au For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at  talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a  resume ,  cover letter , and  responses to the ‘About You’ section.        Other Information    Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, criminal history checks and mandatory immunisations. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.    We're dedicated to  equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing  talent@uq.edu.au  or calling +61 7 3365 2623.    Applications close Monday 19 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68718).  Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for the week of the 2nd November 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Gatton-Campus/Veterinary-Technician_R-68825-1": {
      "title": "Veterinary Technician",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-25\nPart time\n\nUQ VETS . Part-time (50%), permanent position, commencing 4 January 2027. Base salary will be in the range $79,096 to $83,863 + 17% super (HEW Level 4). Join our Gatton Campus community and take a virtual tour .   About This Opportunity Join the purposeful and collaborative UQ VETS Clinical Studies Centre, where you will help coordinate the Teaching Laboratory and provide skilled operational, technical and instructional support for undergraduate practical teaching, continuing education workshops and seminars. Reporting to the Clinical Studies Centre Manager and working alongside a team of veterinary, technical and animal care professionals, you will contribute to high-quality animal care, an exceptional student experience and the development of the next generation of veterinary professionals.   Key responsibilities will include, but not limited to: Coordinate the daily operation of the Teaching Laboratory, class preparation, inventory management, stock rotation, ordering and routine maintenance. Provide professional medical, surgical and technical support, including animal handling and husbandry, anaesthesia and post-operative monitoring, basic laboratory procedures, diagnostic clinical pathology and assistance with specialised imaging procedures. Support practical teaching and continuing education activities by working with academic staff, assisting students and preparing animals, equipment and facilities for teaching classes and practical surgery. Assist with clipping and sorting out cadavers for practicals. Contribute to responsive and efficient Centre operations through equipment care, record keeping, customer service, administrative support and the Veterinary School Animal Adoption Program.   About UQ VETS A career at UQ VETS means making a difference to not only the lives of the people and animals in your care, but also the next generation of vets and vet technicians. At UQ VETS, we offer the opportunity to build a dynamic career through structured development programs and access to industry leaders.   The Veterinary Teaching Hospital is an organisational unit within the School that includes all of the School’s outstanding clinical facilities. UQ VETS Equine Hospital is a largely referral-based hospital with advanced facilities and offers a 24-hour emergency medical and surgical service. UQ VETS Small Animal Hospital boasts the latest veterinary medical and surgical diagnostic and treatment options for small animals, birds, exotic pets and wildlife, as well as a seven-day urgent and emergency care service. UQ staff also supervise clinical placements at the RSPCA’s Brisbane Animal Care Campus, Wacol.   In particular, the UQ VETS Clinical Studies Centre facilitates and supports pre-clinical and clinical training in companion animals and is a renowned research hub for clinical and behavioural research. The Centre includes a 16-table surgical teaching facility and is also used for veterinary continuing education events. The Clinical Studies Centre is also home to the School’s Pets-for-Life Adoption Program.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a visionary, inclusive and collaborative community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available, such as: Free parking and an intercampus bus . On-campus childcare , a medical clinic and a post office. Up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave or paid primary care leave. 17% super contributions and 17.5% annual leave loading. Health and wellness discounts, including Fitness Passport access, free yearly flu vaccinations, discounted health insurance and access to our Employee Assistance Program for staff and their immediate family. Career development opportunities, including access to exclusive internal-only vacancies and our Study for Staff program. A 25% discount on eligible UQ VETS services . Access to our $4.1 million UQ Sport facility .   UQ’s commitment to advancing animal health is reflected in research that broadens understanding of viruses affecting people and animals. Read about UQ researchers’ identification of the first henipavirus detected in North America and their work to identify better ways to protect against this virus family .   About You You will bring practical veterinary nursing or technical experience, sound judgement and a genuine commitment to animal welfare and student learning. You will have: Qualifications and training equivalent to a Certificate IV in Veterinary Nursing, or an equivalent combination of relevant education, training and experience, together with a current open driver licence. Knowledge and experience in low-stress animal husbandry, handling, restraint and care, particularly involving dogs, cats, birds, horses, pigs and sheep. Experience working with animals in a laboratory, teaching, technical or clinical environment, including the handling of cadavers and assisting with animal euthanasia. Knowledge and experience in companion animal behaviour, medical and surgical nursing, and the monitoring of anaesthesia, analgesia and post-operative recovery. Knowledge of the relevant codes, legislation and ethical requirements governing the use of animals in research and teaching, together with a strong commitment to animal welfare. Strong organisational and record-keeping skills, with the ability to prioritise competing demands, work independently, use initiative and contribute to improved work practices. Effective interpersonal and customer service skills, with the ability to build cooperative relationships with veterinary professionals, academic staff, students, animal attendants, clients and other colleagues. Digital capability across word processing, databases, spreadsheets, email and practice management software, together with a commitment to UQ’s values of openness, respect and integrity.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Debra Laws d.laws@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns with the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, criminal history checks, mandatory immunisations. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We’re dedicated to equity, diversity and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Sunday 25 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68825)"
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/School-of-Social-Science--Casual-Academic-Opportunities_R-69676": {
      "title": "School of Social Science: Casual Academic Opportunities",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-25\nCasual\n\nAbout This Opportunity  The School of Social Science is seeking Casual Academics for Summer Semester 2026/2027. Casual Academics make a rich and important contribution to the School’s academic, teaching and research endeavours through the provision of a range of academically focused services and activities. Casual Academics may be engaged to fulfil a wide range of duties, including (but not limited to) lecturing; tutoring; marking; teaching; moderating/facilitating and/or student advising; across multiple academic areas.  Casual Academics are paid according to the category of their duties, as detailed in UQ’s Enterprise Agreement.   Casual Academic responsibilities may include (but are not limited to):  Lecturing/Teaching  Tutoring and Coordinating Tutoring  Assisting Course Coordinators  Marking  What We Can Offer  Current School of Social Science HDR students can apply to undertake casual academic positions for the courses listed on the School of Social Science casual academics webpage .  These are casual academic appointments. As such, there is no firm advance commitment to ongoing or indefinite work. The casual rate will vary depending on the type of work completed. Please view our various casual academic pay scales.    The marking rate for standard essays is 3,500 words per hour and the pay rate code is AC30A or AC30B.  For example, if marking 2,000 words for 18 students, then 2,000 x 18 / 3500 = 10 hours 17 mins. Initial marking hours are based upon expected enrolment figures, which often fluctuate in the first few weeks of semester. After census date, all marking is reassessed using census enrolment figures, which means that your allocated hours for marking may increase or decrease depending on student numbers.  For further information about UQ’s benefits, please visit Why Work at UQ and review  The University of Queensland Enterprise Agreement 2021-2026 (Enterprise Agreement).  Eligibility In the School of Social Science preference is given to our current HDR students and HDR students enrolled in the ISSR for employment opportunities. You must have work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.  We value diversity and inclusion, and actively encourage applications from those who bring diversity to the University. UQ’s Diversity and Inclusion webpage contains further information if you require additional support. Accessibility requirements and/or adjustments can be directed to recruitment@uq.edu.au . Want to Apply? All applicants will be asked to nominate their course and/or general subject area preference/s in the application form. In addition, all applicants must upload the following documents for an application to be considered: A copy of your current CV/resume A brief statement addressing: experience in tutoring/facilitating/marking undergraduate or postgraduate coursework; experience using online tools (Blackboard, and other digital tools, e.g., Zoom, Kaltura, or lecture recording software); and knowledge/expertise in the academic content of the course/s. Please note that you will be asked to add all documents into the one upload box during the application form. The submission of your application is appreciated as soon as possible however is due no later than 11.00 pm AEST on Sunday 18 October 2026. If you are a current employee of the University (including casual staff), or hold a HDR Scholarship with the University, please login to your staff Workday account and visit the internal careers board to apply for this opportunity. Please do NOT apply via the external job board."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Pro-Vice-Chancellor--Indigenous-Education---Research----Identified-s25_R-68552-1": {
      "title": "Pro Vice-Chancellor (Indigenous Education & Research) - Identified s25",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-28\nFull time\n\nThe University of Queensland Pro-Vice-Chancellor (Indigenous Education and Research) Full-time, 5 year Contract | St Lucia, Queensland| Senior Leadership Position The University of Queensland considers that to perform this role, it is essential that the person is an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander person. It is therefore a genuine occupational requirement under section 25 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 (Qld). The position is therefore only open to Australian Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander people.   About the organisation The University of Queensland (UQ) is one of Australia’s most established and respected institutions, known for shaping ideas, advancing knowledge and contributing to positive change for more than a century. UQ offers a rich and contemporary learning experience, combining flexible study pathways, strong industry and community partnerships, and a vibrant campus culture across its major locations in South East Queensland. Students benefit from an environment that blends academic excellence with practical, real‑world learning, supported by a long‑standing commitment to high‑quality teaching and an inclusive, globally minded community.   UQ is recognised for its research leadership, with a network of faculties, institutes and centres that bring together experts across disciplines to address complex challenges and drive innovation. The University has a strong record of translating research into real‑world impact and maintains active engagement with partners across government, industry and communities. As a member of leading national and international university networks, UQ plays a significant role in shaping higher education and research collaboration globally. Its governance is overseen by a Senate that provides strategic stewardship and ensures the University continues to advance its mission and serve the broader community. This position sits within the Office of the Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Indigenous Engagement), a University-wide division advancing UQ’s commitment to recognising Indigenous knowledges, connections to Country and respectful engagement with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples. Led by Professor Bronwyn Fredericks, the Division drives key priorities including the Reconciliation Action Plan, Indigenous employment pathways, student success, community partnerships and the integration of Indigenous perspectives across teaching, research and organisational practice. About the role The Pro‑Vice‑Chancellor (Indigenous Education and Research) is an accomplished Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander academic leader with Professorial‑level standing and strong community trust. The role requires deep cultural authority, a credible academic profile, and a demonstrated record of impact across Indigenous education, research and engagement. Working in close partnership with the Deputy Vice‑Chancellor (Indigenous Engagement), the PVC shapes whole‑of‑University priorities, guides major initiatives such as the Indigenous Curriculum framework, and contributes to senior governance forums with confidence and clarity.   Success in this position relies on collaborative leadership, cultural integrity and the ability to influence a complex academic environment. The PVC fosters meaningful relationships with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities and organisations, champions ethical and sustained partnerships, and strengthens pathways for Indigenous students, researchers and staff. The role calls for highly developed interpersonal and cross‑cultural capability, a steady and reliable presence in senior leadership, and a commitment to advancing reconciliation, Indigenous excellence and long‑term institutional change.   For a full list of responsibilities for the above role and to view the information booklet, please contact Pipeline Talent via jobs@pipelinetalent.com.au .   About you You will be an accomplished Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander academic leader with Professorial‑level standing, recognised for strong community trust, cultural authority and a demonstrated record of impact across Indigenous education, research and engagement.   Bringing credible academic expertise and a collaborative leadership style, you will help shape whole‑of‑University priorities alongside the Deputy Vice‑Chancellor (Indigenous Engagement), guide major initiatives such as the Indigenising Curriculum framework, and contribute meaningfully to senior governance forums. Steady, culturally grounded and influential through relationships rather than directive authority, you will foster ethical engagement with communities, strengthen pathways for Indigenous students, researchers and staff, and advance reconciliation, Indigenous excellence and long‑term institutional change across the University.   Selection Criteria Demonstrated academic leadership at Professorial level, including experience contributing to curriculum, research or governance in a university setting. Proven ability to influence change across a complex organisation, working through relationships, collaboration and cultural authority rather than directive power. Experience applying evidence‑informed and culturally grounded practices to strengthen Indigenous education, research or student success. Credibility to represent Indigenous priorities and perspectives across senior committees, faculties and external partners, with strong cultural integrity and community trust. Strong relationship‑management and communication skills, including cross‑cultural capability, negotiation, diplomacy and the ability to build alignment across diverse stakeholders.   What we can offer you 17% superannuation Cultural Support On-site childcare and school holiday programs Access to Employee Assistance Program  Generous annual leave, personal leave, parental and adoption leave, carer's leave and cultural leave Staff Discounts (including discounted on-campus parking)   How to apply Your application should include: A current resume. A cover letter responding to the selection criteria and outlining your suitability for the position.   Applications are to be submitted via the Pipeline Talent website at: https://www.pipelinetalent.com.au   Applications close 11:59pm Sunday, 25th of October 2026   How to get more information For further information on the position, please contact the team at Pipeline Talent. Dave Collins Director, Recruitment E: dave@pipelinetalent.com.au | jobs@pipelinetalent.com.au PH: 02 8001 6603"
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Lecturer---Senior-Lecturer-in-Economics_R-69343-1": {
      "title": "Lecturer / Senior Lecturer in Economics",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-28\nFull time\n\nSchool of Economics based at UQ’s Faculty of Business, Economics and Law (BEL) Two full-time (100%) permanent positions available Positions can be offered at Academic Level B or C: Base salary will be in the range $119,462.94 - $141,545.09 + 17% super (Academic Level B) Base salary will be in the range $145,961.51 - $168,043.68 + 17% super (Academic Level C) Based at UQ’s vibrant St Lucia Campus .   About This Opportunity  As a teaching and research academic Lecturer (Level B) or Senior Lecturer (Level C) in one of Australia’s leading economics schools, you will join a vibrant, internationally recognised academic community, committed to teaching excellence and international scholarly recognition in your field.   For US applicants: A Lecturer (Level B) is broadly comparable to an Assistant Professor in the US, while a Senior Lecturer (Level C) is a more senior appointment with greater expectations across research, teaching and academic leadership.   Key responsibilities at Academic Level B (Lecturer) will include:   Teaching: Contribute to the development of new programs and course materials in the area of focus, in consultation with program advisors and stakeholders, ensuring courses are engaging, relevant and contemporary. Teach across a range of settings and actively contribute to educational practice and innovative curriculum design, including online learning and alternative teaching methods. Coordinate courses, prepare and deliver lectures and tutorials, and undertake assessment and marking for undergraduate and postgraduate courses. Maintain and improve course quality, informed by evaluation tools, to meet industry and educational standards. Provide high-quality support to students, including academic counselling and advice. Understand and apply University rules relevant to teaching and learning practice.   Research: Develop a publication record in world-leading or internationally excellent journals in any field of Economics. Work with colleagues and postgraduate students to develop and conduct joint research projects and prepare applications for competitive research funding. Contribute to applications for external research funding and actively seek, secure and manage research funding. Present at key domestic and international conferences and other leading universities.   Supervision and Researcher Development: Contribute to the supervision of master’s, honours and higher degree by research students. Contribute to the recruitment, selection and onboarding of appropriately qualified employees in accordance with University policies and procedures. Work to resolve conflicts and grievances promptly, in accordance with University policies and procedures.   Citizenship and Service: Actively develop partnerships and strengthen relationships with colleagues, industry, government departments, professional bodies and the wider community. Demonstrate leadership of self and others through mentoring and collaboration. Efficiently manage allocated internal service roles and processes, including participation in decision-making and service on relevant committees. Effectively perform a range of administrative functions as required. Provide support to other academic staff as needed, including during absences. Consistently demonstrate the UQ values.   In addition to the above, responsibilities at Academic Level C (Senior Lecturer) will include:   Teaching: Build and maintain internal and external education collaborations, and undertake and initiate professional learning in teaching. Lead programs, disciplines, plans, courses and/or student cohorts.   Research: Contribute to the development or delivery of partnerships with research end users and external collaborations that produce high-quality research outputs. Successfully build teams and contribute to discipline service, including service on editorial boards.   Supervision and Researcher Development: Establish a track record of successful supervision outcomes. Demonstrate responsible research conduct and lead others in this practice. Effectively develop supervisees’ capabilities and skills, and manage researcher development. Demonstrate leadership in facilitating engagement opportunities for supervisees.   Citizenship and Service: Consolidate an established record of internal and external service and make appropriate leadership contributions.   This is a Teaching & Research position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance and our Appointment Information Booklet .   About You  For Appointment at Level B (Lecturer) :   Applicants should hold a PhD in Economics or a directly relevant field by the time of commencement. You will also demonstrate: A growing profile in teaching and research in the discipline. An active research program and a pipeline of work in progress expected to lead to publications in world-leading and internationally excellent journals in Economics. The ability to deliver high-quality teaching at undergraduate, honours and postgraduate levels across a range of settings, including small and large groups. The ability to coordinate courses and contribute to the development and ongoing improvement of programs, curricula and course materials in the discipline. The ability to supervise honours and higher degree by research students through to successful completion. Experience in seeking external research funding, or a clear intention to seek, secure and manage such funding. The ability to contribute to internal and external service activities.       For Appointment at Level C (Senior Lecturer) :   Applicants should hold a PhD in Economics or a directly relevant field. You will also demonstrate:   An established profile in teaching and research in the discipline. An active research program, a significant track record of publications in world-leading and internationally excellent journals, and a pipeline of work in progress targeting these journals in Economics. A strong record of securing and managing external research funding. A demonstrated record of high-quality teaching at undergraduate, honours and postgraduate levels across a range of settings, including small and large groups. A strong track record of course coordination and contributions to the development and ongoing improvement of programs, curricula and course materials in the discipline. A solid record of supervising honours and higher degree by research students through to successful completion. A growing record of meaningful contributions to internal service, alongside a strong contribution to external service activities.   About UQ   As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.    The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process.    Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Professor Claudio Mezzetti via email c.mezzetti@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.     When you apply, please upload your resume, cover letter, responses to the ‘About You’ criteria, a job market/research paper, and two written references or letters of recommendation . Please also ensure you answer all required application questions. Please apply through our listing on the AEA JOE Network : HERE . Please note that applications submitted via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.     Relocating from interstate or overseas? We may support you with obtaining employer-sponsored work rights and a relocation support package, however upfront costs will still be expected to be covered. You can find out more about life in Australia’s Sunshine State here .     We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Sunday 1st November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69343). Please note that interviews and seminars are expected to take place over November and December 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Senior-Manager--International-Development_R-68746-1": {
      "title": "Senior Manager, International Development",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-28\nFull time\n\nUQ International Development (UQID), Global Partnerships Full-time (100%), fixed term position through to October 2029. Base salary will be in the range $140,766 - $148,862 + 17% super (HEW Level 9). Based at our picturesque and conveniently located St Lucia campus (occasional domestic and international travel will be required) About This Opportunity UQ International Development (UQID) is one of the leading university development groups in the Asia-Pacific region, helping build the capacity of people and organisations around the world to achieve their development goals. Leveraging the knowledge, expertise and leadership of UQ staff and researchers, UQID delivers impactful international development initiatives, customised training programs, advisory services and technical assistance. This senior leadership opportunity will see you lead a high performing and collaborative project delivery function, working alongside diverse experts, partners and stakeholders to create meaningful change through internationally funded programs and partnerships. Drawing on UQ’s vision of creating change together, you will play a pivotal role in ensuring projects are delivered with excellence, innovation and lasting impact. Key responsibilities will include: Providing strategic leadership of UQID’s project delivery portfolio, ensuring alignment with organisational priorities and development objectives. Leading complex international development projects and programs, including project governance, financial oversight, contract management, monitoring and evaluation, and stakeholder engagement. Building and maintaining strong relationships with government agencies, donors, managing contractors, UQ faculties, researchers and international partners. Driving continuous improvement through the development of scalable systems, policies and processes that enhance project delivery outcomes. Leading, mentoring and supporting multidisciplinary project delivery teams to achieve excellence, performance targets and meaningful development impact. Contributing to business planning, tender development, contract negotiations, forecasting and strategic decision making across UQID, including engaging with internal and external partners to identify and scope opportunities for international development activities and contributing to the design and development of long term projects and programs. About You You are a strategic and collaborative leader with a passion for international development and a track record of delivering complex programs that create meaningful impact. You bring strong commercial and stakeholder management capability, thrive in dynamic and culturally diverse environments, and are motivated by the opportunity to work alongside experts, partners and communities to achieve sustainable development outcomes. We welcome candidates from international development, higher education, government, consulting, donor funded program delivery and related sectors who can demonstrate transferable leadership experience and a commitment to excellence. Essential Degree/qualifications in Development Practice, Public Policy, International Relations, Business, Project Management, Government, or another relevant discipline, or an equivalent combination of education, training and experience. Significant experience leading complex project delivery, program management or international development initiatives, ideally within donor funded, government or international capacity building environments. Demonstrated success building and maintaining productive relationships with diverse stakeholders, including government agencies, donors, industry partners and senior leaders. Strong people leadership experience with the ability to guide multidisciplinary teams, drive performance and foster a collaborative, inclusive and high performing culture. Proven financial, contract and risk management capability, coupled with sound judgement, negotiation skills and a solutions-focused approach. Excellent written, verbal and presentation skills, with the ability to communicate complex information clearly and influence outcomes across a broad range of audiences. Desirable Experience contributing to the design and/or implementation of projects funded through vertical funds, such as the Global Environment Facility (GEF), Green Climate Fund (GCF), Adaptation Fund or similar multilateral financing mechanisms. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available. Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Melissa Tipping, Head, International Development, at m.tipping@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications and criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We’re dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion. We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Applications close Monday 19 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68746). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for 28 October 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow-in-Crop-Quantitative-Genetics_R-68155-1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow in Crop Quantitative Genetics",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-28\nFull time\n\nQueensland Alliance for Agriculture and Food Innovation. Full-time (100%), fixed-term position for up to 2 years. Base salary will be in the range $85,373 to $113,660 + 17% super (Academic Level A). Based at our St Lucia Campus .   About This Opportunity Join a visionary and collaborative research community as a Postdoctoral Research Fellow in Crop Quantitative Genetics. Working within UQ’s Queensland Alliance for Agriculture and Food Innovation, you will develop and apply advanced quantitative genetics methods to improve genomic prediction of complex traits in soybean. As part of the UQ-led ARC Training Centre in Predictive Breeding for Agricultural Futures, you will work with BASF and other research partners to explore Genotype-by-Environment-by-Management interactions and help refine innovative quantitative genetics methodologies for predictive breeding.   Key responsibilities will include, but not limited to: Research: Conduct univariate and multivariate statistical analyses of large, multidimensional crop datasets incorporating trait phenotypes, environmental descriptors and genomic information to investigate Genotype-by-Environment-by-Management interactions. Develop and apply mixed-model statistical methodology, including genomic prediction, crop simulation and machine learning, to improve prediction of crop yield and related traits. Supervision and Researcher Development: Assist with mentoring students, publish scholarly research and contribute to the development of collaborative relationships with industry, government, professional bodies and the wider community. Citizenship and Service: Demonstrate UQ values through purposeful collaboration, contribute to service and administrative activities, support colleagues and participate actively in priority activities across QAAFI and the ARC Training Centre.   This is a research focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance .   About You Completion, or near completion, of a PhD in Quantitative Genetics, Plant Breeding, Crop Genomics or a closely related field. An emerging research profile in quantitative genetics, predictive breeding, plant breeding or crop genomics. Demonstrated capability in the statistical analysis of large, multidimensional agricultural, genomic or environmental datasets. Experience with mixed models, genomic prediction, crop simulation, machine learning, hierarchical modelling or related quantitative methodologies. Evidence of publishing research in reputable refereed journals and communicating findings at conferences or other scholarly forums. Evidence of contributing to external research funding applications or other mechanisms that support collaborative research. The ability to work inclusively and effectively with researchers, students, industry partners and other research end users. A developing record of internal service, external engagement or contributions to collaborative academic and research activities.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.   UQ researchers are creating purposeful change across agriculture by combining crop science, genetics and collaboration with growers. In 2025, UQ announced the development of five new pigeonpea varieties to support the wider adoption of this legume in Australia. Read more about UQ’s pioneering legume varieties for Australian growers .   The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Dr Melanie Wilkinson at m.wilkinson2@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section . Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of qualifications and the right to work in Australia. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   Relocating from interstate or overseas? We may support you with obtaining employer-sponsored work rights, however upfront costs will still be expected to be covered. You can find out more about life in Australia’s Sunshine State .   We’re dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close 28 October at 11.00pm AEST (R-68155)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Herston-Campus/Manager--Infrastructure-Planning-and-Development--Rural-Health-_R-69044-1": {
      "title": "Manager, Infrastructure Planning and Development (Rural Health)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-28\nFull time\n\nFaculty of Health, Medicine and Behavioural Sciences Full-time (100%), continuing position Base salary will be in the range $120,317 to $134,892 + 17% super (HEW Level 8) Based at our Herston location , with travel to rural and regional clinical sites across Queensland About This Opportunity The Manager, Infrastructure Planning and Development (Rural) will coordinate infrastructure planning, development and optimisation across the Faculty’s rural and regional education and training network. Through complex stakeholder engagement with internal teams, health service partners, government agencies, community organisations and technical specialists, you will deliver fit-for-purpose infrastructure outcomes with tangible impacts on rural workforce development and healthcare. Your work will support evolving educational, clinical, research and accommodation needs while advancing the sustainable delivery of education and training throughout Queensland. Key responsibilities will include: Develop coordinated plans for the management, optimisation and future development of rural and regional infrastructure, including teaching, training, administrative and accommodation facilities. Lead investigations, needs analyses, feasibility assessments and business cases that inform evidence-based infrastructure decisions and future investment. Coordinate infrastructure projects from concept and planning through implementation, commissioning and review, monitoring scope, risk, timelines, budgets and outcomes. Build trusted partnerships and support effective governance, consultation, contracts, leases, licences and shared-facility arrangements across complex stakeholder environments. About You Relevant degree qualifications in project management, property, infrastructure, facilities management, business or a related discipline, or an equivalent combination of education, training and experience. Demonstrated experience in infrastructure planning, project development, facilities planning, property management or a related field within a large and complex organisation. Proven ability to coordinate infrastructure projects and planning initiatives involving multiple stakeholders, service providers and specialist consultants. Strong analytical and strategic thinking skills, including the ability to interpret complex information, assess emerging issues and provide evidence-based recommendations. Demonstrated capability in data analysis and reporting to support space utilisation, asset management, investment prioritisation and strategic decision-making. Experience using project management, asset management, property or business information systems to manage information, monitor progress and track outcomes. Demonstrated ability to collaborate with architects, engineers, project managers, contractors and other technical specialists, and communicate technical information clearly to non-technical stakeholders. Exceptional stakeholder engagement and written communication skills, with experience preparing business cases, strategic plans, reports, executive briefings and stakeholder communications. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available. Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on-campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Greg Hafner at g.hafner@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns with the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, and criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Sunday 25 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69044)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Gatton-Campus/Veterinary-Nurse-Intern_R-68872-1": {
      "title": "Veterinary Nurse Intern",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nUQ VETS . Full-time, fixed-term position for 12 months. Base salary will be in the range $70,319 to $77,400 + 17% super (HEW Level 3). Join our Gatton Campus community and take a virtual tour .   About This Opportunity We are seeking a motivated Veterinary Nurse Intern to commence their veterinary nursing career at UQ. Working within a collaborative and inclusive clinical environment, you will receive structured training and supervision while supporting veterinary staff and Clinical Academics involved in the clinical education of veterinary and veterinary technology students. This purposeful development opportunity will help you build practical capability, professional confidence and strong foundations for a rewarding veterinary nursing career.   Key responsibilities will include, but not limited to: Develop practical veterinary nursing skills under supervision while maintaining high standards of patient care and professional practice. Assess, monitor and assist with the treatment of patients under veterinary or nursing supervision, working closely with veterinarians, veterinary specialists and nursing colleagues. Assist with diagnostic procedures, clinical pathology techniques and the care, cleaning and maintenance of specialised clinical equipment. Provide professional customer service and administrative support to clinic clients, students, staff and other stakeholders, including assisting with reception and inventory activities when required.   About UQ VETS A career at UQ VETS means making a difference to not only the lives of the people and animals in your care, but also the next generation of vets and vet technicians. At UQ VETS, we offer the opportunity to build a dynamic career through structured development programs and access to industry leaders.   Instead of solely concentrating on meeting caseload benchmarks or revenue targets, your priorities lie in creating an impact, establishing a lasting impression on the field, and conducting work that brings about positive changes in the lives of animals.   The Veterinary Teaching Hospital is an organisational unit within the School that includes all of the School’s outstanding clinical facilities. UQ VETS Equine Hospital is a largely referral-based hospital with advanced facilities and offers a 24-hour emergency medical and surgical service. UQ VETS Small Animal Hospital boasts the latest in veterinary medical and surgical diagnostic and treatment options for small animals, birds, exotic pets and wildlife, as well as a seven-day urgent and emergency care service. UQ staff also supervise clinical placements at the RSPCA’s Brisbane Animal Care Campus at Wacol.   In particular, the UQ VETS Small Animal Hospital values every animal and offers unrivalled medical, surgical and diagnostic services for dogs, cats, birds, reptiles, small mammals and fish at our world-class veterinary hospital in Gatton. You can take a virtual tour of our Small Animal Hospital here .   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a visionary and collaborative community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available, such as:   Free parking and an https://campuses.uq.edu.au/information-and-services/services-and-shops/campus-bus/intercampus. On-campus childcare , a medical clinic and a post office. Up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave or paid primary care leave. 17% super contributions and 17.5% annual leave loading. Health and wellness discounts, including Fitness Passport access, free yearly flu vaccinations, discounted health insurance and access to our Employee Assistance Program for staff and their immediate family. Career development opportunities, including access to exclusive internal-only vacancies and our Study for Staff program. A 25% discount on eligible UQ VETS services. Access to our $4.1 million UQ Sport facility .   About You A Bachelor of Applied Science in Veterinary Technology, a Bachelor of Veterinary Technology, a Certificate IV in Veterinary Nursing, or an equivalent combination of relevant education and training. A strong interest in commencing and developing a professional career in veterinary nursing within a clinical teaching environment. The ability to communicate professionally and effectively with colleagues, clients, students, veterinarians and veterinary specialists. The capacity to learn and consistently apply clinic-specific protocols, patient care standards and safe work practices. A willingness to work flexibly, use initiative and contribute positively within a collaborative multidisciplinary team. The ability to handle, clean and maintain clinical equipment carefully and develop competence in diagnostic and treatment procedures. A current radiation licence, or the willingness and ability to apply for one. A commitment to ongoing professional development and participation in relevant professional or community service activities.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Martina Smith martina.smith@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns with the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, criminal history checks, mandatory immunisations. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We’re dedicated to equity, diversity and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Sunday 1 November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68872)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Senior-Manager--Technical-and-Scientific-Services_R-69406-1": {
      "title": "Senior Manager, Technical and Scientific Services",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nSchool of Agriculture and Food Sustainability. Full-time, continuing position. Base salary will be in the range $140,766 to $148,862 plus 17% super (HEW Level 9). Based at our St Lucia and Gatton Campus – take a  virtual tour !    About This Opportunity The Senior Manager, Technical & Scientific Services provides strategic leadership across two critical service streams within the School of Agriculture and Food Sustainability. You will lead a diverse, multidisciplinary team delivering scientific, laboratory, technical and operational services across UQ’s St Lucia and Gatton campuses. Working collaboratively with academic leaders, researchers and professional colleagues, you will shape a coordinated, whole-of-School approach to laboratory operations, field services, teaching support, research infrastructure, equipment investment and service improvement. This is a purposeful leadership opportunity to strengthen operational excellence while enabling high-quality teaching and research outcomes.   Key responsibilities will include: Provide strategic leadership and operational management for the School’s Technical and Scientific Services teams, building a positive, inclusive and accountable culture. Develop and implement a coordinated, whole-of-School approach to technical and scientific services, including teaching support resources, research infrastructure, fieldwork planning and equipment investment. Drive continuous improvement across laboratory access, safety, compliance, quality assurance, service delivery and sustainable scientific operations. Provide strategic and operational advice to the School Executive, oversee portfolio budgets and build effective relationships with academic, professional and external stakeholders.   About You Postgraduate qualifications together with significant relevant experience in scientific, laboratory or research service delivery, or an equivalent combination of relevant education, training and experience. Demonstrated experience in organic and/or inorganic chemistry analytical laboratories and broad technical, analytical and scientific knowledge across a range of disciplines, with exposure to agricultural, food, plant, soil, animal and environmental sciences. Demonstrated experience leading multidisciplinary teams in complex teaching, research, laboratory or field-based environments. High-level people, project and resource management capability, including the ability to motivate staff, strengthen team cohesion and foster clear communication. Strong operational judgement and the ability to translate strategic priorities into practical, coordinated and sustainable services. Experience in budget planning, financial oversight, quality assurance, compliance, risk management and workplace health and safety. Strong interpersonal and communication skills, with a proven ability to build collaborative relationships with academic, professional and external stakeholders. A commitment to client service, continuous improvement, research integrity, ethics, openness, respect and inclusion. Capacity to work across both the St Lucia and Gatton campuses, including travelling between locations.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a visionary and collaborative community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available, such as:   Free parking and an intercampus bus - Gatton Campus On-campus childcare , a medical clinic and a post office. Up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave or paid primary care leave. 17% super and 17.5% annual leave loading. Health and wellness discounts, including Fitness Passport access, free annual flu vaccinations, discounted health insurance and access to the Employee Assistance Program for staff and their immediate family. Career development opportunities, including access to exclusive internal-only vacancies and the Study for Staff program. A 25% discount on eligible UQ Vets services. Access to the $4.1 million UQ Sports facility .   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Felicity Ray at f.ray@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns with the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, criminal history checks and mandatory immunisations. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We’re dedicated to equity, diversity and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Sunday 18 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69406)."
