    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Mater-Misericordiae-Hospitals--QCH-South-Brisbane/Manager--Network-Engagement-and-Communications_R-67895-1": {
      "title": "Manager, Network Engagement and Communications",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nPart time\n\nChild Health Research Centre . Part-time (60%), fixed-term position for up to two years. Base salary will be in the range $120,317 to $134,893 + 17% super (HEW Level 8). Based at our Centre for Children’s Health Research, South Brisbane location. About This Opportunity Join the Child Health Research Centre as Manager, Network Engagement and Communications for the First 10K Days Child, Adolescent and Family Wellbeing Network. Working closely with the Network Convenor, leadership team and members, you will provide high-level strategic and operational leadership that turns priorities into purposeful initiatives, partnerships, events and communications. In this collaborative and inclusive environment, you will connect researchers, clinicians, policymakers, service providers, funders and community stakeholders, helping the Network exchange knowledge, translate research and create meaningful change for children, adolescents and families. Key responsibilities will include: Translate the Network’s strategic priorities into actionable workplans, deliverables, operational processes, milestone reports, briefs, communications and evaluation activities. Develop and implement an integrated communication and engagement approach that strengthens connections, collaboration, knowledge exchange, research translation and workforce capability across the Network. Build and sustain mutually beneficial relationships with researchers, partner organisations, clinicians, policymakers, service providers, funders and community stakeholders, while coordinating forums, training sessions, recognition events and partnership meetings. Collaborate with internal specialists to design and continuously improve practical, user-centred digital platforms, communication systems and engagement tools, while supporting effective governance, clear decision-making and sustainable partnerships. About You Demonstrated experience developing and implementing communication and engagement strategies within a large or complex organisation. Demonstrated ability to establish, manage and sustain positive relationships with diverse internal and external stakeholders, including researchers, partner organisations, policymakers, service providers, community stakeholders and funding partners. Experience contributing to communication, engagement, partnership or marketing solutions, including digital platforms, applications or other technology-enabled communication tools. Strong strategic and operational capability, with experience translating priorities into practical plans, deliverables, events, communications and measurable outcomes. Highly developed organisational, problem-solving and attention-to-detail skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities and deliver high-quality work. The ability to collaborate effectively with technical specialists, including information technology, digital, marketing and external vendors, to translate user needs into practical and intuitive solutions. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available. Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on-campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Dr Annie Chen ( chrc.centremanager@uq.edu.au ). For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, criminal history checks and mandatory immunisations checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Wednesday 21 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-67895). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for the week commencing 2nd November 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Principal-Consultant---Biosafety_R-68937": {
      "title": "Principal Consultant – Biosafety",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-01\nFull time\n\nHealth, Safety and Wellness Full-time, fixed-term position through to December 2028 $106,709 to $116,199 + 17% super (HEW Level 7) Based at our St Lucia Campus   About This Opportunity Help enable world-class research by making complex biosafety requirements practical, proportionate and workable. UQ is home to a diverse range of teaching and research involving biological materials, specialist laboratories and research facilities. Behind that work is a Biosafety Advisory team helping our researchers navigate biological risks safely, confidently and in line with regulatory requirements. As a Principal Consultant, Biosafety , you’ll become a trusted specialist adviser across a portfolio of laboratories, facilities and research activities, working directly with researchers, supervisors and managers to find practical pathways for safe and compliant research. This is much more than checking boxes. You’ll apply your scientific expertise and judgement to complex biological risk assessments, laboratory inspections, incident reviews, containment requirements and genetically modified organism dealings , translating technical and regulatory requirements into controls that work in real research environments. You’ll also join the team at an important time, creating capacity to modernise biosafety systems, strengthen guidance and assurance tools, improve training and stakeholder communications, and support implementation of the R3 Lab Activity Module. What will you get involved in? ·         Specialist biosafety advice: Partner with researchers, supervisors and managers to provide practical advice across a diverse portfolio of laboratories, facilities and research activities. ·         Risk and assurance: Lead biological risk assessments, facility inspections, incident reviews and corrective action monitoring, including assessment of low-risk genetically modified organism dealings and hazardous biological applications. ·         Turning regulation into practice: Interpret biological safety procedures, containment standards and regulatory requirements and translate them into proportionate controls for infectious agents, GMOs, biological samples and related materials. ·         Committee and governance support: Contribute specialist expertise to biosafety committee processes and support consistent, well-informed decision-making. ·         Continuous improvement: Help improve procedures, guidance, training materials, assurance tools and communications, while supporting the modernisation of UQ’s biosafety systems.   If you enjoy the point where science, risk and problem-solving meet , this is an opportunity to use your expertise to help researchers do ambitious work safely, rather than simply telling them what they can’t do. About You You’re a biosafety or biological sciences professional who can combine strong technical knowledge with sound judgement and a practical approach. You’ll be comfortable stepping into a laboratory, understanding the science and the risk, asking the right questions and working collaboratively with researchers to find an appropriate way forward. You’ll bring: ·         A degree in biological sciences, microbiology, biotechnology or a related field, together with substantial relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of education, training and experience. ·         Specialist knowledge of biological safety, containment, biological risk assessment and laboratory compliance. ·         Experience leading inspections, risk assessments, compliance reviews or incident investigations in biological or laboratory environments. ·         The ability to interpret policies, procedures, containment standards and regulatory requirements and apply them practically within research and operational environments. ·         Strong analytical and problem-solving skills, including the ability to identify systemic issues and develop practical, proportionate solutions. ·         High-level communication, consultation and influencing skills, with the ability to build credibility with both technical and non-technical stakeholders. ·         The ability to work independently, manage competing priorities and contribute effectively within a collaborative specialist team. ·         A genuine commitment to continuous improvement and supporting safe research practice. ·         Experience in a university or research institute, laboratory or facility management, biological compliance, Microsoft 365 or database systems will be highly regarded.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available. Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on-campus childcare, affordable parking and salary packaging. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Kerrin Henderson at k.henderson@uq.edu.au . For application enquiries, please contact the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , quoting R-68937 in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns with the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications and mandatory immunisations. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. This role involves entering laboratories and animal facilities where personal protective equipment must be worn and where exposure to animal allergens may occur. Immunisation against certain vaccine-preventable diseases, including hepatitis B where relevant to the work undertaken, may be required. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We're dedicated to equity, diversity and inclusion. We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Sunday 18 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68937)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Long-Pocket/Senior-Editor_R-69155-1": {
      "title": "Senior Editor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-01\nFull time\n\nUniversity of Queensland Press Full-time (100%), permanent position Base salary will be in the range $97,268 - $104,348 + 17% super (HEW Level 6)  Based at our  Long Pocket location      About This Opportunity  Join University of Queensland Press (UQP), one of Australia’s most respected publishing houses as a Senior Editor and help bring exceptional stories and ideas to life. Reporting to the Managing Editor and working closely with a collaborative, inclusive and purposeful publishing team, the Senior Editor will independently manage the editorial and production lifecycle of a diverse portfolio of adult fiction, non-fiction and children’s publishing titles. This is an exciting opportunity to contribute to a culture of excellence while working with authors, creatives and publishing professionals to create meaningful impact through books that inform, inspire and connect communities. The role reflects UQ’s commitment to creating change together through collaboration, innovation and high-quality outcomes.   Key responsibilities will include, but are not limited to:  Independently project manage the editorial and production process for multiple book titles from manuscript to print publication. Undertake complex structural editing, copy editing and manuscript assessment to maintain the highest editorial standards. Build and maintain strong professional relationships with authors, freelancers, designers, proofreaders, typesetters and other publishing partners. Write compelling cover copy and marketing content and collaborate with sales and marketing teams on title positioning and promotion.   About You   Tertiary degree in English, Journalism, Communication or a Diploma in Editing and Publishing. Minimum of three years’ experience working within a trade publishing house or another relevant editorial position. Experience in evaluating and editing the content, structure and format of a range of written material. Strong project management skills, from first author contact through to print. Advanced verbal and written communication skills with a demonstrated ability to develop strong professional relationships with authors and liaise with suppliers, including other editors, designers and proofreaders. Ability to work effectively under pressure to tight deadlines and within budget. Exceptional time management and organisational skills. Demonstrated ability to project-manage multiple, competing tasks. Excellent computer skills and experience working with Microsoft Office Suite, Adobe Acrobat and InDesign.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.   Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, free parking at Long Pocket and salary packaging.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Jacqueline Blanchard at jaqueline.blanchard@uqp.com.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.    When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Sunday 25 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69155)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Casual-Academics---UQ-Business-School_R-69862": {
      "title": "Casual Academics - UQ Business School",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-01\nCasual\n\nThe University of Queensland Business School   The University of Queensland Business School is independently ranked as one of the top business schools in Australia and among the leading institutions worldwide. Our mission is to cultivate courageous thinkers who empower future leaders to positively transform business and society.    Based at the St Lucia campus as part of the Faculty of Business, Economics and Law, the School brings together 12,000 students and 200 subject experts.     About This Expression of Interest   The University of Queensland Business School is seeking expressions of interest from Tutors and Facilitators to be engaged on a casual basis.    Casual Academics at The University of Queensland Business School play a significant part in the overall student experience. As such, our successful candidates will participate in the education of undergraduate and/or postgraduate students within the key discipline areas of Accounting, Finance, Business Information Systems, Management, Strategy & Entrepreneurship, International Business, Marketing and Tourism within the School by preparing and delivering tutorials, marking assessment items, conducting student consultations and fostering a positive learning environment. Facilitators are also being sought to assist in activity based seminars, tutorials and workshops.    These positions are located at our picturesque St Lucia campus , renowned as one of Australia’s most attractive university campuses, and located just 7km from Brisbane’s city centre. Bounded by the Brisbane River on three sides, and with outstanding public transport connections, our 114-hectare site provides a perfect work environment – you can enjoy the best of both worlds: a vibrant campus with the tradition of an established university.    Our Ideal Candidate   As a Casual Academic, you'll have a minimum of an undergraduate degree, with a minimum GPA of 5.0, enrolment in a relevant postgraduate program (coursework or HDR), and/or relevant Industry experience ideally within a business or related discipline. Your skills in teaching will be demonstrable and effective, with teaching evaluations (or other evidence) to support your claims. You'll be proficient in using eLearning systems, such as Blackboard, or are capable of swiftly learning new software. Experience in public speaking and leading small groups will be highly regarded, plus a proven ability to work autonomously and meet deadlines.    You must have ongoing unrestricted work rights in Australia to apply for this opportunity.     We value diversity and inclusion, and actively encourage applications from those who bring diversity to the University. Our Diversity and Inclusion webpage contains further information if you require additional support. Accessibility requirements and/or adjustments can be directed to recruitment@uq.edu.au .    What We Can Offer   This is a Casual position. As such, there is no firm advance commitment to ongoing or indefinite work. Casual Academics at The University of Queensland Business School are paid in accordance with The University of Queensland Enterprise Agreement 2021-2026 (Enterprise Agreement).    For further information on casual rates, please visit our casual academic salaries page .     We invest in the development of our Casual Academics through training sessions, plus a host of other University wide development initiatives.    Questions?   If you have any questions regarding this expression of interest, please contact The University of Queensland Business School's Casual Support team at casualsupport@business.uq.edu.au .    Want to Submit an Expression of Interest?   Please hit the APPLY button and follow the prompts.    Expressions of interest must be submitted by Tuesday 27th October 2026 at 11:00pm (AEST) for consideration for semesters 1 & 2 in 2027. If you submit an expression of interest after this date, you may be considered for vacancies that arise throughout the year or the year following.     If you are a current UQ Employee, unpaid staff member or scholar please login to your staff Workday account and visit the internal careers board to apply for this opportunity via our Internal Job Board.      To satisfy pre-requisite questions and ensure your application can be considered in full, all candidates must apply via the UQ Workday portal . Applications received via other channels including direct email will not be accepted.      Applications close: Tuesday 27th October 2026"
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Casual-Academic---UQ-School-of-Economics_R-69707": {
      "title": "Casual Academic - UQ School of Economics",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-01\nCasual\n\nSchool of Economics    Casual Position    Applications Close on 21 October 2026 at 11:00PM AEST     About this opportunity     The successful appointees will undertake delivery of tutorials with associated preparation, student consultation and marking in first year, second year, third year and postgraduate economics courses.     This position is based at the St Lucia Campus and successful appointees must be available to attend on campus.   Casual Academics are remunerated according to the category of their duties, as detailed in The University of Queensland Enterprise Agreement 2021-2026 (Enterprise Agreement) a nd reiterated in this job profile.        Delivery or presentation of tutorials Tutorial means any educational delivery described as a tutorial in a course or unit outline, or in an official timetable issued by the employer. Casual Academics in the School of Economics are required to deliver or present a tutorial (or equivalent delivery through other than face-to-face teaching mode) of a specified duration and relatedly provide non-contact duties in the nature of preparation, directly-associated and contemporaneous marking (see Enterprise Agreement), and student consultation.  These non-contact duties will be directly associated with, and arising from, the delivery of a specific tutorial.     Responsibilities as a Casual Academic may include (but are not limited to):   Preparing and delivering theoretical and/or practical tutorials which are engaging, context-relevant and where possible, participative.    Tutoring students in face to face delivery mode.   Providing support to students, including, consultation, and assessment feedback.  Undertaking marking of assessment tasks, as directed by course coordinators, with hours for marking to be confirmed by School Administration staff prior to commencing marking. Maintaining, and where possible improving, the quality of tutorials, as measured through evaluation instruments to meet industry and educational standards.  Providing tutorial support for other positions during planned or unexpected absences.  Understanding and applying University Rules relevant to teaching and learning practice.  Maintaining class participation and attendance lists as required.  Maintaining a high level of performance on evaluations by students.  Attending meetings, induction sessions or required training sessions.    Compliance     All Casual academics are required to comply with legislation and University policy relevant to the duties undertaken, including but not exclusive to:  The University’s Code of Conduct .  Requirements of the Queensland occupational health and safety (OH&S) legislation and related OH&S responsibilities and procedures developed by the University or Institute/School.  The adoption of sustainable practices in all work activities and compliance with associated legislation and related University sustainability responsibilities and procedures .  Requirements of the Education Services  for Overseas Students Act 2000, the National Code 2007 and associated legislation, and related responsibilities and procedures developed by the University.     About the School of Economics     UQ’s School of Economics is a leading provider of economics education in Australia and ranks in the top 50 among Economics departments worldwide for its research.    Located on the St Lucia campus as part of the Faculty of Business, Economics and Law, the School is home to more than 2753 domestic and international students, 17 professional staff and 66 active teaching and research staff. Its accomplished scholars claim a large share of Australia’s economics honours, national teaching awards, competitive research grants, fellowships, publications and media coverage. The School also has an established research centre and an active seminar and visitor program, providing exposure to researchers from around the world.      About You     Candidates enrolled in the BECON or BAFE must have completed at least 10 units (5 courses) of UQ ECON-coded courses by July 2026 and achieved a GPA of 6.5 or better in economics courses. Candidates enrolled in the BPPE, the Bachelor of Arts (Economics Major), the Bachelor of Business Management (Business Economics Major), Bachelor of Commerce (Finance Major), or the Bachelor of Advanced Business (Finance Major) must have completed at least 6 units (3 courses) of UQ ECON-coded courses by July 2026 and achieved a GPA of 6.5 or better in economics courses. Candidates enrolled in the Economics Masters program must have completed at least one semester full time of postgraduate study including 8 units (4 courses) of ECON-coded courses and achieved a GPA of 6.5 or better.     UQ PhD in Economics candidates are encouraged to apply and do not need to have studied previously at UQ to be eligible.   We welcome and encourage applications from female candidates.  What we can offer     This is a Casual position. As such, there is no firm advance commitment to ongoing or indefinite work. The casual hourly rate is $57.60 per hour, plus super.  For further information about UQ’s benefits, please visit  Why Work at UQ  and review Operational UQ Enterprise Agreement_2021-2026.pdf .      Questions?     To discuss this role please contact Marie Taylor at m.taylor16@uq.edu.au .        For application queries, please contact casualrecruit@uq.edu.au stating the job reference number in the subject line.      Want to apply?     All applicants must provide the following documents:    Resume  Most recent academic study report    To ensure your application is considered, you must apply via the UQ Careers portal by the job closing deadline. Applications received via other channels, including direct email, will not be accepted."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Legal-Counsel_R-69640-1": {
      "title": "Legal Counsel",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-02\nFull time\n\nLegal Services Division , based within the Office of the Chief Operating Officer Full-time (100%), permanent position in a high performing in-house legal team Strong support for your professional development and career growth Base salary will be in the range $120,317.28 - $134,892.52 + 17% super (HEW Level 8) Based at our vibrant St Lucia Campus   About This Opportunity  As Legal Counsel, you will be responsible for providing legal advice on a broad range of property and commercial matters including commercial property transactions, strategic projects and commercial contracts.  The position also provides legal advice on a wide range of other legal issues affecting UQ.   Working in an in-house environment, this unique career opportunity will allow you to be flexible and available to work across all streams of legal work (depending on client demands, expertise and priorities as directed by the Directors and General Counsel). You will undertake work ranging in complexity and will work with and under the direction of the Directors and other senior lawyers in Legal Services as well as assisting the General Counsel in managing the interests of UQ.   You will join a busy, supportive and collaborative in-house legal team that values professional excellence, sound judgement and frank and fearless advice. Your contribution will help protect UQ’s interests, strengthen decision-making and deliver meaningful outcomes across the University.   Key responsibilities will include but are not limited to:  Providing high-quality legal advice to senior leaders and key decision-makers across UQ on property, commercial, contractual and other legal matters. Drafting, negotiating and advising on commercial agreements and property documents, including sale and purchase contracts, leases, licences, easements, construction and development agreements, tender documents and collaboration agreements. Advising on statutory interpretation, governance, administrative law, privacy, confidentiality, financial accountability, policy development and compliance with legal obligations. Building trusted stakeholder relationships and contributing to continuous improvement, legal capability, precedent development and the effective management of current and emerging legal risks. A full position description is available on request by contacting talent@uq.edu.au   About You   You must be admitted as a Solicitor or Barrister of the Supreme Court of Queensland and have demonstrated relevant expertise and experience, with a minimum of four years’ post-admission experience preferred. You hold, or are eligible to hold upon appointment, a Queensland practising certificate. You have extensive experience in property and commercial law, including advising on property transactions, infrastructure or development projects, strategic procurement and complex commercial matters. You demonstrate strong legal drafting skills and the ability to analyse and resolve complex issues with sound judgement, commercial acumen and meticulous attention to detail. You communicate with influence and possess strong written, interpersonal and negotiation skills, enabling you to work effectively with a diverse range of clients and stakeholders. You can manage multiple matters, adjust priorities and deliver accurate, timely advice in a busy environment with changing demands. You build productive working relationships and can work independently while contributing positively to a collaborative, inclusive and service-focused legal team. You demonstrate integrity, resilience and strategic awareness, with the confidence to provide impartial and forthright advice while recognising when guidance from senior lawyers is required. An understanding of the legislative framework applying to UQ, or experience acting for a statutory body or government entity, would be advantageous. Experience in information technology law would also be well regarded.   About Legal Services Led by the General Counsel, UQ Legal Services is responsible for the delivery of legal services to the Vice-Chancellor, executive management, and other key decision makers at UQ to support the achievement of UQ’s strategic objectives while protecting its interests. The General Counsel reports to the Chief Operating Officer and is part of the Chief Operating Officer’s senior management team.   UQ Legal Services has 6 major practice areas comprising: Commercial Advisory and Contracts; Property and Projects; Disputes and Regulatory Law; Workplace Relations; Trusts, Gifts and Work Integrated Learning; and Research and Intellectual Property.   The Legal Services team provides legal advice on a wide range of areas, including: commercial and contract law matters; property law matters; information technology matters; dispute resolution and litigation; administrative law, including advice to ensure defensible decisions by key decision-makers; student disciplinary and appeals matters; research misconduct investigations and processes; research related contracts and advices; governance issues, regulatory issues and statutory interpretation; bequests and gifts to UQ; intellectual property issues.   You can find more information here about Legal Services at UQ.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.   Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Leigh Weyer - l.weyer@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.     When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, professional registration (e.g. QLS + possession of QLD Practising Certificate), criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close 18 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69640). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for the week commencing 26 October 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Casual-Academic---UQ-School-of-the-Environment_R-69870": {
      "title": "Casual Academic - UQ School of the Environment",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-02\nCasual\n\nCasual Academic - UQ School of the Environment Applications Close on 2nd November 2026 at 11:59PM AEST      About this opportunity   The successful appointees will support the Course Coordinator in the delivery of course aims and learning outcomes, in first year, second year, third year and postgraduate courses in the School of the Environment.   Casual academic positions are based at both St Lucia and Gatton Campuses depending on the course you are selected for. Successful appointees must be available to attend on campus.  Casual Academics are remunerated according to the category of their duties, as detailed in The University of Queensland Enterprise Agreement 2021-2026 (Enterprise Agreement) a nd reiterated in this job profile.      Responsibilities as a Casual Academic may include (but are not limited to):   Demonstrator support for the conduct of practical classes, demonstrations, student field work and excursions Marking Exam invigilation Preparation time Facilitation of a class activity not requiring the development of materials Consultation with students Attendance at induction sessions Undertaking of training as required Moderating discussion forums, being available for student consultation online or in person, being available for consultation/ assistance on a specific activity such as a simulation and where no preparation or follow-up is required outside of scheduled hours; and Attendance at, or involvement in, other academic activities as directed by their supervisor. Compliance   All Casual academics are required to comply with legislation and University policy relevant to the duties undertaken, including but not exclusive to:  The University’s Code of Conduct .  Requirements of the Queensland occupational health and safety (OH&S) legislation and related OH&S responsibilities and procedures developed by the University or Institute/School.  The adoption of sustainable practices in all work activities and compliance with associated legislation and related University sustainability responsibilities and procedures .  Requirements of the Education Services  for Overseas Students Act 2000, the National Code 2007 and associated legislation, and related responsibilities and procedures developed by the University.    About the School of the Environment Established in 2023, UQ’s  School of the Environment  brings together multidisciplinary researchers across the physical, biological and social sciences to address the UN Sustainable Development Goals—particularly climate action, life on land, and life below water—through globally relevant research, training and impact. The School makes a major contribution to UQ’s standing, ranking 1st in Australia and 7th globally for Environment/Ecology, and 1st in Australia and 13th worldwide for Environmental Science & Engineering (2025 NTU Performance Ranking of Scientific Papers for World Universities). These strengths support a research culture that connects world‑class capability with real‑world impact.  The  School’s research  spans environmental management; geography and geospatial science; earth and ocean science; biodiversity and conservation science, ecology, genetics and evolution; zoology; and marine biology. With more than 100 academic group leaders, over 100 postdoctoral fellows, UQ’s largest graduate student cohort, and more than $26M in external research funding annually, the School offers a highly collaborative environment for ambitious research and education.  Research within the School integrates fundamental and applied approaches to advance environmental knowledge and deliver practical solutions. This work is supported by specialist analytical and field capabilities in biomonitoring and environmental diagnostics, spatial and statistical analysis, sustainability assessment, and  geoanalytical and chemical methods . Strong partnerships with industry, government and community organisations further enhance research translation and impact. Researchers and students also benefit from three field‑based stations at  Heron Island ,  Moreton Bay  and  Hidden Vale .  The School delivers a comprehensive and interdisciplinary curriculum, emphasising theory, practice and professionalism, and serving more than 1,600 coursework students across undergraduate and postgraduate programs. Learning is enriched through contemporary digital platforms, high‑quality workshops and practical activities, and exceptional field experiences in Australia and overseas.  About You   We welcome expressions of interest from research assistants, postdoctoral fellows, honours, undergraduate/postgraduate and Higher Degree Research students, and applicants who have previously taught in their field at a significant level who want to return to the workforce.  What we can offer     This is a  casual position. As such, there is no firm advance commitment to ongoing or indefinite work. The casual hourly rate can be viewed  here .   Most casual academics rates will be within the AC30 and AC50 pay scales.    For further information about UQ’s benefits, please visit  Why Work at UQ  and review  Operational UQ Enterprise Agreement_2021-2026.pdf .   Questions?     To discuss this role please contact SENV CAHP team: senv.cahp@uq.edu.au . For application queries, please contact casualrecruit@uq.edu.au stating the job reference   Want to apply?      All applicants must provide the following documents:     Resume      To ensure your application is considered, you must apply via the UQ Careers portal by the job closing deadline. Applications received via other channels, including direct email, will not be accepted.     If you are a current employee of the University, including current casual staff and HDR scholars, please login to your staff Workday account and visit the internal careers board to apply for this opportunity. Please do NOT apply via the external job board."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Director--Planning-and-Business-Intelligence_R-68685-1": {
      "title": "Director, Planning and Business Intelligence",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-02\nFull time\n\nLead a highly regarded Planning and Business Intelligence function at a globally recognised university Partner with the Vice-Chancellor, Provost, and senior leaders to shape the University's planning, performance, reporting, analytics and business intelligence Guide a high-performing multidisciplinary team and collaborate with key stakeholders across the University About the Opportunity Universities today operate in an increasingly complex and rapidly changing environment. Within this context, Planning and Business Intelligence (PBI) is a trusted resource for UQ's senior management and broader community, providing the management information and analysis that underpin evidence-based decision-making. PBI delivers five key functions: Strategic and operational planning Business intelligence Federal Government reporting Student load planning, admissions and program development Strategic analysis, surveys and dashboards This is a rare opportunity to influence how one of Australia's leading universities understands its performance, responds to a rapidly changing higher education landscape and makes strategic decisions about its future. Reporting to the Provost and working closely with the Vice-Chancellor and senior leaders, you will lead the University's planning, performance, reporting, analytics and business intelligence functions. You will provide high-quality information, analysis and advice on institutional performance, higher education policy and funding, student demand and growth, resource planning, and other critical matters informing UQ's strategic direction. Leading a high-performing, multidisciplinary team, you will work across the University to ensure leaders have the data and insights they need to plan effectively, respond to change and make well-informed decisions. About You We are seeking a strategic, collaborative and highly credible leader with relevant postgraduate qualifications and extensive experience in the Australian higher education sector. The ideal candidate will bring a strong understanding of government policy, funding methodologies and reporting requirements, together with demonstrated expertise in institutional planning, performance management, student load forecasting, admissions modelling and business intelligence. You will be an accomplished analytical thinker who can interpret complex quantitative and qualitative information, identify emerging trends and translate evidence into clear, practical advice that informs university-wide strategy, resourcing and performance. You will also be an experienced people leader who can guide and motivate a diverse multidisciplinary team, sustain a high-performing and responsive culture, and lead complex organisation-wide processes in a data-rich environment. Exceptional communication, influencing and relationship-building skills are essential, including the capacity to present complex analysis persuasively, advise senior decision-makers, negotiate strategic outcomes and represent the University effectively with government agencies, sector bodies and other key stakeholders. Learn More For a confidential discussion or to receive an Information Booklet, please contact: Andrew Barr , Practice Lead – Education, Slade Group Phone: 0400 762 660 Email: abarr@sladegroup.com.au How to Apply To apply, visit sladegroup.com.au and select Current Opportunities. Your application should include: A comprehensive résumé A cover letter A written response to the selection criteria (maximum two pages)   Applications close Friday the 23rd October, 11pm ( R-69525)"
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Senior-Manager--HR-and-Payroll-Operations_R-69868-1": {
      "title": "Senior Manager, HR and Payroll Operations",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-02\nFull time\n\nHuman Resources Division Full-time, fixed-term position until 31 December 2027 Base salary will be in the range $140,766 - $148,862 + 17% super (HEW Level 9)  Based at our  St Lucia location      About This Opportunity  As the Senior Manager, Salary and Benefits , you will lead the strategic and operational delivery of the University's salary, superannuation, payroll, and employee benefits services. Playing a pivotal role within Human Resources, you will ensure our remuneration and benefits programs are compliant, efficient, and employee-focused, supporting a diverse workforce across a large and complex organisation. Leading a team of payroll, superannuation and benefits professionals, you will drive continuous improvement, enhance service delivery, and provide expert advice on remuneration, superannuation, salary packaging, taxation, and legislative compliance. This is an outstanding opportunity for an experienced leader to influence enterprise-wide practices while ensuring exceptional outcomes for staff and stakeholders. Duties and responsibilities include, but are not limited to: Lead the delivery of payroll, superannuation, salary packaging, and employee benefits services, ensuring compliance with legislative, regulatory, audit, and governance requirements. Provide strategic and operational leadership in the management of complex payroll and remuneration matters, including employee benefits, taxation, salary packaging, overpayments, and payroll risk management. Act as the University's subject matter expert, delivering specialist advice on superannuation, payroll legislation, taxation requirements, and employee benefits programs. Drive business improvement initiatives to enhance operational efficiency, service quality, and stakeholder experience while ensuring compliance with UniSuper performance standards. Oversee financial reporting activities, including the preparation and distribution of monthly payroll and benefits-related reports. Lead reviews of business processes, systems, and policies to identify opportunities for innovation, continuous improvement, and risk mitigation. About UQ  As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of  benefits and rewards  are available.   Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging.    About You You are an experienced people leader with deep expertise in payroll operations, employee benefits, and remuneration governance within a large and complex environment. You combine technical knowledge with a strategic mindset and are passionate about delivering exceptional service and continuous improvement. You will bring: Postgraduate qualifications in a relevant discipline and extensive leadership experience, or an equivalent combination of education, training, and significant professional expertise. Comprehensive knowledge of payroll, superannuation, salary packaging, taxation, and employment-related legislation. Demonstrated success leading complex payroll or remuneration functions within a large organisation. Strong understanding of risk management, governance, compliance, and audit requirements relating to payroll and employee benefits. Proven ability to influence, negotiate, and build trusted relationships with stakeholders at all levels. Exceptional leadership capabilities, with a track record of developing high-performing teams through coaching, mentoring, and professional development. Strong analytical and problem-solving skills, with the ability to navigate complex remuneration and payroll matters. A commitment to continuous improvement and delivering a responsive, customer-focused service experience.   Interested?  For more information about this opportunity, please contact Sandy Tacey, Director, People Services at   s.tacey@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at  talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section.  Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.     Other Information   Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, and criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to  equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing  talent@uq.edu.au  or calling +61 7 3365 2623.  Applications close Sunday 18 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69868). Applications will be reviewed as received and UQ reserves the right to close this recruitment process prior to the advertised close date."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Senior-Principal-Consultant--Work-Injury-Management_R-70087-1": {
      "title": "Senior Principal Consultant, Work Injury Management",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-12\nFull time\n\nHealth, Safety and Wellness Full-time, fixed-term appointment through to 31 January 2027, with the possibility of extension Base salary of $120,317 - $134,893 + 17% superannuation (HEW Level 8) Based at UQ’s St Lucia Campus   About This Opportunity This is an excellent opportunity for an experienced workers’ compensation professional to step into a senior role within one of Queensland’s largest and most complex organisations. Joining UQ’s Health, Safety and Wellness team, you will bring your expertise to a large self-insured workers’ compensation environment, managing complex physical and psychological injury claims and working closely with a broad range of internal and external stakeholders. This fixed-term appointment offers the chance to make an immediate impact, broaden your experience within a large self-insured organisation and work across matters that will genuinely draw on your technical expertise, judgement and stakeholder management capability. With the possibility of extension, you’ll have the opportunity to gain valuable experience within UQ while contributing to meaningful outcomes from day one. This opportunity may particularly suit an experienced claims or injury management professional looking to broaden their self-insurance experience, step into a senior-level role, or gain exposure to a large and complex organisational environment. As Senior Case Manager, Work Injury Management , you will have the autonomy to manage complex matters while also contributing your expertise across the broader team through strategic claim reviews, coaching, mentoring and continuous improvement initiatives. Key responsibilities will include: Manage a portfolio of complex physical and psychological injury workers’ compensation claims, from investigation and determination through to rehabilitation, return to work and claim resolution. Apply your technical expertise and professional judgement to complex matters involving rejection, cessation, review, appeal and common law claims. Coordinate medico-legal reports, submissions, reasons for decision and other complex claims documentation within UQ’s self-insured environment. Partner with injured workers, leaders, treating practitioners, medical specialists, rehabilitation providers, investigators and other stakeholders to achieve safe, sustainable and timely return-to-work outcomes. Provide specialist advice, coaching and mentoring to colleagues and stakeholders, including strategic review of complex and long-duration claims. Contribute to the ongoing improvement of Work Injury Management practices, policies and procedures, and provide leadership support across the team, including relieving the Work Injury Management Manager when required.     About You You will be an experienced workers’ compensation professional who is comfortable navigating complex and sensitive matters, exercising sound judgement and building trusted relationships with a diverse range of stakeholders. You will bring: Undergraduate qualifications in a relevant field such as law, business, management or allied health, with at least five years of subsequent relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and/or education and training. Strong knowledge of workers’ compensation principles and legislation, with demonstrated capability interpreting and applying Queensland workers’ compensation and rehabilitation legislation. Proven experience managing complex statutory workers’ compensation claims and common law damages claims. Highly developed investigative, analytical and problem-solving skills, with the confidence to exercise professional judgement and discretion in complex matters. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to negotiate, advocate and communicate sensitively with a broad range of stakeholders. A collaborative approach and demonstrated ability to establish trusted relationships with injured workers, leaders, health professionals, colleagues and other stakeholders. Leadership capability, including experience providing constructive coaching, mentoring and technical guidance to others. Demonstrated experience preparing complex management reports within a workers’ compensation claims management environment. Experience within a self-insured organisation will be highly regarded.   About UQ At UQ, you will be part of a community of talented people working together to create positive change. In addition to joining a collaborative and experienced Health, Safety and Wellness team, you will have access to a range of benefits designed to support your career, wellbeing and life outside of work. These include 17% superannuation, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on-campus childcare, affordable parking and salary packaging options. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Lucy Beikoff, Director, Health, Safety and Wellness at l.beikoff@uq.edu.au For application enquiries, please contact the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , quoting the job reference number below. To apply, please upload your resume and a cover letter summarising how your background and experience align with the ‘About You’ section. Applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications and criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this position. We are committed to equity, diversity and inclusion and recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ. We encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their capability and potential relative to opportunity. We are also happy to support accessibility requirements throughout the recruitment process. Please contact talent@uq.edu.au or +61 7 3365 2623 to discuss how we can assist. Applications close Sunday 25 October at 11.00pm AEST (R-70087)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Senior-Grants-Officer_R-67442-1": {
      "title": "Senior Grants Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-12\nFull time\n\nResearch Office 2 full time positions (one continuing, and one fixed term through to September 2027). Base salary will be in the range $106,709 - $116,198 + 17% super (HEW Level 7). Champion research excellence across diverse disciplines by leading high stakes grant rounds within a collaborative and supportive team environment. Move beyond delivery into leadership, where your judgement guides a specialist team and strengthens the bids UQ puts forward. Based at our Toowong High Street office. About This Opportunity The Research Office is seeking two Senior Grants Officers (Pre-Award) to help strengthen the quality, competitiveness and compliance of UQ’s national and international research funding bids. Working in an agile, collaborative and newly organised portfolio based team, you will partner with researchers, faculties, institutes and central teams to guide complex grant rounds from planning through to submission. This is a purposeful opportunity to step into senior advisory and leadership work, where your judgement, influence and eye for detail will help UQ researchers create change through research that matters. Key responsibilities will include: Lead end to end pre-award activities for complex national and international grant rounds, including planning timelines, coordinating inputs and managing competing priorities. Provide authoritative, practical and tailored advice on funding rules, eligibility, compliance and non-standard pre-award matters. Deliver high quality application reviews, guidance materials, workshops, presentations and briefings that build grant readiness across UQ. Mentor and support less experienced staff, contribute to a positive team culture, and identify opportunities to improve systems, workflows and service delivery. Additional requirements: Occasional travel to other UQ campuses. Additional working hours during peak periods. Leave restrictions during peak periods. About You You are a confident and collaborative professional who enjoys working in a fast paced environment where quality, stakeholder relationships and sound judgement matter. You bring a strong advisory mindset, can navigate complexity with confidence, and are motivated by helping researchers secure funding that delivers meaningful impact. Demonstrated ability to interpret and apply complex funding rules, guidelines and policy frameworks. Experience coordinating competing priorities, managing deadlines and delivering high quality outcomes in a deadline driven environment. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to provide advice, deliver presentations and build trusted stakeholder relationships. Proven ability to exercise sound judgement and provide practical solutions on complex or non standard matters. Experience mentoring, coaching or leading others, and contributing to service improvement, process optimisation or operational excellence. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. In 2025, UQ News highlighted the continuing impact of research funding, including a UQ researcher receiving $450,000 from the Passe & Williams Foundation to improve health outcomes for patients with a rare form of cancer. Read more about UQ research grants . Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available. Enjoy up to 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program and salary packaging. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Josh at j.sweeney@research.uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. If you wish to be considered for only one of the available positions (either the continuing or fixed term opportunity), please indicate this in your cover letter. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia and criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Wednesday 21 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-67442). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for 29 October 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Long-Pocket/Administration-Officer--UQ-Biological-Resources-_R-69230-1": {
      "title": "Administration Officer (UQ Biological Resources)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-12\nFull time\n\nUQ Biological Resources Full-time, fixed-term position up to 22 October 2027 Base salary will be in the range $85,451 - $94,983 + 17% super (HEW Level 5) Based at our Long Pocket campus location   About This Opportunity Join the UQ Biological Resources (UQBR) team in this dynamic Administration Officer role, where you’ll play an important part in supporting the smooth and efficient operation of one of the University’s key research infrastructure centres. Based at Long Pocket, you will contribute to the coordination of essential administrative and operational processes that enable world-class animal research at UQ. Working within a collaborative and supportive team environment, you will help streamline workflows, maintain critical animal management database systems, support continuous improvement initiatives and provide high-quality administrative services across UQBR operations. This is an excellent opportunity for a motivated and organised professional to contribute to a purpose-driven team that plays a vital role in advancing UQ’s research excellence. Key responsibilities will include: Maintain and update the Mosaic animal management database, support data integrity activities, assist with ethics protocol management, create, and review online training material and provide training and guidance to staff and students Support daily animal movements, orders, sales and deliveries across UQ campuses, ensuring efficient and accurate administration processes, when required Conduct asset stocktakes, maintain equipment and asset registers, coordinate equipment movements, and support office administration systems and workflows Provide specialised administrative support to the Director and Administration Manager, including stakeholder liaison, billing processes, reporting assistance, record management and general operational support About You Completion of a degree with limited subsequent relevant experience; or completion of an advanced diploma qualification with at least one year of subsequent relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and education/training Demonstrated administration and coordination experience, including the ability to review work practices and contribute to improvements in procedures and processes Strong proficiency in Microsoft Office applications and experience maintaining databases, managing data integrity and supporting and training users Excellent interpersonal and communication skills, with a demonstrated ability to provide high-quality customer service and build effective working relationships with a diverse range of stakeholders Strong organisational and time management skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities and meet deadlines in a dynamic environment Demonstrated initiative, sound judgement and the ability to work both independently and collaboratively as part of a team An understanding of research involving animals and the importance of accurate data collection, compliance and record keeping Demonstrated understanding of relevant Animal Ethics, Biosafety, Biosecurity and Workplace Health and Safety requirements, or the ability to rapidly acquire this knowledge About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside some of the brightest minds from around the world in an environment where collaboration, innovation and purpose drive meaningful outcomes. We are committed to creating change together through research excellence, operational excellence and a culture that values diversity of thought and experience. At UQ, you’ll enjoy a workplace where your contribution is recognised and where you can continue to grow your career while making a tangible impact. The broader benefits of joining the UQ community include up to 26 weeks paid parental leave, 17% superannuation, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements where operationally appropriate, access to health and wellbeing initiatives, and ongoing professional development opportunities. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Rebecca Bruce at r.bruce1@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, criminal history checks, mandatory immunisations, First Aid and/or CPR certification, and other role-specific checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. Working with laboratory animals is an inherent requirement of the position. The appointment is therefore subject to, and conditional upon, satisfactory medical clearance(s), including disclosure of relevant medical history, to undertake such work without unreasonable risk to your health and safety. In addition, the employee is required to fully comply with necessary ongoing health monitoring procedures and control measures associated with the position. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion. We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Monday 26 October at 11.00pm AEST (R-69230)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Gatton-Campus/Security-Officer---Gatton-Campus_R-70033-2": {
      "title": "Security Officer - Gatton Campus",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-12\nFull time\n\nProperty and Facilities Division, Security Full-time, continuing and casual leave-coverage roles available Base Salary of $70,318 + 17% super (HEW Level 3) Casual rate of $45.55 per hour, inclusive of 25% casual loading Based at UQ’s Gatton Campus   About UQ's Gatton Campus At The University of Queensland, we are creating change together. UQ Gatton combines the proud rural traditions of its Queensland Agricultural College heritage with innovative teaching and research in agriculture, animal science, veterinary science and the environment. With expansive grounds, specialist facilities and a strong sense of community, Gatton offers a workplace unlike any other UQ campus. You will work alongside passionate, purpose-driven people while helping create a safe, welcoming and supportive environment for students, staff and visitors. About This Opportunity Join the Security team within Property and Facilities and play an important role in protecting the people, facilities and grounds of UQ’s Gatton Campus. We currently have full-time, continuing and casual leave-coverage roles available within our close-knit operational team. This is a varied, hands-on role where no two shifts are quite the same. You may respond to an emergency, assist a student or visitor, patrol campus facilities, investigate an incident or support a major event. Throughout each shift, you will provide a professional, calm and responsive security service that supports the safety and wellbeing of the wider UQ Gatton community. UQ Gatton operates 24 hours a day, seven days a week. We are looking for officers who are comfortable working independently, following established procedures and exercising sound judgement while remaining connected to central security operations. The casual role will provide important coverage during planned leave, unexpected absences and other operational requirements. Please note: Hours for the casual role are not guaranteed, and shifts may be offered at short notice depending on operational requirements. Key responsibilities include: Responding to alarms, medical emergencies, fire emergencies and other incidents as they arise. Locking, patrolling and providing access to authorised personnel across University facilities and grounds. Conducting preliminary investigations and preparing accurate incident reports using established systems and procedures. Providing professional assistance and responding to enquiries from students, staff and members of the public. Enforcing parking and traffic regulations where required. Escalating priority and emergency matters to the Security Supervisor in a timely manner.   Please Note: A successful applicant will need full availability to attend Three weeks of full-time training . About You You are a calm, reliable and approachable security officer who can follow established procedures, work independently when required and make sound decisions when responding to incidents. To be successful in this role, you will demonstrate: A current Queensland Certificate II in Security Operations, including Security Officer Unarmed, Crowd Control, Bodyguard and Monitoring modules Current HLTAID011 Provide First Aid certification Current HLTAID009 Provide Cardiopulmonary Resuscitation (CPR) certification A current Queensland manual open driver licence The ability to work independently with minimal supervision in a regional campus environment Sound judgement and confident decision-making during emergency and operational situations Strong communication and interpersonal skills, with the ability to engage respectfully with people from diverse backgrounds Experience using security monitoring systems and preparing accurate incident reports Professionalism, resilience, tact and restraint when managing challenging situations Experience responding to emergencies in security, operational or public-facing environments       The successful candidate will be required to complete background checks including right to work in Australia verification and a criminal history check. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this opportunity. What We Can Offer A full-time, continuing position or casual leave-coverage opportunity A supportive, experienced and collaborative security team Comprehensive training and ongoing development Varied work across one of Australia’s most distinctive university campuses The opportunity to make a visible contribution to the safety and wellbeing of the UQ community Employment with one of Australia’s leading universities   Questions? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Mr Andrew Barling at a.barling@pf.uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please contact the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number in the subject line. Want to Apply? All applicants must upload the following document for their application to be considered: Resume. Other Information UQ is committed to a fair, equitable and inclusive selection process which recognises that some applicants may face additional barriers and challenges that have impacted, or continue to impact, their career trajectory. Candidates who do not meet every criterion are encouraged to apply and demonstrate their potential. Assessment will consider both performance and potential relative to opportunity. We know one of our strengths as an institution lies in our diverse colleagues. We are dedicated to equity, diversity and inclusion, fostering an environment that reflects our wider community. We are committed to attracting, retaining and promoting diverse talent. Please reach out to talent@uq.edu.au if you require accessibility support or adjustments throughout the recruitment process. Applications close Sunday 25 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-70033)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow-in-Coastal-Restoration-Law_R-69621": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow in Coastal Restoration Law",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-12\nPart time\n\nUQ Law School within the Faculty of Business, Economics and Law Part-time (80%), fixed-term position commencing in early January 2027 through to December 2028. Base salary will be in the range $85,372.89 to $113,659.72 + 17% super (Academic Level A). Based at our vibrant St Lucia Campus     About This Opportunity This is an exciting opportunity to join a visionary, multidisciplinary team working on an Australian Research Council-funded project examining regulatory permitting for marine and coastal restoration. Embedded within a collaborative research environment spanning law, ecology, modelling and social science, you will undertake purposeful legal research that traces existing regulatory requirements, identifies opportunities for reform and helps evaluate how proposed reforms could support effective restoration. You will work closely with academic colleagues, computational modelling experts, government and industry collaborators to create research with practical and globally relevant impact.   Key responsibilities will include: Research: Undertake high-quality applied legal research aligned with the aims of the ARC Discovery Project, contribute to the co-design and delivery of the project, and produce research outputs for highly regarded law and environmental policy journals. Research collaboration and engagement: Work collaboratively across law, ecology, modelling and social science, communicate legal research effectively to multidisciplinary audiences, and engage constructively with government, industry and other research end users. Supervision and Researcher Development: Contribute to the effective supervision and development of Honours and Higher Degree by Research students, as appropriate, and support a responsible, inclusive and collaborative research culture. Citizenship and Service: Demonstrate UQ values through active participation in priority activities, internal service and administrative processes, support for colleagues, and the development of partnerships with government, industry, professional bodies and the wider community.   This is a Research Focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance .   About You Completion, or near completion, of a PhD in environmental law or a closely related discipline. An emerging research profile in environmental law, marine and coastal law, regulatory law or a related field. Evidence of producing, or the demonstrated potential to produce, high-quality research outputs in reputable refereed journals. Evidence of presenting research at academic conferences, professional forums or other relevant settings. Demonstrated ability to undertake rigorous applied legal research and analyse complex legislation, policy and regulatory frameworks. Experience collaborating across disciplines, ideally with researchers in environmental science, ecology, computational modelling or the social sciences. Strong written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to explain legal concepts and findings to multidisciplinary and non-legal audiences. Experience with social science research methodologies, including surveys and interviews, is desirable.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.   The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Professor Justine Bell-James at j.bell-james@law.uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section . Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia and qualifications. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We’re dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Monday 9th November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69621). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for late November, early December."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Finance-Partner_R-69986-1": {
      "title": "Finance Partner",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-12\nFull time\n\nFinance Professional Services Full time (100%); 1 x continuing position, 1 x fixed term position through to October 2027 Base salary will be in the range $106,709 - $116,198 + 17% Superannuation (HEW Level 7)  Based at our St Lucia Campus     About This Opportunity We are seeking a driven and proactive Finance Partner to support the Chief Operating Officer (COO) portfolio. Working within a collaborative and high-performing finance advisory team, you will partner with senior leaders to provide trusted financial insights that support informed decision-making and strategic outcomes. This is an opportunity to build strong partnerships, influence key decisions and contribute to meaningful outcomes across the University. Key responsibilities will include: Providing trusted financial advice to leaders and chief investigators to support cost management and the optimal utilisation of funding. Building and maintaining strong stakeholder relationships, acting as a trusted advisor and managing the escalation of complex financial matters. Applying financial processes, governance frameworks and policy controls to ensure compliance with University and external requirements. Facilitating collaboration between finance advisory and transaction services teams to enhance service delivery, financial performance and strategic outcomes. About You Degree qualifications in accounting, business, commerce or a related discipline, or an equivalent combination of extensive training and professional experience. Strong financial accounting, analysis and reporting capability, including experience delivering project-based accounting services. Demonstrated experience within financial advisory, business partnering or service delivery functions operating in complex stakeholder environments. Proven ability to build, influence and maintain productive working relationships with a diverse range of stakeholders and clients. Highly developed communication and problem-solving skills, with the ability to present insights clearly and drive informed decision-making. Demonstrated capability to provide strategically aligned financial advice that supports organisational priorities and objectives. Strong understanding of financial governance, compliance requirements, policy frameworks and risk management principles. Progress towards, or membership of, a professional accounting body such as CPA Australia, Chartered Accountants Australia and New Zealand, or equivalent. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available. Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on-campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Dane Cruickshanks at d.cruickshanks@uq.edu.au. For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au, stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section, and whether you are applying for the continuing or fixed-term position. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, professional registration, criminal history checks and other relevant checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion. We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Monday 26 October at 11.00pm AEST (R-69986)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Dutton-Park/Laboratory-Assistant--Sample-Management---Lab-Operations-_R-69643": {
      "title": "Laboratory Assistant (Sample Management & Lab Operations)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-12\nFull time\n\nQueensland Alliance for Environmental Health Sciences Full-time, fixed-term position through to 31 December 2027   Base salary will range from $79,096 - $83,863 (HEW Level 4) + 17% Superannuation Based at our Dutton Park campus About This Opportunity Are you a practical, organised, safety-conscious and team-focused person? Do you have experience supporting laboratory or research operations? If the answer is yes, we have the ideal role for you! Join the dedicated and supportive research team at the Queensland Alliance for Environmental Health Sciences and help deliver world-class research across environmental toxicology, epidemiology, microbiology, health risk assessment, and state-of-the art monitoring and analytical technologies. As a Laboratory Assistant , you will provide a range of technical, operational and administrative support that keeps our busy laboratories and research programs running safely and efficiently. Working collaboratively with researchers and support staff, you will help manage samples, equipment, consumables and laboratory records, contributing to vital research that identifies and responds to emerging environmental health risks. Key responsibilities will include: Receive, register, organise and store incoming research and environmental samples using laboratory databases and sample management systems. Maintain sampling equipment, laboratory consumables and inventory records, including ordering, receiving and registering new equipment. Prepare and distribute equipment and materials for clients, while maintaining accurate laboratory documents, databases and registers. Support the safe and efficient operation of laboratory facilities, including equipment and facility upkeep, glassware management, and chemical and clinical waste management. Please note, aspects of this role will require work in cold environments, including cold room and -20°C storage areas. About You Completion of a Diploma in Laboratory Technology, or completion of a Certificate IV with relevant work experience, or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and education or training. Experience working in a laboratory, research or similar technical environment. Experience using laboratory equipment and supporting equipment maintenance, consumables management, stock control and inventory activities. Experience receiving, recording, handling and storing research or environmental samples. Strong computer and database literacy, including experience maintaining accurate records and using electronic record systems. Excellent attention to detail, with the ability to prioritise and manage multiple tasks accurately. The ability to work independently under routine direction and contribute effectively within a multidisciplinary team. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.   Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program and other opportunities for career development and progression.  Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Gabriele Elisei at g.elisei@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (R-69643) in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns with the ‘About You’ selection criteria above. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks will include verification of your right to work in Australia and completion of mandatory immunisations. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Sunday 25 October at 11.00pm AEST (Job Reference Number: R-69643)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Customer-Service-Assistant_R-69823-1": {
      "title": "Customer Service Assistant",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-09\nPart time\n\nAustralian Institute for Bioengineering and Nanotechnology (AIBN) Part-time (60%, W,Th,F required), permanent position with a busy, collaborative team Base salary will be in the range $79,096.42 - $83,863.22 + 17% super (HEW Level 4, pro rata) Based at our St Lucia Campus   About This Opportunity  We are seeking an organised and reliable Administration Assistant to work 3 days per week providing essential frontline administrative support for the efficient operation of the Receptions team at the Australian Institute for Bioengineering and Nanotechnology. Working in a busy, collaborative environment, you will provide responsive reception duties, office coordination and administrative support that enables colleagues to focus on research with meaningful impact.   This is a part-time position working Wed, Thurs, Fri per week, with some flexibility for start/finish times and the possibility to work 0.6FTE across 4 days.   Key responsibilities will include:  Provide high-level administrative support to staff under general supervision, using sound judgement to prioritise activities and meet operational needs. Coordinate routine events, meetings and engagement activities, including scheduling, room bookings, documentation, minute-taking, RSVP monitoring and liaison with attendees. Assisting with organising student/visitor travel arrangements when required. Support efficient day-to-day office procedures, logistics and operations in accordance with relevant administrative protocols, quality assurance systems and standards. Liaise with internal and external stakeholders, respond to routine enquiries, welcome and brief visitors, maintain information systems and administer relevant financial transactions in accordance with University policies and procedures.   About You   You will have: A Certificate IV or Diploma with relevant work experience, a Certificate III with extensive relevant work experience, or an equivalent combination of relevant education, training and experience. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to communicate clearly and professionally with diverse stakeholders. Demonstrated experience providing responsive, high-quality customer service and building effective working relationships. Strong organisational and time-management skills, with the ability to prioritise competing tasks and meet deadlines. Well-developed problem-solving skills and a reliable, adaptable approach to day-to-day administrative requirements. A high level of proficiency in Microsoft Word, Excel, PowerPoint and Outlook. Experience coordinating meetings, events, documentation, visitors and office logistics. The ability to follow administrative, financial and quality assurance processes accurately while contributing positively to a supportive and inclusive team.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.   Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Lesley Green – l.green@uq.edu.au   For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.     When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close 18 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69823). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for the week commencing 26 October 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Principal-Officer--Corporate-Affairs-and-Stakeholder-Relations_R-69687-1": {
      "title": "Principal Officer, Corporate Affairs and Stakeholder Relations",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-09\nFull time\n\nMarketing and communication Full-time, continuing position. Base salary will be in the range $106,709 to $116,199 + 17% super (HEW Level 7). Based at our St Lucia campus. About This Opportunity Join UQ’s Corporate Affairs team to deliver strategic communication and stakeholder engagement programs that strengthen and protect the University’s reputation. You’ll contribute across a range of communication and engagement activities, from major infrastructure and campus development projects to corporate initiatives, industry engagement and incident management. You will help to build trusted relationships with key stakeholders including industry, government, partners, staff, students and the wider community while helping communicate UQ’s contribution to Queensland. Key responsibilities will include: Support the development and implementation of communication and stakeholder engagement programs for major infrastructure, campus development and corporate initiatives aligned with UQ’s strategic priorities. Develop clear, purposeful communication plans, materials and assets for a range of audiences, channels and formats. Contribute to industry and government engagement activities that strengthen UQ’s profile, positioning and relationships. Analyse and provide advice on potential stakeholder impacts and support relationship management practices across UQ’s activities. Support reputation management activities and contribute to communication responses during incidents and emerging issues.   About You Tertiary qualifications in communication, public relations, journalism or a related discipline, with at least six years of subsequent relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of relevant education, training and industry experience. Proven success in developing and implementing contemporary communication and stakeholder engagement plans for diverse audiences. Experience supporting infrastructure projects that require proactive engagement, careful relationship management and early identification of stakeholder issues. Highly developed written communication skills, with the ability to write clearly and precisely for multiple audiences, channels and purposes. Strong interpersonal and verbal communication skills, with a demonstrated ability to build collaborative and trusted working relationships. Excellent organisational and time-management skills, including the ability to plan, manage a high volume of work and respond effectively to changing priorities. Sound judgement and the ability to analyse potential stakeholder and reputational impacts in complex environments. Knowledge of, or experience in, the Australian higher education sector is desirable but not essential.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a visionary and inclusive community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available. UQ’s standing reflects the collective impact of its people and the strength of its relationships. In 2025, UQ was named among the world’s leading universities in the Times Higher Education World Reputation Ranking, rising to fourth in Australia and 78th globally. Read more about UQ’s reputation ranking . Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on-campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Rebecca Schrauf at r.schrauf@uq.edu.au   For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns with the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, professional registration checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Sunday 25 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69687)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Senior-Lecturer-in-Microelectronics_R-69524": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer in Microelectronics",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-09\nFull time\n\nFaculty of Engineering, Architecture and Information Technology / School of Electrical Engineering and Computer Science Full-time permanent position Base salary will be in the range $119,462.94 - $141,545.09 + 17% super (Academic Level C) Based at our St Lucia Campus   About This Opportunity  Join a visionary and collaborative academic community where you will help create change through world-class teaching, research, and industry engagement in microelectronics. The School of Electrical Engineering and Computer Science is seeking an ambitious and inquisitive Senior Lecturer to contribute to the development of future engineers while advancing impactful research in microelectronics and electronic systems.   Working alongside leading researchers, industry partners, and talented students, you will be supported by a connected environment that values innovation, excellence, and meaningful real-world impact. Drawing on UQ’s culture of collaboration and world-leading teaching and research, you will have the opportunity to build a distinctive research profile while contributing to the School’s growing strengths across sensing, instrumentation, communications, healthcare technologies, photonics, computing, and emerging technologies.   Key responsibilities will include:  Research: Establish and grow an internationally recognised research program in microelectronics, publish high-quality research outputs, attract and manage external research funding, contribute to translation and commercialisation activities, and develop strategic collaborations with industry and research partners. Teaching: Develop, coordinate and deliver high-quality undergraduate and postgraduate courses across computer architecture, analogue and mixed-signal design, digital and VLSI design, embedded systems and related areas. Lead curriculum innovation, coordinate courses, provide academic guidance to students, and contribute to continuous improvement of teaching programs. Supervision and Researcher Development: Supervise Higher Degree by Research students, foster researcher capability development, demonstrate leadership in responsible research conduct, and provide mentorship and performance support to staff and students. Citizenship and Service: Contribute actively to School, Faculty and University service, build strong external partnerships, mentor colleagues, support staff wellbeing, participate in governance activities, and embody UQ values through leadership and collaboration.   This is a teaching & research position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance .   About You  You will bring a strong academic reputation and a demonstrated commitment to excellence in teaching, research, supervision and service, together with: A PhD in Microelectronics, Computer Engineering, Electrical Engineering, VLSI Design, Electronic Systems Engineering, or a closely related discipline. Demonstrated expertise in microelectronics and electronic systems, with strengths in areas such as integrated circuit design, VLSI systems, computer architecture, embedded systems, RF systems, sensing technologies, photonics, communications, healthcare technologies, quantum technologies, or related fields. Expertise in analogue and mixed-signal integrated circuit design, digital and VLSI design, embedded systems, system-on-chip architectures, or related technologies, with experience using industry-standard electronic design automation tools considered advantageous. An established record of high-quality research outputs and growing international recognition through publication in leading journals and conferences. Demonstrated success in securing, managing, or contributing to competitive research funding opportunities. Evidence of high-quality undergraduate and postgraduate teaching, including curriculum design, course coordination, and innovative approaches to learning and assessment. A successful track record of supervising Honours and Higher Degree by Research students to completion. Evidence of leadership, collaboration, and meaningful contributions to internal service, external engagement, industry partnerships, and professional activities.   We particularly welcome applications from women, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people with disability, members of the LGBTQIA+ community, and others who may be underrepresented in this discipline.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.   The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Professor Markus Barth m.barth@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.    When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume including your public Google Scholar profile , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia and qualification checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   Relocating from interstate or overseas? We may support you with obtaining employer-sponsored work rights and a relocation support package, however upfront costs will still be expected to be covered. You can find out more about life in Australia’s Sunshine State here .   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Monday 9 November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69524). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for late November to early December 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Casual-Academics---School-of-Languages-and-Cultures_R-69874": {
      "title": "Casual Academics - School of Languages and Cultures",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-09\nCasual\n\nSchool of Languages and Cultures Casual Academic positions About This Opportunity The School of Languages and Cultures (SLC) is seeking Casual academics across the discipline below: Undergraduate Language, Technology and Global Communication - specifically recruiting for the course WRIT1001 – Writing for International Students   Casual academics provide a rich and important contribution to the University’s academic, teaching, research, artistic, outreach and clinical endeavours through the provision of a range of academically focused services and activities. In SLC, Casual academics may be engaged to fulfil a wide range of duties, including (but not limited to) tutoring; marking; demonstrating; teaching; student outreach and engagement; moderating/facilitating and/or student advising across multiple academic disciplines. Hours will vary according to the class timetable. SLC classes typically run between 8:00 am and 4:50pm with occasional evening classes beginning at 5:00pm or later for the entire semester. A commitment for the whole of the semester is required. Please click here for details of the programs and courses offered in the School of Languages and Cultures. Please note that these opportunities are subject to The University’s Code of Conduct . About You You must possess a minimum of an undergraduate degree, preferably in an area relevant to the discipline in which you seek to deliver content. You must have demonstrated language ability commensurate with the requirements of a given course and have relatively recent experience with the course material or a demonstrated capacity to become familiar with the material in a short time. You might be studying at Honours or postgraduate level at UQ or another university or be working as a research assistant or postdoctoral fellow. Perhaps you have taught in your field at a significant level in the past and are looking to return to the workforce. The selection process involves assessment of your application where consideration will be given to the following factors, in no particular order: Current postgraduate students whose supervisors approve the time spent teaching; Outstanding student feedback from prior courses; Demonstrated flexibility and ability to adapt to different teaching modes; Formal training and practical experience in teaching; Demonstrated expertise or relevant education in the course subject; For new postgraduate students: lack of other tutoring or academic work experience. What We Can Offer These are casual appointments. As such, there is no firm advance commitment to ongoing or indefinite work. Casual academics are remunerated according to the category of their duties, as detailed in UQ’s Enterprise Agreement and reiterated in this job profile. Please click here to view our various casual academic pay scales. The current pay rate is $57.60 per hour ($68.13 for PhD-qualified). Some training is provided. For further information about UQ’s benefits, please visit Why Work at UQ and review The University of Queensland Enterprise Agreement 2021-2026 (Enterprise Agreement) Questions? For application queries, please contact casualrecruit@uq.edu.au  stating the job reference number in the subject line. Other Information You must have work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. At UQ we know that our greatest strengths come from our diverse mix of colleagues, this is reflected in our ongoing commitment to creating an environment focused on equity, diversity and inclusion .  We ensure that we are always attracting, retaining and promoting colleagues who are representative of the diversity in our broader community, whether that be  gender identity ,  LGBTQIA+ ,  cultural and/or linguistic ,  Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander peoples , or  people with a disability . Accessibility requirements and/or adjustments can be directed to recruitment@uq.edu.au . How to Apply Applications should be made using the apply button below. If you are a current employee of the University, or hold an unpaid or affiliate appointment with the University, please login to your staff Workday account and visit the internal careers board to apply for this opportunity. Please do NOT apply via the external job board. You must include an updated CV, supporting statements or other evidence such as previous recent teaching evaluations as required.  Please also indicate which course(s) you may be interested in teaching. All applications will be considered. Those accepted will be included in the School of Languages and Cultures Talent Pool for the year 2027 unless you indicate that you do not wish for your application to be held on file. Please note if you are interested in being included in the SLC Talent Pool for future years you will need to reapply each year. Please note, an application does not guarantee a position. If your application is successful, you will then be contacted by relevant Program and Major Convenors with details of specific course activities that are available within the given Program or Major/s in which you have expressed interest. You will be requested to identify for which activities you wish to be considered and to indicate your availability to undertake them in the relevant Semester. You will be advised of the relevant due date for this process. Applications Close on 11 December 2026 at 11:59PM AEST"
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Gatton-Campus/Trainer-and-Assessor---BSB50420-Diploma-of-Leadership-and-Management---Casual_R-68639": {
      "title": "Trainer and Assessor – BSB50420 Diploma of Leadership and Management - Casual",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nCasual\n\nUQ Skills . Casual position. This role supports the delivery of training and assessment in a remote environment.   About This Opportunity. Join UQ Skills as a casual Trainer and Assessor, specialising in the delivery and assessment of the BSB50420 Diploma of Leadership and Management. Working within a collaborative vocational education environment, you will help learners develop practical leadership capabilities through purposeful, engaging and industry-relevant training delivered on campus, online, remotely and at off-campus locations.   Key responsibilities will include, but not limited to: Deliver engaging training using approved training plans, lesson plans, learning resources, equipment and practical demonstrations. Assess and mark learner work, provide constructive feedback, manage reassessment requirements, and monitor attendance, progress and results in consultation with the Training Manager. Contribute expert advice to the development, validation and moderation of training and assessment processes, tools and resources to support quality and continuous improvement. Complete required administration through aXcelerate, maintain the confidentiality of learner and assessment materials, and comply with UQ Skills policies, procedures and Australian Skills Quality Authority requirements.   About You. Industry qualifications within your area of expertise at least one level higher than the qualification being delivered and assessed, or an equivalent combination of extensive relevant industry experience and qualifications. A Certificate IV in Training and Assessment TAE40116, or TAE40110 with TAELLN411 and TAEASS502, or a higher qualification in adult and vocational education. Recent and relevant industry and vocational education and training experience. Knowledge of Australian Skills Quality Authority standards relating to learning, assessment and the mapping of training materials, with the ability to apply these requirements in practice. Demonstrated knowledge of the Principles of Assessment and Rules of Evidence, or the ability to rapidly acquire and apply this knowledge. Experience using learning management systems and student management systems, together with strong capability across Microsoft Office applications. High-level written, verbal, organisational and interpersonal skills, including the ability to build cooperative working relationships, manage competing priorities and maintain close attention to detail. The ability to engage, influence and motivate learners and present contemporary teaching material in a meaningful, accessible and stimulating way.   About UQ. As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world.   Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available, such as access to exclusive internal-only vacancies,   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Eimear Vallely at eimear.vallely@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns with the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information. As a casual employee, and in line with Fair Work , please note that there is no commitment to ongoing work in this position.   Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We’re dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Sunday 8 November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68639).   Please note applications will be reviewed as received. Candidates may be interviewed prior to the job closing date. We encourage candidates to apply as soon as possible. The University of Queensland reserves the right to close this application process early."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Dutton-Park/Animal-Technical-Assistant_R-69637-1": {
      "title": "Animal Technical Assistant",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nFull time\n\nBiological Resources Full-time, fixed-term position up to 25/06/2027 Base salary will be in the range $79,096 - $83,863 + 17% super (HEW Level 4). Based at our Dutton Park location. About This Opportunity Join a collaborative and purpose-driven team supporting world-class biomedical and scientific research through the delivery of exceptional animal care and technical services. As an Animal Technical Assistant, you will contribute to a regulated research environment where animal welfare, operational excellence, compliance and teamwork are at the heart of everything we do. Working alongside researchers, supervisors and fellow technical professionals, you will help create the conditions that enable impactful scientific discoveries while upholding the highest standards of care and biosecurity. Key responsibilities will include : Deliver high-quality animal care, husbandry and breeding services for laboratory rodents and other species, ensuring optimal animal welfare, colony health and accurate record-keeping. Perform routine and technical procedures, including injections, sample collection, breeding support and euthanasia, in accordance with approved protocols and regulatory requirements. Maintain facility operations, cleanliness and biosecurity by operating equipment, monitoring life-support systems, managing inventory and responding promptly to alarms or incidents. Provide effective operational and compliance support to researchers and stakeholders through clear communication, sound technical advice and collaborative teamwork, including participation in rostered after-hours work. About You Relevant qualifications in animal technology, biological sciences or a related discipline, and/or an equivalent combination of training and practical experience. Demonstrated capability to work confidently within a regulated laboratory animal environment while maintaining high standards of animal welfare. Sound knowledge of animal breeding programs, colony management and record-keeping processes, or the ability to rapidly develop this expertise. Experience handling laboratory animals and performing routine technical procedures with accuracy, care and professionalism. Strong understanding of biosecurity, workplace health and safety, and compliance requirements relevant to animal research environments. Excellent communication and interpersonal skills, with the ability to build collaborative working relationships with researchers, technical staff and stakeholders. Strong organisational skills with the ability to prioritise competing tasks, maintain attention to detail and work both independently and as part of a team. A commitment to service excellence, continuous improvement and supporting research outcomes that create meaningful impact. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available. Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on-campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, Rebecca Bruce, r.bruce1@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, mandatory immunisations. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. Working with laboratory animals is an inherent requirement of the position. The appointment is therefore subject to, and conditional upon, satisfactory medical clearance(s), including disclosure of relevant medical history, to undertake such work without unreasonable risk to your health and safety. In addition, the Employee is required to fully comply with necessary ongoing health monitoring procedures and control measures associated with the position. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion. We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Thursday 22 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69637)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Herston-Campus/Administration-Officer_R-68336-2": {
      "title": "Administration Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nFull time\n\nAbout This Opportunity    The Administration Officer provides high-quality administrative, stakeholder engagement and event support to the Medical School and its Mayne Academies. Working within a collaborative, agile and inclusive team, you will help deliver operational excellence across School activities, governance processes, strategic initiatives and community engagement programs. In line with UQ’s commitment to creating change together, this role offers the opportunity to contribute to a connected, supportive and purpose-driven environment that values collaboration, responsiveness and continuous improvement Key responsibilities will include :  Providing high-level administrative support across School initiatives, projects, operational activities and events. Coordinating committee and governance support, including meeting administration, documentation, minutes and action tracking. Supporting the Deputy Head of School (Clinical Engagement) and Mayne Academy Heads through travel, event and financial administration. Maintaining effective stakeholder communications, website and intranet content, and assisting with engagement and recognition initiatives. This is a full-time, fixed-term position until December 2028 at HEW Level 5.  About You As the Administration Officer, you will be a highly motivated and collaborative professional who thrives in a dynamic environment and is committed to delivering exceptional administrative and stakeholder support. You will bring: An undergraduate degree in a relevant discipline, or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and education/training. Demonstrated experience providing high-quality administrative and organisational support in a complex, service-focused environment. Excellent interpersonal, verbal and written communication skills, with the ability to build effective working relationships with a diverse range of stakeholders. Strong organisational, time management and problem-solving skills, with the ability to prioritise competing demands and meet deadlines with accuracy and attention to detail. Proficiency in the Microsoft Office suite, including Word, Excel, PowerPoint and Outlook, together with the ability to quickly learn new systems, databases and processes. Experience interpreting policies and procedures, exercising sound judgement, and working both independently and collaboratively to deliver positive outcomes.  The successful candidate may be required to complete a number of pre-employment checks, including right to work in Australia and a criminal check. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.    Please note that this role will require occasional out-of-hours work to support events and related activities. Flexibility to work outside standard business hours on occasion is essential. Questions?    For more information about this opportunity, please contact Kendall Hepple at k.hepple@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job requisition ID in the subject line.     Want to Apply?   We welcome applications from all individuals and are committed to an inclusive and accessible recruitment process. To be considered, please ensure you upload:       Resume  A cover letter summarising how your background aligns with the 'About You' section      Our strength as an institution lies in our diverse colleagues. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion , fostering an environment that mirrors our wider community. We're committed to attracting, retaining, and promoting diverse talent. If you require an alternative method to submit your application due to accessibility needs or personal circumstances, please contact talent@uq.edu.au .      Other Information    UQ is committed to a fair, equitable and inclusive selection process, which recognises that some applicants may face additional barriers and challenges which have impacted and/or continue to impact their career trajectory. Candidates who don’t meet all criteria are encouraged to apply and demonstrate their potential. The selection panel considers both potential and performance relative to opportunities when assessing suitability for the role.    Applications close Saturday 17 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68336). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for the week of 19th October 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Remote-University-Sites---Off-Campus/Casual-Trainer---Assessor--Procurement-and-Contracting-_R-68327-1": {
      "title": "Casual Trainer & Assessor (Procurement and Contracting)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nCasual\n\nUQ Skills Casual position. This role supports the delivery of training in a remote environment.   About This Opportunity  UQ Skills is seeking a collaborative, purposeful and industry-connected Trainer and Assessor to support the delivery and assessment of nationally recognised procurement and contracting qualifications. Working within a supportive and inclusive Registered Training Organisation, you will play a key role in developing capability across government and industry learners while contributing to UQ’s vision of creating meaningful change through high-quality vocational education. Drawing on your expertise, you will help learners build practical skills and confidence while supporting continuous improvement across training and assessment practices.   Key responsibilities will include, but not limited to:  Deliver and/or assess PSP40616 Certificate IV in Procurement and Contracting, PSP50616 Diploma of Procurement and Contracting and PSP60616 Advanced Diploma of Procurement and Contracting. Prepare and maintain training plans, lesson plans, learning resources, assessment tools and training materials. Monitor student attendance, progress and assessment outcomes, providing constructive feedback and managing reassessment activities where required. Participate in validation, moderation and continuous improvement activities to ensure compliance and quality outcomes. Complete student administration and record management activities, including use of aXcelerate and associated systems.   About You Industry qualifications in procurement and contracting at least one level higher than the qualification you train and assess in, or equivalent extensive industry experience and qualifications. Certificate IV in Training and Assessment TAE40116, or TAE40110 with the required upgrades including TAELLN411 and TAEASS502, or higher qualifications in adult and vocational education. Recent and relevant industry and vocational education and training experience. Knowledge of ASQA standards and demonstrated ability to apply quality training, assessment and mapping practices. Understanding of the Principles of Assessment and Rules of Evidence. Experience using Learning Management Systems and Student Management Systems. Strong written and verbal communication skills, organisational capability and attention to detail. Ability to motivate learners and present engaging, practical and meaningful training content.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world.   Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available, such as access to exclusive internal-only vacancies and affordable parking from just $6.40 a day.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Eimear Vallely at Eimear.vallely@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.    When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information   As a casual employee, and in line with Fair Work , please note that there is no commitment to ongoing work in this position.    Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Sunday 8 November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68327).   Please note applications will be reviewed as received. Candidates may be interviewed prior to the job closing date. We encourage candidates to apply as soon as possible. The University of Queensland reserves the right to close this application process early."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Dutton-Park/Lecturers-in-Pharmacy-Practice_R-69865-2": {
      "title": "Lecturers in Pharmacy Practice",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nFull time\n\nSchool of Pharmacy and Pharmaceutical Sciences Two full-time, fixed-term positions for up to three years commencing January 2027 Base salary will range from $119,462 - $141,545 (Academic Level B) + 17% Superannuation Based at our Dutton Park campus About This Opportunity Our School of Pharmacy and Pharmaceutical Sciences is seeking two experienced and inspiring Lecturers in Pharmacy Practice to support the development of UQ’s new Doctor of Pharmacy program. Working within a collaborative and inclusive academic team, you will shape contemporary pharmacy education, mentor the next generation of pharmacy professionals and pursue impactful research that advances clinical practice and community outcomes. Key responsibilities will include: Teaching: Develop and deliver engaging courses in pharmacy practice and cognate disciplines, including clinical therapeutics, professional practice, the social and ethical dimensions of practice and clinical epidemiology. Coordinate courses, assess students and provide high-quality academic advice and support. Research: Build an independent or collaborative research program with national recognition and impact. Contribute to competitive funding applications, publish in reputable journals and use best-practice research methodologies. Supervision and Researcher Development: Contribute to the supervision and development of Honours and Higher Degree by Research students, supporting their capabilities, professional growth and responsible conduct of research. Citizenship and Service: Build purposeful partnerships with colleagues, industry, government, professional bodies and the wider community. Contribute to School service, mentor others and consistently demonstrate UQ’s values. This is a teaching & research position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance . About You A PhD in pharmacy, pharmacy practice or a related field. Experience and evidence of high-quality teaching at undergraduate or postgraduate level. Professional experience and expertise enabling you to teach pharmacy practice and cognate disciplines, including but not limited to, clinical therapeutics, professional practice, social and ethical dimensions of practice and clinical epidemiology. Demonstrated experience in course coordination, contemporary curriculum design and the development or continuous improvement of pharmacy programs and course materials. A developing reputation and track record of publications in reputable refereed journals and presentations at conferences. Evidence of successfully seeking, obtaining and managing external research funding. A growing record of supervising Research Higher Degree students. Experience leading internal service roles at a School level and contributing to external activities within the wider profession. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.   At UQ, you will join a visionary and collaborative community committed to creating change together. You will be supported to pursue innovative teaching, meaningful research and professional growth while helping prepare future pharmacy professionals to make a positive impact.   The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Peter Cabot, Head of School, at pcabot@pharmacy.uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number R-69865.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter including responses to the ‘About You’ Selection Criteria above . Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks will include verification of your right to work in Australia, your qualifications and full AHPRA registration. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Friday 30 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (Job Reference Number R-69865)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/The-Park-Centre-for-Mental-Health---Wacol/Senior-Research-Fellow---Evaluation-Research-Stream_R-69530-1": {
      "title": "Senior Research Fellow - Evaluation Research Stream",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nFull time\n\nQueensland Centre for Mental Health Research within the School of Public Health . Full-time (100%), fixed-term position for 12 months. Base salary will be in the range $145,962 to $168,044 + 17% super (Academic Level C). Based at our Wacol location About This Opportunity This is an exciting opportunity for a Senior Research Fellow to join the Evaluation Research Stream at the Queensland Centre for Mental Health Research. You will bring specialist quantitative research expertise to a collaborative and purposeful program of applied research and evaluation focused on mental health service improvement and system reform. A key initial focus will be the Health Translation Queensland-funded Mental Health and Emergency Department project, where your work will help illuminate how different mental health service ecosystems influence emergency department patient flow and access, identify gaps and populations not adequately served by current arrangements, and shape evidence-informed options for new or enhanced models of care. This role is ideally suited to someone with strong project management, initiative and stakeholder engagement skills, and the ability to work independently while collaborating effectively with researchers, clinicians, data custodians, health services and other partners. Key responsibilities will include: Research: Lead the quantitative components of the project, including developing research questions, analytic frameworks, outcome measures and analysis plans, and applying contemporary quantitative and health services research methods to routinely collected Queensland Health data. Research translation and collaboration: Integrate quantitative findings with other project evidence, work closely with researchers, health services, clinicians, data custodians and partners, and translate rigorous analysis into meaningful findings for mental health policy and service improvement. Supervision and Researcher Development: Provide quantitative methodological leadership, advice and mentorship to research staff, students and collaborators, while promoting responsible research conduct, reproducibility, research integrity and sound data governance. Citizenship and Service: Build purposeful partnerships, demonstrate leadership through mentoring and collaboration, contribute to internal service and decision-making, support colleagues, and consistently uphold UQ’s values. This is a research focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance. About You Completion of a PhD in epidemiology, biostatistics, health services research, public health, mental health, health sciences or another relevant discipline, or an equivalent combination of relevant qualifications and substantial research experience. An established profile in quantitative health services, epidemiological, evaluation or related applied health research. Demonstrated expertise in analysing and interpreting large and complex routinely collected health or administrative datasets, including the development of research questions, analysis plans, variables, indicators and outcome measures. Strong knowledge of quantitative research design and statistical methods, with the ability to select, apply and interpret approaches appropriate to complex health services research questions. Demonstrated ability to critically assess data quality, completeness, bias, confounding and other limitations, and appropriately account for these in analysis, interpretation and communication of findings. Experience with data governance, ethics, privacy and data access requirements associated with routinely collected or administrative health data. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development. The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Associate Professor Zoe Rutherford . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section . Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information. Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. Relocating from interstate or overseas? We may support you with obtaining employer-sponsored work rights and a relocation support package, however upfront costs will still be expected to be covered. You can find out more about life in Australia’s Sunshine State here . We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Sunday 25 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69530). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for Thursday 5th November 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow---Research-Fellow-in-Agricultural-Systems-Assessment_R-68711-1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow / Research Fellow in Agricultural Systems Assessment",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nFull time\n\nQueensland Alliance for Agriculture and Food Innovation. Full-time (100%), fixed-term position for up to 2 years. Base salary will be in the range $85,373 to $113,660 plus 17% super at Academic Level A, or $119,463 to $141,545 plus 17% super at Academic Level B. Based at our St Lucia location .   About This Opportunity. Join a purposeful and collaborative research environment within QAAFI’s Centre for Crop Science and contribute to SorGain, a research, development and extension program co-funded by GRDC and UQ to improve Australian sorghum production. You will lead the day-to-day delivery and integration of the “Knowledge Integration, Grower Insight and Decision Frameworks” work package, drawing together crop agronomy, adaptive research, stakeholder decision research and advanced analytics to create practical, regionally relevant outcomes for growers and industry.   Key responsibilities will include, but not limited to: Research: Conduct regional evidence syntheses and meta-analyses of sorghum agronomy, integrate agronomic, survey and decision data using qualitative, mixed-method, statistical and machine-learning approaches, and translate findings into transparent and practical decision-support resources. Research: Lead stakeholder decision dynamics research with growers, advisers and industry representatives, including research design, sampling, ethics, quality assurance, interviews, focus groups and participatory workshops. Supervision and Researcher Development: Contribute to the supervision and development of Honours and Higher Degree by Research students, research assistants and project staff through methodological advice, mentoring, constructive feedback and shared planning. Citizenship and Service: Coordinate project activities, milestones, risks and reporting, contribute to publications and funding proposals, represent SorGain and QAAFI at scientific and industry forums, and actively support the priorities of the Centre for Crop Science.   This is a research focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance .   For further information, please click here to view the information booklet .   About You. A PhD, or a doctoral thesis submitted, in agronomy, crop science, farming systems, agricultural systems science, agricultural extension or innovation, decision science, or another closely related discipline with a substantial applied crop-agronomy component. Demonstrated expertise in crop agronomy and broadacre farming systems, including the ability to interpret interactions among crop genotype, environment, management, operational constraints and seasonal risk. Experience in adaptive, participatory or on-farm research, with the ability to translate agronomic evidence and stakeholder knowledge into well-defined research questions, practical study designs and iterative learning activities. Experience designing and analysing qualitative or mixed-method research involving growers, advisers or other agricultural stakeholders, including interviews, focus groups, workshops or surveys. Strong quantitative and computational skills, including demonstrated ability to analyse complex agronomic, environmental, survey or behavioural data using R, Python or equivalent software. Experience in evidence synthesis, meta-analysis or the integration of heterogeneous research and field datasets, with careful attention to study design, data quality, uncertainty and reproducibility. Demonstrated ability to engage respectfully and productively with growers, advisers, industry, government and research partners, together with the capacity to undertake regular travel to sorghum-growing regions of Queensland and New South Wales. An emerging or established record of high-quality peer-reviewed publications, with the ability to prepare clear technical reports, presentations and stakeholder-focused materials.   For appointment at Academic Level B, you will also demonstrate: At least 3 years of independent and collaborative research beyond the doctoral project, supported by peer-reviewed publications or other substantial scholarly outputs. Experience leading significant research components, coordinating multidisciplinary contributors and external stakeholders, and taking responsibility for the quality and timely delivery of outputs. Evidence of contributions to postgraduate supervision or research training, funding proposals, partnership development or other forms of external research support.   Knowledge of sorghum or other summer cereal production in dryland broadacre systems would be advantageous, as would experience in field trials, on-farm experimentation, farmer-participatory research, survey methodologies, research ethics, monitoring and evaluation, relational databases, SQL or AI-supported decision-support systems.   About UQ. As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.   At QAAFI, we work in partnership to translate visionary crop research into outcomes that respond to grower needs and strengthen Australian agriculture. One such partnership is the Sorghum Strategic Alliance, a seven-year collaboration between UQ and the Grains Research and Development Corporation (GRDC) that brings together expertise in genetics, breeding and agronomy to improve sorghum productivity, resilience and grain quality. Read more about the Sorghum Strategic Alliance   The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad, from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Sudhir Yadav sudhir.yadav@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section . Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information. Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We’re dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Sunday 25 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68711)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow-Research-Officer---Grain-Refinement-of-Ferrous-Alloys_R-69773": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow/Research Officer - Grain Refinement of Ferrous Alloys",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nPart time\n\nSchool of Mechanical and Mining Engineering – Faculty of EAIT Part-time (50%), fixed-term position for up to 12 months Base salary will be in the range $85,372.89 - $113,659.72 + 17% super (Academic Level A) Visa sponsorship is not available for this position Based at our St Lucia Campus - Brisbane   About This Opportunity  This is an exciting opportunity for a part-time (0.5 FTE) Postdoctoral Research Fellow / Research Officer to join a collaborative ARC Linkage project developing innovative grain refiners for ferrous alloys.  You will be working alongside leading researchers and industry partners, conduct experimental metallurgical research, perform microstructural analysis, and help deliver solutions to real-world manufacturing challenges. Advance your research career by working on industry-driven innovation, publishing high-quality research, and developing valuable academic and industry connections.   Key responsibilities will include:    Research: Contribute to an ARC Linkage project developing new grain refiners for ferrous alloys through experimental research, laboratory testing and analysis. Working with university and industry partners, you will deliver project outcomes, report findings, and communicate results through publications and presentations.   Teaching: Participate in undergraduate and postgraduate teaching, maintaining high teaching quality and responding to student feedback.   Supervision and Researcher Development: Contribute to the supervision and development of Honours and HDR students, providing mentoring, feedback and professional support, while effectively managing research staff and resolving workplace issues in line with University policies and procedures.   Citizenship and Service: Demonstrate UQ values through collaboration, service and active participation in unit activities, while supporting colleagues, contributing to committees and administrative processes, and building relationships with industry, government and community partners.   This is a research focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance .   About You  Completion or near completion of a PhD in materials or mechanical engineering or related areas. An emerging profile in research in materials engineering. Evidence of publications in reputed refereed journals and presenting at conferences. Evidence of contributions towards successfully obtaining external research funding. Some experience in meaningful internal service roles and contributions towards external activities.   We particularly welcome applications from women, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people with disability, members of the LGBTQIA+ community, and others who may be underrepresented in this discipline.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.   The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Dr Yahia Ali via y.ali@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.    When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia and qualifications, professional registration checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We are dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We are also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Wednesday, 21 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69773)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Clinical-Educators---Speech-Pathology---Telerehabilitation_R-68520-1": {
      "title": "Clinical Educators - Speech Pathology & Telerehabilitation",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nPart time\n\nSchool of Health and Rehabilitation Sciences | UQ Health and Rehabilitation Clinics Two part-year, part-time (40% FTE), continuing positions at HEW Level 7 Base salary will range from $106,709 - $116,198 (full-time equivalent) + 17% Superannuation Primarily based at our vibrant St Lucia campus with some work at external sites as required   About This Opportunity    The School of Health and Rehabilitation Sciences welcomes applications for Clinical Educators in Speech Pathology to support the practice education and clinical development of our speech pathology students on a part-year, continuing basis. We are specifically looking for Speech Pathologists who can deliver: Position 1: Fluency services across the lifespan and general paediatric speech pathology services in the UQ Clinics delivered via telehealth Position 2: Paediatric speech pathology services to paediatric and school-aged children either in local schools or in the UQ Clinics Key responsibilities will include:    Practice Education & Student Supervision: Facilitate and supervise speech pathology and interprofessional clinical placements; monitor, assess and support student learning, skill development and clinical performance; and deliver clinical supervision, professional development activities and work integrated learning support.   Clinical Practice & Service Delivery: Deliver evidence-based speech pathology services in-person and/or via telehealth, support continuity of care, and provide clinical leadership, guidance and oversight of operational and clinical procedures.   Research & Industry Engagement: Contribute to research in clinical practice or practice education, and foster relationships with industry partners, professional bodies, government and the wider community.   Administration & Governance: Contribute to clinic and school operations, manage student placement and assessment administration, and maintain compliance with professional, legislative and organisational standards. Please note, Speech Pathology student-led clinics operate on specific days of the week due to student timetabling. Successful applicants must be available to work on a Thursday commencing January 2027. As these are part-year appointments, it is expected that planned leave will be taken outside of the primary clinical teaching periods that align with the academic calendar or clinical placement periods. Successful applicants will be required to take a combination of paid and unpaid leave over the November to January period, and during the mid-year break. Please take this into consideration before submitting your application. About You Qualifications and training equivalent to an undergraduate/graduate-entry degree in Speech Pathology with at least four (4) years subsequent relevant experience. Demonstrated recency and currency of practice with advanced knowledge and ability to deliver evidence based clinical practice across a range of clinical areas, including telerehabilitation. Demonstrated abilities as an effective educator and communicator in the area of speech pathology practice education such as student supervision, educational evaluation, training and assessment marking. Demonstrated knowledge of clinical education issues and blended learning approaches, with the ability to develop and implement innovative clinical teaching methods that enhance students' clinical skills and knowledge development. Ability to establish effective relationships and to represent and promote the clinical discipline at an interprofessional, university and wider community level, including industry, government and professional bodies. Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively and autonomously, effectively managing competing priorities and multiple tasks while utilising technology systems and software applications to achieve outcomes.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Andrea Abel, Clinic Manager (Speech Pathology & Telerehabilitation), at andrea.whitehead@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (R-68520).    When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter. Your cover letter should indicate the position/s you are applying for (Position 1 and/or 2) along with responses to the ‘About You’ selection criteria above. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information   Pre‑employment checks will include verification of your right to work in Australia, qualifications, Speech Pathology Australia membership and current CPSP status, a Working with Children Blue Card and Drivers Licence, eligibility for a Medicare Provider Number and completion of mandatory immunisations. This may also include checks relating to gender‑based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We’re dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling 07 3365 2623. Applications close Wednesday 21 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (Job Reference Number: R-68520)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Casual-Academics---School-of-Political-Science-and-International-Studies_R-69983": {
      "title": "Casual Academics - School of Political Science and International Studies",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nCasual\n\nSchool of Political Science & International Studies Multiple Casual Academic positions Closing date 8 January 2027 at 11:59pm AEST About This Opportunity    This is a call for applicants for Casual academics in the School of Political Science and International Studies in support of our course offerings in Semester 1 2027.   Casual academics will typically be engaged in a range of duties, including (but not limited to) tutoring; marking; teaching and moderating/facilitating. Casual academics are remunerated according to the category of their duties, as detailed in UQ’s Enterprise Agreement.   Please click here for a list of POLS courses, plus one PPES course which are likely to need tutors. Please indicate a maximum of six courses you are interested in teaching into. Although you will be asked to state why you have picked a course your response is optional.   NB: Tutoring opportunities are reliant on course enrolment numbers and some courses at postgraduate level may not attract sufficient enrolments to require a tutor.   Please note that these opportunities are subject to The University’s Code of Conduct .    What We Can Offer    These are casual appointments. As such, there is no firm advance commitment to ongoing or indefinite work. The casual rate will vary as per the work completed. Please click here to view our various casual academic pay scales. For further information about UQ’s benefits, please visit Why Work at UQ and review  The University of Queensland Enterprise Agreement 2021-2026 . Questions?    For application queries, please contact casualrecruit@uq.edu.au stating the job reference number in the subject line.   Other Information    You must have work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.    We value diversity and inclusion, and actively encourage applications from those who bring diversity to the University. Our Diversity and Inclusion webpage contains further information if you require additional support. Accessibility requirements and/or adjustments can be directed to recruitment@uq.edu.au .   Applicants please note:   New applicants use the Apply button  Existing casual staff login via your staff Workday account and apply via the internal careers board.     If you are a current employee of the University (including casual staff), or hold a HDR Scholarship with the University, please login to your staff Workday account and visit the internal careers board to apply for this opportunity. Please do NOT apply via the external job board."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow-in-Stem-Cell-Population-Genetics_R-69544": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow in Stem Cell Population Genetics",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nFull time\n\nInstitute for Molecular Bioscience Full-time fixed-term position for up to 2 years Base salary will be in the range $85,372.89 - $113,659.72 + 17% super (Academic Level A) Based at our St Lucia Campus   About This Opportunity  Join a visionary, collaborative and rapidly growing research team led by Dr Drew Neavin at the intersection of population genetics, stem cell biology and single-cell genomics. The Context-dependent Genetics Lab is pioneering innovative “village-in-a-dish” stem cell culture systems that enable researchers to study how genetic regulation changes across cellular contexts, disease states and human genetic diversity. Supported by world-class infrastructure, a connected network of like-minded researchers, and a culture of collaboration and excellence, this role offers an exceptional opportunity to contribute to impactful science while shaping your own research career.   Key responsibilities will include: Research: Design, lead and troubleshoot large-scale stem cell experiments, including population-scale iPSC culture, differentiation and single-cell profiling; contribute to project direction across disease systems; publish high-quality research outputs; analyse single-cell datasets in collaboration with computational researchers; develop robust and reproducible protocols; and contribute to research translation and commercialisation activities. Supervision and Researcher Development: Mentor junior researchers and students in stem cell culture and experimental design; contribute to the supervision and development of Honours and Higher Degree by Research students as appropriate. Citizenship and Service: Foster a safe, inclusive and collegial laboratory environment; contribute to shared infrastructure and laboratory practices; demonstrate behaviours aligned with UQ values; and develop partnerships with industry, government and the broader research community.   This is a research focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance .   About You  Completion, or near completion, of a PhD in Stem Cell Biology, Genetics, Genomics, Cell Biology, Computational Biology or a closely related discipline. Demonstrated hands-on experience with human pluripotent stem cell culture and differentiation. Experience planning, executing and troubleshooting large-scale or technically complex biological experiments. Evidence of high-quality publications and conference presentations consistent with career stage. Demonstrated ability to work effectively within a multidisciplinary and highly collaborative research team. Experience mentoring, supervising or supporting the development of students or junior researchers. Excellent communication, organisation, collaboration and problem-solving skills. An emerging research profile in stem cells, genomics, genetics, or closely related fields. Highly regarded Experience with single-cell technologies, including single-cell RNA sequencing or multiome approaches. Bioinformatic or computational experience in for example R or Python, or analysis of single-cell or genomic datasets or a willingness to develop these skills with support from the team. Experience with organoid or other complex differentiation systems.  About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.   The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Dr Drew Neavin at d.neavin@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.    When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia and qualification checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   Relocating from interstate or overseas? We may support you with obtaining employer-sponsored work rights and a relocation support package, however upfront costs will still be expected to be covered. You can find out more about life in Australia’s Sunshine State here .   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Wednesday 4 November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69544). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for mid to late November."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow-Research-Officer---Hybrid-Glass-X-ray-Scintillators_R-69233-1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow/Research Officer - Hybrid Glass X-ray Scintillators",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nFull time\n\nSchool of Chemical Engineering – Faculty of EAIT Full-time (100%), fixed-term position for up to 2 years Base salary will be in the range $85,372.89 - $113,659.72 + 17% super (Academic Level A) Visa sponsorship may be offered for this position Based at our St Lucia Campus - Brisbane   About This Opportunity  The School of Chemical Engineering is seeking a Postdoctoral Research Fellow / Research Officer to join Associate Professor Jingwei Hou's group on the NHMRC Investigator Grant \"Wearable and High-Sensitivity X-ray Detectors for Managing Respiratory Diseases in Infants and Children\".   The project develops hybrid glass scintillators that can be processed into flexible, wearable detectors for low-dose X-ray imaging of children. You will lead the materials work: designing, synthesising and characterising hybrid glass scintillators, and relating glass structure to scintillation performance.   Key responsibilities will include:    Research: Develop and characterise advanced hybrid glass scintillator materials, leading synchrotron experiments, collaborating on detector prototype development, and contributing to high-impact publications, conference presentations, and research funding applications.   Supervision and Researcher Development: Supervise HDR and undergraduate students, supporting researcher development and fostering high performance through mentoring, feedback and professional development. You will also contribute to the effective management of research support staff and help resolve workplace issues in line with University policies and procedures.   Citizenship and Service: Contribute to a positive and collaborative research environment by supporting unit operations, maintaining compliance with health and safety requirements and engaging with internal and external stakeholders to advance the University's goals.   Teaching: Contribute to undergraduate and postgraduate teaching, delivering high-quality learning experiences and continuously improving teaching practice in response to student feedback and outcomes.   This is a research focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance .   About You  A PhD (awarded or near completion) in materials science, chemistry, chemical engineering or a related field. In-depth understanding of hybrid glass materials, such as MOF glasses, coordination polymer glasses or hybrid metal halide glasses, including melt-quenching and structure-property analysis. Hands-on experience with synchrotron techniques, from beamtime proposals through data collection to analysis. A publication record in relevant journals appropriate to career stage. Ability to work independently and in a multidisciplinary team. Experience with MOF glass synthesis and characterisation, thin-film or flexible device fabrication, and structural modelling of amorphous materials, such as reverse Monte Carlo or molecular dynamics would be considered favourably.   We particularly welcome applications from women, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people with disability, members of the LGBTQIA+ community, and others who may be underrepresented in this discipline.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.   The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Associate Professor Jingwei Hou via jingwei.hou@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.    When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia and qualifications checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   Relocating from interstate or overseas? We may support you with obtaining employer-sponsored work rights and a relocation support package, however upfront costs will still be expected to be covered. You can find out more about life in Australia’s Sunshine State here .   We are dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We are also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Thursday, 5 November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69233)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Dunwich-Marine-Station---Stradbroke-Island/Senior-Project-Officer--Moreton-Bay-Research-Station--Identified-s25_R-63610-2": {
      "title": "Senior Project Officer (Moreton Bay Research Station) Identified s25",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nFull time\n\nCentre for Marine Science Full-time, fixed term position for up to 2 years   Base salary will be in the range $97,268.85 - $104,348.98 + 17% super (HEW Level 6) This position is open to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander candidates only, as a genuine occupational requirement, as the role works directly with Quandamooka organisations, Elders and community members to support Indigenous‑led and co‑developed research on Country Based at our Moreton Bay Research Station, Minjerribah Acknowledgement of Country  The University of Queensland (UQ) acknowledges the Traditional Owners and their custodianship of the lands on which UQ operates. We pay our respects to their Ancestors and their descendants, who continue cultural and spiritual connections to Country. We recognise their valuable contributions to Australian and global society.     About This Opportunity    This identified role offers a meaningful opportunity to support Indigenous‑led and co‑developed environmental and cultural research on Minjerribah. Working predominantly in community and based at the Moreton Bay Research Station, the Senior Project Officer acts as a vital interface between Quandamooka organisations, Elders and community members, and UQ researchers. The role exists to strengthen respectful, community‑driven research partnerships and ensure research activity aligns with cultural protocols, community governance and shared priorities. Key responsibilities will include:   Coordinating and supporting multiple community‑led research projects, including planning, scheduling, milestone tracking and reporting. Supporting culturally safe engagement with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander organisations, Elders and community members, including meetings, workshops, yarning sessions and on Country activities. Collating, organising and supporting analysis of project data, and contributing to accessible reports, maps and knowledge products that meet community needs. Supporting clear communication and knowledge sharing between community partners and university researchers, including the return of research findings in agreed and culturally appropriate formats. For further information, please click here to view the information booklet. About You    The University of Queensland considers that to perform this role, it is essential that the person is an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander person. It is therefore a genuine occupational requirement under section 25 of the  Anti-Discrimination Act 1991  (Qld). The successful candidate will be required to provide evidence to confirm that they are an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person.  Demonstrated capability through relevant study or equivalent community-based cultural or professional experience to work effectively on environmental, research or Country-focused projects. Strong understanding of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander cultures, histories and community dynamics, with the ability to work in accordance with cultural protocols, community guidance, and on-Country expectations. Proven experience supporting project coordination, administration and reporting, with attention to accuracy, care and accountability. Ability to respectfully gather, interpret and share scientific and cultural data in alignment with Indigenous Data Governance principles and culturally safe research practices. Well-developed interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to build trusted relationships with Elders, community members and research teams. Confidence using digital tools such as Microsoft Office to support coordination, documentation and reporting. Experience working in community-based settings and managing multiple responsibilities while meeting agreed timeframes. Highly desirable: An Aboriginal person who is a part of the Minjerribah |Terrangerri Quandamooka community or is known to the community. ​ About UQ   As part of the UQ community, you will work alongside bright minds from around the world, united by a shared commitment to creating a better world. Excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued, and staff are supported through a wide range of benefits, including up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% superannuation, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, salary packaging, and cultural leave.    UQ is committed to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples’ learning, discovery and engagement, and to playing a leading role in reconciliation in Australia. Guided by our Strategic Plan, Stretch Reconciliation Action Plan and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Employment Strategy, we work in partnership with communities to embed reconciliation across our operations. You will be supported to connect with vibrant staff networks, including the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Staff Network, and your cultural identity will be respected, valued and embraced.  Interested?   For more information about this opportunity, please contact Associate Professor Karen Cheney via k.cheney@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at  talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a  resume  and  cover letter  summarizing how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section.     Other Information    Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   We're dedicated to  equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing  talent@uq.edu.au  or calling +61 7 3365 2623.  Applications close Thursday 5 November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-63610).  Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for mid to end of November 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Research-Fellow-Senior-Research-Fellow-in-Plant-Genome-Editing_R-69795-1": {
      "title": "Research Fellow/Senior Research Fellow in Plant Genome Editing",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nFull time\n\nQueensland Alliance for Agriculture and Food Innovation. Full-time, fixed-term position from January 2027 to January 2032. Base salary will be in the range $119,463 to $168,044 + 17% super (Academic Level B or C). Based at our St Lucia location .   About This Opportunity This is an exciting opportunity to build and lead purposeful research in crop biotechnology and plant genome editing. Working within a collaborative and multidisciplinary environment, you will contribute to research involving cereal, and forage crops important to Queensland, connecting expertise across crop breeding and genetics, crop physiology and agronomy, and food science. You will have access to UQ’s broad talent pool and research infrastructure as you help translate discovery into meaningful outcomes for research end users, industry and the wider community.   Key responsibilities will include but not limited to: Research: Develop an independent and collaborative research program in crop biotechnology and gene editing, producing high-quality research outputs and contributing to national recognition and international impact. Research funding and translation: Seek, secure and manage competitive research funding, while progressing the translation and potential commercialisation of knowledge, technologies and UQ intellectual property. Supervision and Researcher Development: Supervise Honours and Higher Degree by Research students, develop researcher capability, facilitate engagement opportunities and promote the responsible conduct of research. Citizenship and Service: Build productive partnerships across UQ and with industry, government, professional bodies and the wider community, while contributing to internal service, mentoring, committees and University priorities.   This is a research focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance .   For further information, please click here to view the information booklet.   About You Completion of a PhD in crop biotechnology, plant molecular genetics, plant gene editing or a closely related field. A growing or established research profile in crop biotechnology and gene editing, commensurate with the level of appointment. A strong record of quality publications in reputable refereed journals and presentations at academic or industry conferences. Demonstrated experience in designing and delivering research projects, with expertise relevant to cereal or forage crops. Evidence of successfully seeking, securing and managing external research funding, commensurate with the level of appointment. A developing or established record of successfully supervising Honours and Higher Degree by Research students. Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively across disciplines, including crop breeding and genetics, crop physiology and agronomy, or food science. Evidence of meaningful internal service and engagement with external partners, with the capacity to build relationships across industry, government, professional bodies and the wider community.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.   The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Professor Ian Godwin at i.godwin@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume, cover letter, and responses to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia and qualification checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.     Applications close Sunday 1 November at 11.00pm AEST (R-69795)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Herston-Campus/Senior-Clinical-Research-Assistant---Speech-Pathology_R-69897-1": {
      "title": "Senior Clinical Research Assistant - Speech Pathology",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nFull time\n\nQueensland Aphasia Research Centre (QARC) | Centre for Living Experience Research (LEX) Full-time, fixed-term position for 9 months (also open to part-time/job share arrangements) Base salary will range from $97,268 - $104,348 + 17% Superannuation (HEW Level 6)  Based at our Herston campus with some travel to regional Queensland trial sites About This Opportunity Join the collaborative team delivering the Conversations About Care trial, a purposeful research project working to improve communication support for older people with communication disability in residential and community aged care. We are seeking a qualified Speech Pathologist to work alongside clinical researchers and aged care partners to support high-quality trial delivery across metropolitan and regional Queensland, helping create meaningful change for older people, families and care communities. This is a rare opportunity to combine clinical expertise with impactful research. Joining an established and successful research team, you will leverage your clinical knowledge to contribute to innovative research that makes a difference. Whether you are seeking a change of pace from frontline clinical work or looking to broaden your career horizons, this role provides a valuable entry point into research and a strong foundation for future career growth.   Key responsibilities will include: Support the delivery of the Conversations About Care trial across metropolitan and regional aged care services. Recruit and consent participants using supported processes that enable older people with communication disability to make informed decisions, in accordance with Good Clinical Practice and ethical requirements. Conduct research assessments, observations and interviews with residents, family members and aged care staff, and assist with the delivery of trial interventions. This includes travel to regional Queensland for data collection, including planning and coordinating site visits, travel, and overnight stays (all fully funded). Maintain accurate, high-quality research records through scoring, data entry, data checking and report preparation. Build strong working relationships with aged care providers, site contacts, and staff, and represent the project professionally. About You Completion of a tertiary qualification in Speech Pathology. High-level interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to build strong working relationships, manage research issues sensitively and assertively, and engage with residents, families, aged care staff, and providers. Excellent administrative and organisational skills, including the ability to coordinate site visits and travel schedules, manage competing priorities, meet deadlines and work independently. Demonstrated analytical and research skills, including experience completing high-quality data collection, scoring, data entry and analysis. An understanding of confidentiality, privacy and information-handling principles within research ethics and governance requirements. A current driver’s licence and willingness to travel to regional Queensland, including overnight stays. Clinical experience working with older adults with communication disability, or experience in clinical research environments and qualitative interviewing, would be highly regarded. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.   Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, and salary packaging. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Associate Professor Sarah Wallace at s.wallace3@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number R-69897.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter . In your cover letter, please include responses to the ‘About You’ selection criteria above and, if interested in part-time/job share arrangements, indicate your level of availability. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks will include verification of your right to work in Australia, Speech Pathology Australia membership (or eligibility), a driver’s licence, a criminal history check and evidence of mandatory immunisations as required by participating aged care providers. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We’re dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Sunday 1 November at 11.00pm AEST (Job Reference Number: R-69897)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Senior-Project-Officer_R-69571": {
      "title": "Senior Project Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nFull time\n\nIndigenous Business Hub within the Business School Full-time (100%), fixed-term position for up to 12 months Base salary will be in the range $97,269 to $104,349 + 17% super (HEW Level 6) Based at our St Lucia Campus   About This Opportunity Join the UQ Business School as a Senior Project Officer and help deliver purposeful projects that strengthen the School’s Indigenous Business Hub, Strategic Directions and accreditation activities. Working within a small, collaborative and high-performing team, you will provide project management advice and coordination across a diverse portfolio, build respectful relationships with Indigenous communities and project partners, and work closely with internal stakeholders to translate ideas into well-planned initiatives and meaningful outcomes.   Key responsibilities will include:   Provide project management advice and support for strategic projects aligned with the Indigenous Business Hub and the Strategic Projects team. Support the planning and delivery of annual Indigenous Business Hub activities, including events, logistics, stakeholder engagement, data collation and project communications. Coordinate research grant submissions with relevant stakeholders, ensuring activities align with UQ policies and procedures. Contribute to business cases and project plans that incorporate budgets, risk assessments, task allocation, resource coordination, schedules and clear delivery milestones. Develop and maintain comprehensive project documentation, data collections, reports, presentations and communication materials that monitor progress and share project outcomes. Support reporting for the School’s Strategic Directions and accreditation requirements, working collaboratively with the Manager, Strategic Projects and internal teams.   About You Tertiary qualifications in business or a relevant discipline, project management accreditation, or an equivalent combination of relevant education, training and experience. Demonstrated experience providing project management support within a complex, multifaceted work environment. Strong organisational and administrative capabilities, with the ability to manage competing priorities and coordinate multiple projects and activities. Demonstrated cultural capability and experience building respectful, reciprocal relationships with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, communities and organisations. Experience working in partnership with Indigenous stakeholders, together with lived experience and/or cultural knowledge, would be highly regarded. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, including experience preparing and interpreting project plans, reports, flowcharts, presentations, schedules and stakeholder materials. Strong interpersonal, consultation and negotiation skills, with the ability to work effectively with community partners and stakeholders at all organisational levels. Excellent critical thinking and problem-solving skills, with an adaptable, innovative and solutions-focused approach. Knowledge of strategic planning and performance review processes in the tertiary education sector would be highly regarded.   About UQ  UQ is committed to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples’ learning, discovery and engagement, and to playing a leading role in reconciliation in Australia. Guided by the University’s Strategic Plan, Stretch Reconciliation Action Plan and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Employment Strategy, we work in partnership with communities to embed reconciliation across our operations.    As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.     Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging.    Interested?  For more information about this opportunity, please contact Rebekah Woodward, Project Manager via email R.Woodward@business.uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.     When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.      Other Information   Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.     You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.     We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion and particularly welcome applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples. We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.    Applications close 23rd October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69571) . Please note applications will be reviewed as received. Candidates may be interviewed prior to the job closing date. We encourage candidates to apply as soon as possible."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Placement-Manager--Clinical-Psychology---Neuropsychology-_R-69558-1": {
      "title": "Placement Manager (Clinical Psychology & Neuropsychology)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nFull time\n\nSchool of Psychology Full-time, fixed-term position for 12 months (also open to part-time/job share arrangements) Base salary will range from $120,317 - $134,892 + 17% Superannuation (HEW Level 8) Based at our vibrant St Lucia campus About This Opportunity Join our world-leading School of Psychology in a rewarding leadership role supporting high-quality professional training for future psychologists. As Placement Manager , you will manage existing student placements, develop new placement opportunities and models, and work collaboratively with students, colleagues and external providers to strengthen professional practice competence. You will contribute to an inclusive and supportive environment where operational excellence, trusted partnerships and innovative approaches create meaningful educational impact.   This is an ideal opportunity for experienced psychologists looking to broaden their career beyond traditional clinical practice. Combining student supervision, placement leadership, partnership development and strategic project work, the role offers variety, flexibility and the chance to shape the next generation of practitioners while maintaining a strong connection to the profession.   We welcome all applications. Flexible work arrangements will be considered for this parental leave cover role, including full-time, part-time and job share options, helping you achieve greater work-life balance.   Key responsibilities will include: Manage and continuously improve student placements, placement processes and supporting resources for postgraduate students in the UQ Psychology Clinic and external placement settings. Build collaborative relationships with external placement providers to establish new opportunities and support successful placement experiences. Help ensure placements provide appropriate training and meet relevant University, Psychology Board of Australia and Australian Psychology Accreditation Council requirements. Lead rural placement projects, develop promotional resources, supervise students as required, and coordinate case presentations, information sessions and consultation support for student clinicians. About You Postgraduate qualifications in Psychology, with Clinical Psychology or Clinical Neuropsychology Area of Practice Endorsement. Psychology Board of Australia Board-approved supervisor. A minimum two years’ professional experience as a psychologist within a clinical psychology or clinical neuropsychology context. Familiarity with the clinical psychology or clinical neuropsychology sector, including relevant organisations, practitioners and professional networks. Sound knowledge of contemporary evidence-based psychology practice, clinical education and accreditation requirements for professional psychology training. Highly developed interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to negotiate and build effective relationships with psychologists, psychologists in training, University colleagues and external partners. High-level organisational and coordination skills, including the ability to work independently, exercise sound judgement, manage competing priorities and take responsibility for decisions within established guidelines. Strong digital capability, with the ability to work effectively across technology systems and quickly develop proficiency in new software applications. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.   Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on-campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Kym Adkinson, School Manager, at schoolmanager@psy.uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number R-69558.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter. In your cover letter, please include responses to the ‘About You’ selection criteria above and, if interested in part-time/job share arrangements, indicate your level of availability. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks will include verification of your right to work in Australia, your qualifications and AHPRA registration, a Working with Children Blue Card, criminal history check and completion of mandatory immunisations. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We’re dedicated to equity, diversity and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Thursday 29 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (Job Reference Number: R-69558)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Lecturer-in-Audiology_R-69564-1": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Audiology",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nFull time\n\nSchool of Health and Rehabilitation Sciences Full-time, continuing position at Academic Level B Unique opportunity for a Teaching & Research or Teaching Focussed appointment Base salary will range from $119,462 - $141,545 + 17% Superannuation Based at our vibrant St Lucia campus   About This Opportunity    Our world-leading School of Health and Rehabilitation Sciences is seeking a dynamic and experienced Lecturer, with expertise in the delivery of rehabilitative audiology services, to join our dedicated Audiology teaching team. This opportunity will be offered as either a Teaching & Research or Teaching Focussed appointment, commensurate with the successful candidate’s skills, experience, and preference.   In this role, you will coordinate and teach across key postgraduate courses, such as AUDL7121 (Clinical Audiology II) and AUDL7211 (Clinical Audiology III) . You will have the opportunity to build a strong academic profile through contributions to teaching, research, service, and engagement, while extending your impact through course leadership, curriculum development, and educational innovation. Join us in inspiring and shaping the next generation of exceptional audiologists.   We encourage applications from across Australia. For those committed to relocating to Brisbane, we will support you with a relocation support package covering relocation expenses for which you can claim reimbursement. You can find out more about life in Australia’s Sunshine State here.   For further information about this position, please click here t o view the information booklet.   If you are unable to access this link, please email talent@uq.edu.au for a copy of the booklet.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Associate Professor Carlie Driscoll, Head of Audiology, at carlie.driscoll@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (R-69564) in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter addressing the relevant ‘About You’ selection criteria found within the information booklet above. In your cover letter, please also indicate which position you are applying for (Teaching & Research or Teaching Focussed). Please note that applications received via email cannot be accepted.   Other Information    Pre‑employment checks will include verification of your right to work in Australia, qualifications, Audiology Australia membership (or eligibility), and completion of mandatory immunisations. This may also include checks relating to gender‑based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We’re dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling 07 3365 2623.   Applications close Sunday 8 November at 11.00pm AEST (Job Reference Number: R-69564)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Herston-Campus/National-Clinical-Trials-Lead--MRI-Network_R-69439-1": {
      "title": "National Clinical Trials Lead, MRI Network",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nPart time\n\nHerston Imaging Research Facility (HIRF) Part-time (0.5 FTE), fixed-term position through to June 2028. FTE base salary will be in the range $120,317 - $134,892 + 17% super (HEW Level 8). Based at our Herston Imaging Research Facility, Brisbane. About This Opportunity Join Australia's National Imaging Facility (NIF), a world class collaborative network that provides open access to advanced imaging infrastructure, expertise, tools, data and analysis. This newly created national role offers the opportunity to shape and strengthen clinical trial capability across NIF's Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) network. Working closely with the Human Molecular Imaging Network Clinical Trial Coordinator and reporting to the HIRF Director, you will play a pivotal role in establishing nationally consistent approaches to clinical trial engagement, governance, coordination and capability development across 14 participating nodes throughout Australia.   This is a unique opportunity for an experienced clinical trial professional to create meaningful impact through a collaborative, visionary and purpose driven national network.   Key responsibilities will include: Leading the development and implementation of nationally consistent clinical trial governance, coordination and capability frameworks across the NIF MRI network. Building and maintaining strategic relationships with researchers, clinicians, medical physicists, clinical trial cooperative groups, sponsors, CROs, research organisations and industry partners. Coordinating and overseeing multicentre clinical research projects, including trial start-up activities, ethics and governance processes, site activation, contracting and stakeholder engagement. Driving improvements in data management, central data review, imaging quality processes, image repositories, education initiatives and network-wide clinical trial capability.   Please note this position is advertised externally as National Clinical Trials Lead, MRI Network to reflect its national scope and strategic focus. Within UQ, the role is classified as Senior Principal Clinical Trials Coordinator.   About You You are a collaborative and highly experienced clinical trials professional who is passionate about strengthening research capability and creating positive change through nationally coordinated initiatives. You thrive in complex stakeholder environments, exercise sound professional judgement, and enjoy building connections across organisations and disciplines.   You will demonstrate: A postgraduate qualification in health, science, clinical research or a related discipline, or an equivalent combination of relevant experience, education and training. Significant experience leading and/or coordinating clinical trial operations, programs or multicentre research activities within a health, research or academic environment. Strong knowledge of Good Clinical Practice (GCP), human research ethics, governance and Australian clinical trial regulatory requirements. Proven ability to build productive relationships and work effectively with researchers, clinicians, sponsors, CROs and other stakeholders. Excellent organisational, project management and communication skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities and work autonomously within a distributed national network.   The following would be highly regarded: Experience working within a CRO, sponsor organisation, clinical trial network or coordinating centre. Knowledge of Australian clinical trial regulatory pathways (CTN/CTA) and experience supporting trial start-up activities, including ethics, governance, contracting and site activation processes.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you'll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.   Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program and salary packaging.   At UQ, you will be challenged, inspired and rewarded while working alongside a connected network of like-minded people who share a commitment to collaboration, innovation and excellence. This role offers significant flexibility, including hybrid working arrangements and a 0.5 FTE appointment, while providing the opportunity to make a lasting national impact across Australia's clinical imaging research landscape.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Prof Katie McMahon, Academic Lead - HIRF at katie.mcmahon@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the 'About You' section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.     Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, criminal history checks, and mandatory immunisations. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We're dedicated to equity, diversity and inclusion. We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We're also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Wednesday 28 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69439). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for 9 November 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Director--Graduate-Research-School_R-69462-1": {
      "title": "Director, Graduate Research School",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\nUQ’s Graduate Research School Full-time, fixed-term position for up to 5 years  Based at our St Lucia Campus About This Opportunity  This is an excellent opportunity to shape the future of graduate research at one of Australia’s leading universities. As Director, Graduate Research School, you will lead the professional services that enable more than 4,000 higher degree by research candidates to pursue ambitious ideas and create meaningful impact. Working in close partnership with the Dean and Deputy Dean, you will set strategic direction, strengthen service delivery and ensure the School’s people, resources and operations are positioned for excellence. This is an opportunity to lead an accomplished and committed professional team while influencing UQ’s broader graduate research ecosystem. You will build trusted partnerships across the University, champion purposeful innovation and translate complexity into clear priorities and lasting improvement. Your leadership will create the conditions for talented people to thrive and for research candidates to succeed, from their first enquiry through to thesis submission and beyond. For more information about this opportunity, please view the information pack. About You You are a visionary and considered executive leader who combines strategic perspective with the judgement and discipline to deliver. You will bring postgraduate qualifications and extensive senior management experience in a large, complex organisation, or an equivalent combination of relevant knowledge, training and experience. Your background will demonstrate success in strategic and operational planning, resource and workforce leadership, complex service delivery, business transformation and cultural change. Credible, collaborative and purposeful, you build confidence across diverse stakeholder groups and know how to bring people with you through change. You set a compelling direction, communicate with clarity and trust the expertise of your people, creating autonomy with accountability. With highly developed analytical, interpersonal and influencing skills, you will foster an inclusive, high-performing culture in which contribution is recognised, independent thinking is encouraged and individuals and teams are empowered to excel. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.  Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging. Interested? For enquiries regarding the application process, please contact Executive Recruitment via executiverecruitment@uq.edu.au . When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section in the attached information pack .   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.  You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity.  We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing executiverecruitment@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Wednesday 28th October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69462)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Herston-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow-Research-Fellow---Health-Policy_R-69657": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow/Research Fellow - Health Policy",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nPart time\n\nSchool of Public Health Part-time (80%), fixed-term position for up to 3 years Base salary will be in the range $85,373 to $141,545 + 17% super (Academic Level A or B) Based at our Herston Campus About This Opportunity This is an exciting opportunity for a Postdoctoral Research Fellow/Research Fellow to lead qualitative and quantitative research within the NHMRC-funded partnership project, “Understanding and harnessing messenger effectiveness to translate evidence into health policy in Australia”. Working in a purposeful and collaborative research environment within the School of Public Health, you will be part of a multi-institutional research team working alongside five NGOs  to explore how credible and persuasive messengers can strengthen understanding, trust and the use of evidence among the public and policy makers. You will contribute to research with the potential to create meaningful change in Australian health policy. Key responsibilities will include: Research: Lead qualitative and quantitative research, including interviews with members of the public and policy makers, an experimental study, and media analysis.Produce high-quality research outputs consistent with discipline norms. Research translation and engagement: Translate research findings for research end users and work collaboratively with government, NGOs, professional bodies and the wider community to strengthen the influence of evidence on health policy. Supervision and Researcher Development: Contribute to, or lead as appropriate to the level of appointment, the effective supervision and development of Honours and Higher Degree by Research students and research support staff. Citizenship and Service: Demonstrate UQ values through collaboration, active participation in priority activities, internal service and the development of productive external partnerships. This is a research focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance. For further information, please click here to view the information booklet. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development. UQ researchers are advancing discoveries into practical health outcomes, including through a next-generation ovarian cancer screening trial designed to improve early detection . This reflects the visionary and collaborative research environment in which evidence is translated into meaningful change for communities. The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Associate Professor Katherine Cullerton . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section (found in the appointment booklet). Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia and qualifications checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We’re dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Sunday 1st November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69657). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for Monday 9th November 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Principal-Coordinator--Teaching-Recognition_R-69647-1": {
      "title": "Principal Coordinator, Teaching Recognition",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\nInstitute for Teaching and Learning Innovation . Play a key role in strengthening UQ’s culture of recognition, innovation and educational excellence. Full-time, permanent with Base salary in the range of $106,709 - $116,198 + 17% super (HEW Level 7).  Based at our vibrant St Lucia campus .     About This Opportunity    An exciting opportunity has become available to join UQ as the Principal Coordinator, Teaching Recognition. In this unique role, you'll lead a portfolio of prestigious awards, grants and professional recognition programs that showcase outstanding educators and advance teaching excellence across the University. Working within ITaLI, you'll partner with academics, senior leaders and key stakeholders to deliver impactful initiatives, provide specialist advice and help foster a culture of innovation, achievement and educational excellence. Key responsibilities will include:   Lead the end-to-end delivery of teaching awards, grants, accredited fellowship programs and other recognition initiatives. Manage multiple concurrent program cycles, including promotion, applications, assessment, approvals, outcomes, reporting and review. Provide specialist advice on program requirements, policy and governance matters to applicants, committees and stakeholders. Build and maintain strong relationships with academic and professional staff, senior leaders, committees and external organisations. Drive continuous improvement through enhanced systems, processes, stakeholder engagement and data-informed decision making.   About You     A degree with at least four years of relevant experience, extensive experience in an administrative or professional field, or an equivalent combination of education, training and experience. Substantial experience coordinating complex programs or end-to-end processes, ideally involving awards, grants, accreditation, professional recognition, assessments or committee decisions. Excellent planning and organisational skills, with the ability to manage concurrent activities and deliver accurate outcomes within fixed deadlines while working independently under broad direction. Sound judgement and demonstrated experience interpreting requirements, resolving complex issues and recommending appropriate action. Strong written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to prepare clear guidance, reports, briefings, recommendations and correspondence. Highly developed stakeholder engagement skills and the confidence to provide specialist advice to diverse audiences. Experience reviewing and improving systems and processes using data, feedback and practical insights. A high level of attention to detail and the ability to manage sensitive information appropriately.   About UQ   As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.     Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging.    Interested?   For more information about this opportunity, please contact Michelle Quinn on michelle.quinn@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.     When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.      Other Information    Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia and criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.     You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.     We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.    Applications close 28th October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69647)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Engagement-and-Operations-Coordinator_R-69105-1": {
      "title": "Engagement and Operations Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nPart time\n\nSupport the Chair in Climate Action and Ethical Transitions and Net Zero Observatory within the UQ Business School . Part-time (0.4 FTE), fixed-term position for up to 12 months, with potential for extension. Commencing in early 2027, with the possibility of an earlier start date. Base salary will be in the range $97,269 - $104,349 + 17% super (HEW Level 6). Based at our St Lucia location. About This Opportunity Join a purposeful and collaborative team working to accelerate the translation of research into real-world climate action. Working closely with Professors Belinda Wade and Matthew Hornsey, you will connect research with industry, government, philanthropy and the wider community while coordinating the systems, partnerships and activities that support the Chair in Climate Action and Ethical Transitions and the Net Zero Observatory. While the opportunity is presented as a 2 day a week , fixed term position there is some flexibility in this arrangement.   Key responsibilities will include: Identify and cultivate strategic partnerships, coordinate industry and policy engagement, and support productive relationships with business, philanthropic and community stakeholders. Translate research findings into accessible briefing notes, reports, impact summaries and other practical outputs for diverse audiences. Coordinate projects, events, budgets, timelines, reporting, grant administration and compliance activities. Navigate university systems and coordinate procurement, human resources processes, event logistics and external communications while identifying opportunities for continuous improvement.   About You A degree in business, psychology, social sciences, a technical discipline or a related field is highly desirable, although equivalent experience gained through administrative, operational or office-based roles will also be considered. Demonstrated experience in stakeholder engagement, partnerships or program management. Strong operational and project management capability, including the ability to coordinate competing priorities, timelines and deliverables. Excellent written communication skills and the ability to tailor information for academic, policy, industry, philanthropic and community audiences. An ability to translate complex research findings into clear, practical and engaging outputs. Experience working across academia, policy, industry or the not-for-profit sector. A collaborative and inclusive approach, with the confidence to build trusted relationships across organisational and sector boundaries. Experience in a university environment, particularly at UQ, would be highly regarded.   About UQ   As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.     Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging.    Interested?   For more information about this opportunity, please contact Professor Belinda Wade via email b.wade@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.     When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.      Other Information    Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.     You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.     We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.    Applications close 4th November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69105). Please note applications will be reviewed as received. Candidates may be interviewed prior to the job closing date. We encourage candidates to apply as soon as possible. UQ reserves the right to close this recruitment process prior to the advertised close date."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Clinical-Education-Liaison-Manager---Physiotherapy_R-69355-1": {
      "title": "Clinical Education Liaison Manager – Physiotherapy",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\nSchool of Health and Rehabilitation Sciences Full-time, continuing position at HEW Level 8 Base salary will range from $120,317 - $134,892 + 17% Superannuation Based at our vibrant St Lucia campus About This Opportunity We are seeking an experienced Physiotherapist to join the dedicated and supportive Clinical Education team within our world-leading School of Health and Rehabilitation Sciences as a Clinical Education Liaison Manager .   In this pivotal role, you will lead the coordination and continuous improvement of high-quality clinical practice experiences for our physiotherapy students. Working collaboratively with practice educators, academic colleagues, placement providers and administrative teams, you will strengthen clinical education, support students as they move towards professional practice and build purposeful partnerships across the health and education sectors.   Key responsibilities will include: Develop, deliver and evaluate education, training strategies and resources for practice educators who facilitate and assess student learning in clinical settings. Manage practice placement allocations and associated processes, ensuring students receive balanced experiences aligned with relevant professional standards. Build collaborative relationships with public, private, education, industry and non-government stakeholders to develop and evaluate placement opportunities. Monitor clinical education activities, lead placement administration and reporting, and support compliance with relevant legislation, policies and procedures. About You A degree in Physiotherapy with a minimum of five years relevant clinical experience. Demonstrated knowledge and experience in the clinical education of students. Excellent interpersonal skills, including negotiation and conflict management, with the ability to build positive relationships with stakeholders through networking and engagement. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to advise diverse stakeholders and present effectively across a range of forums. Strong organisational and time-management skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities through to successful completion. Expert knowledge of, or ability to rapidly acquire, UQ and placement provider IT and human resource management systems to support effective leadership and management of clinical placements. Initiative, sound judgement and problem-solving capability, supported by a commitment to improving operational and strategic outcomes. A collaborative approach to working in interprofessional teams, with experience delivering workshops, tutoring, group facilitation or classroom teaching considered desirable. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.   Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on-campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Pascale Nijssen, School Manager, at p.nijssen@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number R-69355.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns with the ‘About You’ selection criteria above. Please note that applications received via email cannot be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks will include verification of your right to work in Australia, your qualifications, AHPRA registration, First Aid/CPR certification, driver’s licence and completion of mandatory immunisations. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We’re dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Saturday 31 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (Job Reference Number: R-69355). Please note that interviews will be scheduled in the fortnight following the closing date."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Lecturer-in-Software-Engineering-and-or-Cyber-Security_R-69593-1": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Software Engineering and/or Cyber Security",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\nFaculty of Engineering, Architecture and Information Technology / School of Electrical Engineering and Computer Science Full-time permanent position Base salary will be in the range $119,462.94 - $141,545.09 + 17% super (Academic Level B) Based at our St Lucia Campus   About This Opportunity  This is an exciting opportunity for a teaching-focused Lecturer in Software Engineering and/or Cyber Security to create meaningful impact on the next generation of technology professionals. Working in a collaborative and inclusive academic environment, you will lead engaging, studio-based learning experiences, coordinate contemporary courses and help students develop the practical and human-centred capabilities needed to navigate a rapidly changing discipline, including the opportunities and challenges presented by AI assistants.   Key responsibilities will include: Teaching: Develop and deliver engaging undergraduate and postgraduate courses in software engineering and/or cyber security, including studio-based learning, course coordination, assessment, academic counselling and the application of relevant University teaching rules. Curriculum and educational innovation: Collaborate with program advisers, colleagues and stakeholders to design contemporary programs, learning materials and assessment practices that respond to developments in technology, industry and student learning. Scholarship of teaching and learning: Build an emerging profile in teaching impact and the scholarship of teaching and learning by exploring, evaluating and communicating evidence-based pedagogical practices and contributing to relevant projects and funding applications. Citizenship and service: Demonstrate UQ values through collaboration, participation in priority activities and committees, and the development of productive relationships with industry, government, professional bodies and the wider community.   This is a teaching focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance .   About You   You will bring disciplinary expertise and a purposeful, student-centred approach to teaching. You will also demonstrate: A PhD or equivalent qualification in Information Technology, Software Engineering, Computer Science or a related area. Expert knowledge gained through academic or industry practice in software engineering, cyber security or a closely related field. Experience teaching software engineering and/or cyber security at tertiary level, with the ability to contribute to broader computer science and information technology curricula. An emerging profile of teaching impact and/or education research, including involvement in curriculum development at course or program level. Experience designing engaging learning materials, assessment tasks and educational experiences, including studio-based learning or other innovative teaching approaches. Participation in projects that have improved curriculum, pedagogy or assessment practices, with experience contributing to funding applications considered desirable. Evidence of involvement in the supervision of Honours or Higher Degree by Research students, where appropriate, in education or discipline-based research. Well-developed communication, interpersonal and consultative skills, with a demonstrated ability to collaborate effectively with colleagues, students and external stakeholders.   We particularly welcome applications from women, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people with disability, members of the LGBTQIA+ community, and others who may be underrepresented in this discipline. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.   The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Associate Professor Mark Utting at m.utting@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.    When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume that includes a link to your public Google Scholar profile , an 1-page cover letter , and responses of no more than three pages total addressing the requirements in the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. A teaching seminar may be requested for selected candidates. Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia and qualification checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   Relocating from interstate or overseas? We may support you with obtaining employer-sponsored work rights and a relocation support package, however upfront costs will still be expected to be covered. You can find out more about life in Australia’s Sunshine State here .   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Thursday November 12th 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69593)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Manager--Resource-Delivery-and-Physical-Collections_R-68987-2": {
      "title": "Manager, Resource Delivery and Physical Collections",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\nLibrary Information Resources team (UQ Library) Shape the future of library service delivery at UQ, combining strategic leadership, people management and library systems expertise in a role with university-wide impact. Full-time, permanent with Base salary in the range $120,317.28 - $134,892.52 + Competitive 17% super (HEW Level 8)  Based at our Vibrant St Lucia Campus About This Opportunity    Join UQ Library in a pivotal leadership role as Manager, Resource Delivery and Physical Collections, where you'll lead four specialist teams comprising 16 staff and shape the delivery of contemporary library services across circulation, membership, document delivery and physical collections. You'll oversee critical library systems, drive service improvements, resolve complex operational and technical challenges, and build strong partnerships across the Library and University. This is an exciting opportunity to influence strategic service delivery and make a lasting impact on the services that support our students, researchers and wider community. Key responsibilities will include:   Provide effective leadership and management of the Resource Delivery and Physical Collection Team ensuring operational efficiency within a collaborative culture that supports the vision of the Library and the University and providing guidance, feedback, and coaching to help improve the capability and performance of the team.  Manage the development, implementation, monitoring and reporting on the library’s global circulation, membership and document supply policies and procedures.    Manage the Library’s physical collections (including the Warehouse) and information delivery services and associated policies.   Manage the document delivery and library services platform configuration and development in relation to fulfillment, patron management and document delivery.  Resolve complex problems including system configuration and integration, liaise with the system vendors and service providers and manage support and enhancement requests.  Participate in the University of Queensland’s overall strategic planning and policy development.   Manage relationships with library clients, library vendors, publishers, data owners and library staff from other institutions.  Please note regular work at the Library Warehouse at the Gatton Campus will be required. About You     You bring a combination of strategic thinking, technical capability and people leadership, enabling you to deliver high-quality services in a dynamic environment. To be successful in this role, you will also demonstrate: Relevant postgraduate and/or professional library qualifications, or an equivalent combination of experience and education. Proven ability to lead, motivate and develop high-performing teams while fostering a culture of collaboration, inclusion and continuous improvement. Significant experience leading library services, including circulation, membership and document delivery operations. Strong expertise in document delivery and library management systems, configuration and development, particularly in fulfilment and patron management. A sound understanding of physical collections management and information delivery workflows. Exceptional communication and stakeholder engagement skills, with the ability to influence, build partnerships and work effectively across diverse groups. Strong analytical and problem-solving skills, with experience using evidence-based insights to drive operational and service improvements. Excellent organisational skills, with the ability to manage multiple priorities, projects and stakeholders in a dynamic environment. A proactive, collaborative approach, with the initiative, drive and enthusiasm to contribute to a positive and high-performing team culture. About UQ   As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.     Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging.    Interested?   For more information about this opportunity, please contact Karen Seymour on k.seymour@library.uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.     When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.      Other Information    Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia and criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.     You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.     We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.    Applications close Wednesday 28 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68987)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Principal-Technical-Officer---Robotics_R-69551-1": {
      "title": "Principal Technical Officer - Robotics",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\nFaculty of Engineering, Architecture and Information Technology / School of Electrical Engineering and Computer Science Full-time permanent position Base salary will be in the range $106,709.01 - $116,198.52 + 17% super (HEW Level 7) Based at our St Lucia Campus   About This Opportunity  The Principal Technical Officer - Robotics provides high-level specialist technical support across UQ’s robotics teaching and research environments. As part of the Engineering & Technical Support Group, you will bring deep expertise in robotics and mechatronic engineering to support safe, reliable and innovative laboratories and technical infrastructure.   Working independently and collaboratively with academic and professional colleagues, you will help create practical environments where staff and students can experiment, learn and solve complex engineering challenges together.   Key responsibilities will include: Provide expert technical support for the design, operation, maintenance, repair and calibration of specialised robotics equipment and infrastructure, including mobile platforms, robot arms, mobile manipulators, aerial platforms and motion capture arenas. Develop and maintain safe systems of work for robotics laboratories, including risk assessments, Safe Operating Procedures, laser safety procedures, electrical safety requirements and other relevant documentation. Support the design and construction of experimental systems and practical teaching activities, applying specialist knowledge across sensors, actuators, PCB design, embedded systems, ROS2 and hardware-software integration. Provide technical leadership in robotics laboratory environments through training, mentoring and supervision of staff and students, laboratory oversight, equipment procurement and continuous improvement of technical infrastructure and services.   About You   A relevant degree with at least four years of subsequent relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of extensive experience and/or education and training in robotic and mechatronic engineering and systems engineering. Demonstrated expertise in robotic and mechatronic systems, including installation, integration, networking, troubleshooting, testing and maintenance. Proficiency with Robot Operating System 2 (ROS2), Python and C++, together with experience operating, programming and maintaining ground robot platforms and robot arms. Demonstrated experience integrating sensors and actuators with robotic platforms, including designing custom PCBs for bespoke configurations and networking multiple robots and devices. Strong knowledge of applicable Australian electrical standards and safety legislation, with experience preparing and implementing safe systems of work and risk assessments. High-level organisational, analytical and problem-solving capabilities, with the ability to work independently while contributing effectively within a collaborative technical, academic and professional environment. Excellent communication and interpersonal skills, including demonstrated experience training, supervising or mentoring technical staff and/or students. Experience in a higher education or research environment is highly regarded. You will need to meet relevant University WHS requirements and be eligible to obtain a Blue Card. First Aid and laser safety qualifications can be obtained in the role, while a restricted electrical licence is desirable.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.   Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Malcolm Marker at m.marker@uq.edu.au .  For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.    When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, and Blue Card checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Wednesday October 28th 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69551). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for the week commencing November 2nd 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Herston-Campus/Casual-Senior-Research-Assistant---IVCare-Adaptive-Platform-Trial_R-69644-1": {
      "title": "Casual Senior Research Assistant – IVCare Adaptive Platform Trial",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nCasual\n\nSchool of Nursing, Midwifery and Social Work Casual position with an hourly rate in the range of $56.47 to $62.77, plus 12% super (HEW Level 5) Based at our Herston location About This Opportunity Casual Senior Research Assistant opportunity with the Infection Prevention and Vascular Access Group contributing to purposeful clinical research focused on preventing and detecting infection. Working collaboratively with multidisciplinary clinical and research teams, consumers and other key stakeholders, you will coordinate and implement research projects and clinical trials, supporting high-quality delivery from project establishment through to participant recruitment, data collection and progress reporting. Key responsibilities will include: Develop, coordinate and maintain regulatory submissions for multi-site clinical research projects in accordance with Good Clinical Practice guidelines, ethics regulations and research governance requirements. Support project establishment and delivery by coordinating stakeholders, research meetings, participant recruitment, engagement activities, assessments and intervention episodes. Maintain clinical trial management systems, including database design and construction, data collection, cleaning and entry, and the preparation of reports arising from data analysis. Monitor and report on research implementation, ethical considerations, data collection progress and compliance, and contribute to abstracts, posters, presentations and publications. About You Completion of a degree level qualification in Science, Nursing, Medicine or related discipline; or completion of a Diploma with relevant work experience and/or clinical research experience; or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and/or education/training. Ability to work collaboratively with colleagues. Ability to work independently on routine procedures. Excellent oral and written communication skills. Well-organised, methodical and self-motivated. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available, such as access to exclusive internal-only vacancies and affordable parking from just $6.40 a day. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Daner Ball at d.ball@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns with the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information  As a casual employee, and in line with Fair Work , please note that there is no commitment to ongoing work in this position.  Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We’re dedicated to equity, diversity and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Tuesday 27 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69644). Please note that applications will be reviewed as received. Candidates may be interviewed prior to the job closing date. We encourage candidates to apply as soon as possible. The University of Queensland reserves the right to close this application process early."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Herston-Campus/Casual-Research-Data-Officer---IVCare-Adaptive-Platform-Trial_R-69660": {
      "title": "Casual Research Data Officer - IVCare Adaptive Platform Trial",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nCasual\n\nSchool of Nursing, Midwifery and Social Work Casual position with an hourly rate in the range of $56.47 to $62.77, plus superannuation of 12% (HEW Level 5) Based at our Herston location About This Opportunity Join the Infection Prevention and Vascular Access Group as a Casual Research Data Officer and contribute to purposeful health research that helps clinicians and consumers prevent and detect infection across clinical specialties. Working within a collaborative, inclusive and interdisciplinary environment, you will support the delivery of high-quality research by managing complex health data, strengthening data integrity and helping transform evidence into meaningful research outputs. Key responsibilities will include: Support quantitative data analysis for systematic reviews and meta-analyses. Design, develop, test and maintain electronic research data capture systems, including undertaking quality checks, monitoring, data manipulation, extraction and archiving. Monitor data quality, produce descriptive statistics and data quality reports, and support secure data backup, transfer and archiving. Support statisticians with data preparation for Data and Safety Monitoring Board reports and contribute to funding applications, manuscripts, conference presentations, reports, graphs and figures. About You Completion of a degree-level qualification in data science, biostatistics, epidemiology or a related area, with relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of relevant education, training and experience. Demonstrated research experience working with health data. Developed information technology skills relevant to database development and research data management. An interest in innovative data architecture, including combining primary and secondary datasets. Advanced skills using Microsoft Office products. The ability to work collaboratively with colleagues while also working independently under broad direction. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, together with an organised, methodical and self-motivated approach. Knowledge of clinical data management, data privacy, biomedical research methods, statistical concepts or electronic research systems such as REDCap, Medidata, RevMan or Covidence would be highly regarded. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available, such as access to exclusive internal-only vacancies and affordable parking from just $6.40 a day. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Daner Ball at d.ball@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns with the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information As a casual employee, and in line with Fair Work , please note that there is no commitment to ongoing work in this position.  Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We’re dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Tuesday 27 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69660). Please note applications will be reviewed as received. Candidates may be interviewed prior to the job closing date. We encourage candidates to apply as soon as possible. The University of Queensland reserves the right to close this application process early."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow-Research-Officer-in-Microfluidics_R-69888": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow/Research Officer in Microfluidics",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nFull time\n\nInstitute for Molecular Bioscience Full-time fixed-term position for up to 12 months Base salary will be in the range $85,372.89 - $113,659.72 + 17% super (Academic Level A) Based at our St Lucia Campus   About This Opportunity  This is an exciting opportunity for a Postdoctoral Research Fellow or Research Officer to contribute to innovative research in microfluidics. Working in a collaborative and interdisciplinary research environment, you will design and fabricate microfluidic devices and apply expertise in polymer chemistry, hydrogels and surface immobilisation chemistry to advance the team’s research program in peptide and protein drug discovery.   The position will also collaborate with researchers working across complementary areas such as protein biochemistry, molecular biology and computational biology. This provides an opportunity to connect microfluidic technologies with broader biological research applications while developing your research expertise and emerging profile.   Key responsibilities will include:  Research: Develop a coherent research program in microfluidics and drug discovery, produce quality research outputs, draw upon best-practice methodologies, and contribute to joint projects and competitive funding applications. Research translation: Progress the transfer of knowledge, technology and research practices to end users, including opportunities to contribute to the commercialisation of UQ intellectual property. Supervision and Researcher Development: Contribute to the effective supervision and development of Honours and Higher Degree by Research students, as appropriate, through constructive feedback, coaching and responsible research practice. Citizenship and Service: Demonstrate UQ values through collaboration, active participation in priority activities, support for colleagues, internal service and the development of relationships with industry, government, professional bodies and the wider community.   This is a research focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance .   About You  You will possess: Completion or near completion of a PhD in bioengineering or a closely related discipline. Demonstrated expertise in microfluidic device design and fabrication. Demonstrated experience in polymer chemistry, hydrogels or surface immobilisation chemistry. Additional expertise in 1) cell and animal biology, 2) computational biology, or 3) molecular biology and protein expression would be highly regarded. Experience applying microfluidic technologies to biological systems, biomolecular analysis, protein engineering or related applications would be advantageous. Effective written, verbal and interpersonal communication skills. An emerging research profile, including evidence of quality research outputs in relevant refereed journals or conferences. The ability to work collaboratively across disciplinary boundaries and contribute to joint research projects. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.   The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Associate Professor Conan Wang at c.wang@imb.uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.    When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume (2 pages) and responses to the ‘About You’ section (2 pages). Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia and qualification checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Tuesday October 27th 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69888)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Lecturer---Robotics-and-Mechatronics-Engineering_R-69302-2": {
      "title": "Lecturer - Robotics and Mechatronics Engineering",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nFull time\n\nFaculty of Engineering, Architecture and Information Technology / School of Electrical Engineering and Computer Science Full-time fixed-term position for up to 12 months Base salary will be in the range $119,462.94 - $141,545.09 + 17% super (Academic Level B) Based at our St Lucia Campus   About This Opportunity  Join a collaborative community of robotics and mechatronics academics working across disciplinary boundaries to shape the next generation of engineers. As a Lecturer, you will deliver engaging teaching, coordinate courses and help design the new Robotics and Mechatronic Engineering plan, connecting contemporary practice in robotics, mechatronics, signal processing, control systems and engineering programming with an exceptional student experience. You will also contribute to purposeful research, student supervision, academic service and partnerships within and beyond UQ.   Key responsibilities will include:  Teaching: Design, coordinate and deliver undergraduate and postgraduate courses, including lectures, tutorials, assessment and student supervision. Contribute to innovative curriculum and assessment design informed by contemporary educational practice, student feedback and industry needs. Research: Collaborate with colleagues and postgraduate students on interdisciplinary research projects, contribute to quality research outputs and apply responsible, contemporary research methodologies. Supervision and Researcher Development: Supervise Honours, bachelor’s thesis and Higher Degree by Research students, supporting the development of their technical capabilities, professional confidence and responsible research practice. Citizenship and Service: Build productive partnerships with colleagues, industry, government, professional bodies and the wider community. Contribute to internal service roles, mentor others and consistently demonstrate UQ values.   This is a teaching focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance .   About You  A PhD in robotics or mechatronic engineering, or a closely related discipline. A growing profile in teaching and research in robotics, mechatronics or related areas such as signal processing, control systems or engineering programming. Demonstrated ability to deliver high-quality teaching at undergraduate and postgraduate levels across different settings, including small and large student groups. Experience designing effective learning materials and assessment tasks, using contemporary teaching approaches and technologies to promote student engagement. Demonstrated course delivery experience, with course coordination experience highly regarded. Experience contributing to curriculum development, pedagogical innovation or continuous improvement in programs, courses and assessment practices. A developing record of supervising undergraduate, postgraduate coursework, Honours or Higher Degree by Research students. Evidence of collaborative research, responsible research practice, effective academic service and engagement with colleagues, industry or other external partners. We particularly welcome applications from women, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people with disability, members of the LGBTQIA+ community, and others who may be underrepresented in this discipline. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.   The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Associate Professor Jen Jen Chung at jenjen.chung@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.    When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia and qualification checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Tuesday October 27th 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69302)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/IT-Project-Manager_R-69615-1": {
      "title": "IT Project Manager",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nFull time\n\nInformation Technology Services Full-time (100%), fixed-term position for up to 12 months Base salary will be in the range $120,317 - $134,893 + 17% super (HEW Level 8) Based at our St Lucia location About This Opportunity   Join Information Technology Services as a Project Manager supporting the successful delivery of a portfolio of strategic University-wide initiatives spanning digital accessibility, cyber security uplift, service improvement and organisational change. Working in a collaborative and purposeful environment, you will bring together business and technical stakeholders to deliver sustainable outcomes that improve digital experiences, strengthen security controls and risk management, build organisational capability and embed effective governance across UQ.    Key responsibilities will include:  Lead end-to-end project planning and delivery across multiple concurrent cyber security, accessibility, digital transformation and service improvement initiatives, coordinating technical, governance, operational and change workstreams.  Manage project performance, including plans, schedules, milestones, budgets, benefits, risks, issues, dependencies, decisions and deliverables, while coordinating resources, vendors, consultants and business stakeholders.  Support cyber security uplift through application security improvement, risk remediation, compliance activities, security assessments, governance forums and executive reporting on risks, controls, progress and organisational capability.  Build strong relationships across ITS and the wider University, facilitating workshops and consultations and delivering governance reporting, stakeholder engagement, communications, change management and business readiness activities that support successful adoption.    About You   Significant experience delivering technology, cyber security, digital transformation or organisational change initiatives within a large and complex organisation.   Demonstrated understanding of project governance, risk management and organisational change practices, with experience managing multiple concurrent projects or workstreams.   Demonstrated ability to coordinate stakeholders across business and technical domains and build effective working relationships across diverse organisational groups.   Strong written communication skills, including experience preparing executive reports, governance papers, project documentation, business cases, implementation plans and stakeholder communications.   Experience monitoring project scope, schedules, budgets, quality and benefits, and maintaining project risks, issues, actions, decisions, assumptions and dependencies.   Experience supporting cyber security programs, application security initiatives, technology risk management or compliance activities would be highly regarded.   Knowledge of digital accessibility, inclusive design, user experience or digital governance would be advantageous.   Experience working within higher education or another similarly large and complex environment would be highly regarded.    About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a visionary and inclusive community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.    Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on-campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging.    Interested?   For more information about this opportunity, please contact Ian Duncan - i.duncan@uq.edu.au .  For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line.    When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns with the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.     Other Information   Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, and criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.    You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.    We’re dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.    Applications close Tuesday 27 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69615)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Gatton-Campus/Emergency---Urgent-Care-Veterinarian_R-66721-1": {
      "title": "Emergency & Urgent Care Veterinarian",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nPart time\n\nSchool of Veterinary Science Full-time (100%), fixed-term position for up to 7 months Base salary will be in the range $120,317- $134,892+ 17% super (HEW Level 8) Join our Gatton Campus community – take a virtual tour ! About This Opportunity Join a collaborative, high-performing clinical team within UQ’s Veterinary Teaching Hospital, where clinical excellence meets hands-on learning and real-world impact. In this role, you will deliver advanced emergency and urgent veterinary care to small animal patients, with opportunities to provide care for exotic and small ruminant patients as required, while contributing to a dynamic teaching environment that supports students, interns and early-career clinicians. This is an opportunity to apply your clinical expertise in a purposeful and inclusive setting, working alongside colleagues who are insightful, supportive and committed to continuous improvement. Key responsibilities will include, but not limited to: Delivering high-quality emergency and urgent care to small animal patients, including independent management of complex and critical cases. Providing clinical leadership during after-hours and weekend services, directing nursing and technical teams. Contributing to clinical teaching, mentoring and supervision of undergraduate students, interns and junior clinicians. Supporting service development and continuous improvement initiatives within the Small Animal Hospital. As a 24-hour operation, the rotating roster provides exposure to a broad range of cases and opportunities to develop your skills across multiple specialty areas. Your take-home salary will be dependent on your specific roster, including loadings of 50% for weekend shifts, 25% for night shifts, and 15% for afternoon shifts. Afternoon shifts are shifts commencing at or after midday and finishing at or before midnight, Night Shift are shifts extending beyond midnight and finishing at or before 9AM. About UQ VETS A career at UQ VETS means making a difference to not only the lives of the people and animals in your care, but also the next generation of vets and vet technicians. At UQ VETS, we offer the opportunity to build a dynamic career through structured development programs and access to industry leaders. Instead of solely concentrating on meeting caseload benchmarks or revenue targets, your priorities lie in creating an impact, establishing a lasting impression on the field, and conducting work that brings about positive changes in the lives of animals. The Veterinary Teaching Hospital is an organisational unit within the School that includes all of the School’s outstanding clinical facilities. UQ VETS Equine Hospital is largely referral-based hospital with advanced facilities and offers a 24-hour emergency medical and surgical service. UQ VETS Small Animal Hospital boasts the latest in veterinary medical and surgical diagnostic and treatment options for small animals, birds, exotic pets and wildlife, as well as a 7-day urgent and emergency Care service. UQ staff also supervise clinical placements at the RSPCA’s Brisbane Animal Care Campus, Wacol.  In particular, the UQ VETS Small Animal Hospital values every animal and offers unrivalled medical, surgical and diagnostic services for dogs, cats, birds, reptiles, small mammals and fish at our world-class veterinary hospital in Gatton. You can take a virtual tour of our Small Animal Hospital here . About You A Bachelor of Veterinary Science or equivalent, registrable in Queensland. Demonstrated experience performing at an advanced clinical level in small animal emergency and critical care, with evidence of advanced clinical training, professional accreditation or equivalent experience. Demonstrated relationship-centred approach to veterinary care, with a commitment to developing genuine partnerships with the entire healthcare team, including the client. Demonstrated accountability and a proactive attitude with the enthusiasm and drive to identify and contribute to improvements the clinical context. Commitment and enthusiasm for the education of Veterinary Science and Veterinary Technology students. An understanding and knowledge of relevant legislative and professional requirements relating to veterinary practice in Queensland and to veterinary health and safety requirements. Ability to establish effective professional relationships and to represent the School of Veterinary Science to the university and wider community, including industry, government and professional bodies. Demonstrated effective interpersonal, teamwork, negotiation and consultation skills and formal written and oral communication skills. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds from around the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available, such as: Free parking and intercampus bus On campus childcare , medical clinic, and post office Up to 26 weeks paid parental leave/paid primary care leave 17% superannuation contributions + 17.5% annual leave loading Health and wellness discounts: fitness passport access, free yearly flu vaccinations, discounted health insurance, and access to our Employee Assistance Program for staff and their immediate family Career development opportunities: access to exclusive internal-only vacancies and our Study for Staff program 25% discount on eligible UQ Vets services Access to our $4.1 million UQ Sports facility Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Emma Gumz at e.gumz@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, criminal history checks, mandatory immunisations. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We're dedicated to equity, diversity and inclusion. We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Applications close 9 November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-66721)"
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Lecturer-in-Structural-Engineering_R-70515": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Structural Engineering",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nFull time\n\nSchool of Civil Engineering – Faculty of EAIT Full-time (100%), Permanent position Base salary will be in the range $119,462.94 - $141,545.09 + 17% super (Academic Level B) Visa sponsorship may be available for this position Based at our St Lucia Campus - Brisbane   About This Opportunity  We are offering an exciting Lecturer (Teaching and Research) position for a civil engineering academic with a strong background in structural engineering to join an accomplished structural engineering research group and contribute to engineering teaching excellence.   In this role, you will be teaching into the undergraduate and postgraduate engineering coursework programs, providing supervision to Higher Degree by Research (HDR) students, and contributing to the further development of programs within the School.  In addition, you will be expected to undertake research in the area of structural engineering and collaborate in research with other groups within the School, the wider university and industry.   Key responsibilities will include:    Teaching: Contribute to high-quality teaching in civil and structural engineering through engaging course delivery, innovative curriculum design and continuous improvement. You will coordinate and teach undergraduate and postgraduate courses, develop contemporary learning materials, undertake assessment, and provide academic advice and support to students.   Research: Develop an impactful research program in civil and structural engineering, collaborating with colleagues and postgraduate researchers on joint projects and competitive funding applications. You will contribute to securing and managing research funding, publish in high-quality journals and conferences, and apply best-practice research methodologies to deliver high-quality research outcomes.   Supervision and Researcher Development: Supervise Honours and HDR students while supporting staff recruitment, development, performance and workplace matters.   Citizenship and Service: Build strong partnerships across academia, industry and the wider community, while contributing to leadership, collaboration and University service.   This is a teaching & research position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance .   About You  Completed PhD in Structural Engineering, and/or Civil Engineering. Desirable that applicants have expertise in structural design (particularly concrete structures) and knowledge of Australian Standards or equivalent. Research interests in structural engineering may include such as areas as emerging structural materials, timber engineering, low-carbon-footprint high-performance concrete, behaviour of steel structures, structural wind engineering, and risk and reliability. Expert knowledge in a relevant discipline and ability to develop innovative research programs in the field of structural engineering. A growing profile in teaching and research in the discipline area. A developing reputation and track record of publications in reputed refereed journals and presenting at conferences. Evidence of successfully seeking, obtaining and managing external research funding. Evidence of a high level of quality teaching at undergraduate, Honours and postgraduate level across a variety of settings, including small and large groups. Experience in course coordination and contribution to the development and continuous innovation of new programs, curriculum design and course material in the discipline area. A growing record of supervision of Honours and HDR students to successful completion. Experience in meaningful internal service roles in conjunction with active contributions to external activities.   We particularly welcome applications from women, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people with disability, members of the LGBTQIA+ community, and others who may be underrepresented in this discipline.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.   The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Professor Tom Baldock via hos@civil.uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.    When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, mandatory immunisations checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   Relocating from interstate or overseas? We may support you with obtaining employer-sponsored work rights and a relocation support package, however upfront costs will still be expected to be covered. You can find out more about life in Australia’s Sunshine State here .   We are dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We are also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close on Wednesday, 25 November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-70515)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Lecturer-in-American-Australian-Studies_R-69944-1": {
      "title": "Lecturer in American/Australian Studies",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nPart time\n\nCentre for Western Civilisation / School of Communication and Arts Part-time (60%), with the opportunity to progress to full-time Fixed-term position commencing in Semester 1 2027, through to 30 June 2031 Base salary will be in the range $119,462 - $141,545 + 17% super (Academic Level B) Based at our St Lucia location About This Opportunity  In collaboration with the Centre for Western Civilisation, the School of Communication and Arts seeks to appoint an exceptional individual as a Lecturer in American/Australian Studies and contribute to teaching, research, and service within a Great Books program.   This is an exciting opportunity to develop your academic profile through teaching, research and service, lead and coordinate teaching activities, and supervise Honours and Higher Degree by Research students. As a Level B academic, you will build your research standing and work towards national recognition in your field.   Join a team delivering an innovative curriculum focused on foundational texts and ideas that have shaped Western thought. The Centre for Western Civilisation aims to recover the meaning of ‘great books’ and to critically evaluate their value and significance.   Key responsibilities will include, but are not limited to:    Teaching: Develop and deliver courses, engage in curriculum design, coordinate courses, assess students, provide academic counselling, and adhere to university teaching rules.   Research: Establish a research program, collaborate on research projects, seek and manage research funding, publish in reputable journals, utilize best practice research methodologies, and participate in project discussions.   Supervision and Researcher Development: Supervise students, recruit and manage employees, provide performance feedback and training, resolve conflicts, and ensure correct remuneration and benefits.   Citizenship and Service: Develop partnerships, demonstrate leadership through mentoring, engage in internal service roles and committees, perform administrative functions, provide support to colleagues, and uphold university values.   This is a teaching & research position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance .   About You Applicants should possess a PhD in Australian or American literature. Additionally, you will demonstrate: A growing profile in teaching and research in the teaching of American and Australian literature and culture as well as experience in teaching Great Books programs or other similar programs. A developing reputation and track record of publications in reputed refereed journals and presenting at conferences. Evidence of successfully seeking, obtaining and managing external research funding. Evidence of a high level of quality teaching at undergraduate, honours and postgraduate level across a variety of settings, including small and large groups. Experience in course coordination and contribution to the development and continuous innovation of new programs, curriculum design and course material in the discipline area. Experience in meaningful internal service roles in conjunction with active contributions to external activities.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.   The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Professor Alastair Blanshard at a.blanshard@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.    When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   About The Selection Process   As part of the Selection Process, applicants shortlisted for interview will be required to present a teaching demonstration to members of the school community prior to their scheduled interview. Precise instructions will be provided with adequate notice to shortlisted candidates.     Other Information Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia and qualifications. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Sunday, 15 November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69944)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Casual-Academic---School-of-Mathematics-and-Physics--SCIE1000-SCIE1100-_R-70246": {
      "title": "Casual Academic - School of Mathematics and Physics (SCIE1000/SCIE1100)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nCasual\n\nAbout This Opportunity The School of Mathematics and Physics is looking for casual academics who are enthusiastic about sharing their discipline knowledge, who can convey concepts to students with clarity, who are able to provide feedback in a timely fashion, and who are interested in developing and applying teaching strategies to support student learning in a range of different settings. Casual Academics are expected to foster caring, collaborative, and inclusive classrooms and laboratory environments.  The School of Mathematics and Physics values diversity and inclusion and seeks to ensure that all members of mathematics and physics communities at UQ feel empowered to apply to participate in such opportunities. We particularly encourage people who bring diversity to UQ – for example, gender identity, LGBTQIA+, cultural and/or linguistic, Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander peoples, or people with a disability - to apply for casual academic work.  Responsibilities Casual Academic responsibilities may include (but are not limited to):    Delivering applied teaching sessions    Marking   Teaching Support  Background Applicants should possess a strong academic background in mathematics or any area of science (including medical / biomedical science), and in addition have a good understanding of computer programming fundamentals (conditionals, loops, arrays, functions, graphing).    Prospective SCIE1000/1100 casual academics should normally be achieving a GPA of at least 5.5 and have completed at least 18 months of undergraduate study, and not be studying any first-year courses. Honours, Masters by Research and PhD candidates are encouraged to apply.  Postgraduate coursework students with completion of at least one semester of full-time postgraduate study are also eligible to apply.  What We Can Offer  Limited positions for SCIE1000 & SCIE1100 for casual academic work. These positions are casual academic appointments. As such, there is no firm advance commitment to ongoing or indefinite work. The casual rate will vary depending on the type of work completed. Please view our various casual academic pay scales.   Further details are available at the UQ Casual Academic website and the SMP Casual Academic Staff Opportunities website . For further information about UQ’s benefits, please visit Why Work at UQ and review  The University of Queensland Enterprise Agreement 2021-2026 (Enterprise Agreement).  Questions?   To discuss this role please contact Dr Mel Robertson-Dean ( m.robertsondean@uq .edu.au). For application queries, please contact  casualrecruit@uq.edu.au  stating the job reference number in the subject line.  Want to Apply?  Expressions of Interest can be submitted via the Apply Now link. Please provide your details, a resume and complete the questionnaire in full. If you are a current employee of the University (including casual staff), or hold a HDR Scholarship with the University, please login to your staff Workday account and visit the internal careers board to apply for this opportunity. Please do NOT apply via the external job board. The submission of your EOI is appreciated as soon as possible however is due no later than 11.59 pm on Sunday, 15 November 2026. Tips / Notes New SCIE1000/1100 applicants should request 4 hours of paid work a week . This is equivalent to 1 standard applied class and includes the contact and preparation/meeting time. Experienced SCIE1000/1100 casual academics may request more hours.  International students should ensure they have a visa that permits this type of casual employment before applying for a position as a casual academic.  Note that the application involves uploading a video explanation in answer to a question (choose between two questions provided in the application).   Classes will start in Week 1 of semester and will run for most weeks of semester.  The exact schedule is to be determined.  Experienced SCIE1000/1100 casual academics can also apply for a lead casual academic position – if you are interested in this, please add a comment at the end of your application. Normally lead casual academics have completed or are completing an honours degree.  If you are able and willing to undertake hours the advanced version of this course (SCIE1100), please indicate this with a comment at the end of your application.  If you are shortlisted, you will be contacted and will need to attend an interview. These are usually short (5-10 mins) and held on Zoom in December 2026. Details of interview times will be shared within a week of the job ad closing."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Dutton-Park/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow---Plastic-Chemicals-and-Human-Health_R-70079": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow - Plastic Chemicals and Human Health",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nFull time\n\nQueensland Alliance for Environmental Health Sciences Full-time, fixed-term position through to 31 December 2028   Base salary will range from $85,372 – $113,659 (Academic Level A) + 17% Superannuation Based at our Dutton Park campus   About This Opportunity Supported by research funded by the Minderoo Foundation, this is an exciting opportunity for a Postdoctoral Research Fellow to join the world-class, multidisciplinary team at our Queensland Alliance for Environmental Health Sciences.   Working with project lead Dr Xianyu (Fisher) Wang, you will investigate key exposure sources and measure plastic additive chemicals in human matrices. You will conduct independent research across environmental and analytical chemistry, build an emerging research profile, support and supervise students, contribute to service and engagement activities, and collaborate with experts who are advancing knowledge of plastics and human health.   Key responsibilities will include: Research: Lead or co-lead analytical method development, validation and application for measuring plastic additive chemicals in human and other complex matrices, while producing quality research outputs and publications. Research funding, translation and impact: Contribute to competitive funding applications, collaborative research projects and the translation of knowledge, technologies and practices to research end users. Supervision and Researcher Development: Contribute to the effective supervision and development of research support staff, Honours and Higher Degree by Research students, providing constructive feedback, training and professional development. Citizenship and Service: Participate in internal and external service activities and develop links and partnerships with industry, government, professional bodies and the wider community. This is a research focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance .  About You Completion, or near completion, of a PhD in environmental chemistry, analytical chemistry, environmental health, toxicology or a related discipline, with relevant research experience. Strong laboratory capabilities and demonstrated experience in trace organic chemical analysis of complex matrices, including human body fluids or tissues. Demonstrated experience operating and troubleshooting LC-MS/MS and GC-MS/MS instrumentation, and/or LC-MS QToF or GC-HRMS instrumentation. Knowledge of analytical method development, validation and application for trace-level chemical analysis. An emerging research profile in environmental chemistry, analytical chemistry or a closely related field. Evidence of publications in respected refereed journals and presenting findings at conferences. Evidence of contributing to applications for external research funding or other mechanisms of research support. The ability to work collaboratively within a multidisciplinary research environment and contribute to student supervision, service activities and external engagement. About the Team The Queensland Alliance for Environmental Health Sciences is committed to multidisciplinary research across environmental toxicology, epidemiology, microbiology, health risk assessment, risk communication and advanced monitoring of environmental hazards and exposures. Through the Minderoo Centre – Plastics and Human Health , researchers are developing methods to accurately sample and measure plastic chemicals and particles in humans, bringing together expertise in environmental health, toxicology, epidemiology and more to advance our understanding of the health outcomes associated with exposure to plastics.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.   The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Dr Xianyu (Fisher) Wang at x.wang18@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number R-70079.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter including responses to the ‘About You’ selection criteria above. Please note that applications received via email cannot be accepted.   Other Information Pre-employment checks will include verification of your qualifications and mandatory immunisations. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   We encourage all applications, both domestic and international. For those relocating to Brisbane, we may support you with obtaining employer-sponsored work rights (subject to candidate eligibility) and relocation support for which you may seek reimbursement (upfront costs will need to be covered). You can find out more about life in Australia’s Sunshine State here .   We’re dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Sunday 8 November at 11.00pm AEST (Job Reference Number: R-70079)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Principal-Learning-Designer--Assessment-Transformation-_R-70107-1": {
      "title": "Principal Learning Designer (Assessment Transformation)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nFull time\n\nBring your student-centred perspective, educational design expertise and excellent time management skills to a creative and purposeful role enhancing the quality of learning, teaching and assessment at UQ. Full-time (100%), fixed-term position until December 2027. ·         Base salary will be in the range $106,709 - $116,198 + 17% super (HEW Level 7) Based at our vibrant St Lucia location .   About This Opportunity Join the Business, Economics and Law (BEL) Faculty as a Principal Learning Designer (Assessment Transformation) and help shape innovative, secure and student-centred assessment at UQ. Working within the BEL Education Team, you will partner with academic staff, the BEL Senior Manager, Education, the Strategic Lead, Assessment Transformation in the Institute for Teaching and Learning Innovation (ITaLI), Faculty Principal Learning Designers and other specialists to strengthen curriculum, assessment, and active and flexible pedagogies across courses and coursework programs. This is an opportunity to bring your expertise to a collaborative and forward-looking environment, influencing assessment practice that supports student learning, academic integrity and preparation for an AI-integrated workplace.   Key responsibilities will include: Support Faculty staff to create, implement and evaluate innovative and secure assessment strategies that enhance student learning outcomes, align with curriculum goals and industry standards, and prepare students for an AI-integrated workplace. Develop assessment strategies informed by Universal Design for Learning (UDL) and programmatic approaches, and advise on the design, development, implementation and evaluation of resources supporting assessment feedback, security and integrity across courses and degree programs. Collaborate with the Strategic Lead, Assessment Transformation, Faculty Principal Learning Designers, senior stakeholders and subject matter experts to advance pedagogical outcomes, respond to University assessment transformation priorities, support the responsible use of AI and meet reporting requirements used to measure assessment transformation. Maintain knowledge of current and emerging assessment technologies and lead learning and educational design activities and projects, including defining scope and objectives, coordinating resources and sharing contemporary practice through Faculty and University communities of practice.   About You A relevant degree and at least four (4) years of subsequent relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and/or education and training in designing learning and developing resources that support students’ learning and assessment experiences. Demonstrated understanding of educational technologies and contemporary pedagogical practices that support student achievement, engagement and assessment security, particularly in the context of AI. Strong analytical skills, including demonstrated ability to analyse and interpret detailed and complex information, identify issues and solve problems relating to assessment security and integrity. Demonstrated ability to apply pedagogical theory, contemporary practice and practical experience to complex teaching and learning situations, using high-level problem-solving skills to develop innovative face-to-face and online learning and assessment solutions. Demonstrated capability to develop inclusive assessment approaches informed by Universal Design for Learning principles and the needs of students across degree programs. Highly developed interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to develop and maintain strong collaborative relationships with diverse academic, professional and senior stakeholders and a demonstrated commitment to responding to client needs. Demonstrated ability to work iteratively, adapt quickly to change and operate effectively both autonomously and as a member of a collaborative, client-focused team. Demonstrated capability to plan and lead learning and educational design activities and projects, balance priorities, coordinate resources and translate strategic objectives into practical outcomes.   About UQ  As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.     Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging.    Interested?  For more information about this opportunity, please contact Aliisa Mylonas, Senior Manager, Education via email a.mylonas@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.     When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.      Other Information    Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.     You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.     We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.    Applications close Wednesday 4 November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-70107) . Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for week beginning 9 November 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Toowong/Manager--Awards_R-70373-1": {
      "title": "Manager, Awards",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nFull time\n\nResearch Office This is a full time continuing position with salary in the range $120,317 - $134,892 (HEW 8), plus a generous super allowance of up to 17%. Lead a high performing awards team delivering specialist post-award research administration services that enable world class research outcomes across UQ. Lead the delivery of post-award research administration services, drive continuous improvement initiatives, and contribute to the successful management of research funding across the University. Currently based at our Toowong office on High Street, with occasional travel to other UQ campuses. (This role may potentially be relocated to our UQ St Lucia campus). About This Opportunity  The Research Office plays a pivotal role in supporting UQ's research enterprise, partnering with researchers, faculties and professional teams to secure, establish and manage research funding that drives innovation and global impact.   As the Manager (Awards), you will lead a team responsible for delivering high quality post-award research administration services across the University. Combining strategic leadership with hands on operational involvement, you will oversee complex funding agreements, provide specialist advice on policy and compliance matters, support team capability development and drive continuous improvement initiatives that enhance service delivery.   Reporting to the Senior Manager (Awards), you will foster a collaborative, customer-focused culture while building strong stakeholder relationships across UQ and with external funding partners.   Key responsibilities will include:  Leading, mentoring and developing a high performing Awards team to deliver customer-focused post-award research administration services. Managing complex funding agreements and providing specialist advice on research administration, compliance and contractual matters. Driving continuous improvement initiatives to enhance operational processes, service delivery and resource allocation. Building collaborative relationships with internal and external stakeholders and contributing to strategic projects, policy development and governance activities. About You   You are a collaborative and capable people leader who thrives in a fast paced operational environment and enjoys balancing strategic priorities with hands-on service delivery. You bring a client focused mindset, sound judgement, and the ability to build productive relationships across diverse stakeholder groups. Postgraduate qualifications, or equivalent extensive experience within research administration, grants management, higher education, government, compliance, finance or another complex operational environment. Demonstrated experience leading, coaching and developing teams within a complex administrative, operational, compliance or service delivery environment. Strong stakeholder engagement and relationship management skills, with the ability to influence, negotiate and provide trusted advice to a broad range of internal and external partners. Proven ability to manage competing priorities, coordinate high volume workloads and deliver quality outcomes within demanding timeframes. Experience driving continuous improvement initiatives, reviewing business processes and implementing practical solutions that enhance service delivery and operational effectiveness. Well developed analytical, problem solving and communication skills, with the ability to interpret policy, prepare reports and present complex information to diverse audiences. About UQ At The University of Queensland, we're creating change together. Ranked among the world's leading universities, UQ is a place where talented people come together to solve complex challenges, make meaningful contributions and shape the future.   Join a community where contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available. The broader benefits of joining UQ are extensive, including 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on-campus childcare and salary packaging.   Want to Apply?  All applicants must upload the following documents for their application to be considered: Resume. Cover letter addressing how your skills, experience and achievements align with the requirements of the role.   For more information about this opportunity, please contact Arian at a.ploeg@uq.edu.au . For application enquiries, please contact the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line.   When you apply, please upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns with the 'About You' section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, education and criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity and inclusion. We recognise that career pathways differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate transferable skills, relevant experience and their potential to succeed in the role. We're also happy to support accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process.   Applications close Sunday 8 November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-70373). Interviews will be tentatively scheduled for 13 November 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Toowong/Senior-Manager--Research-Grants-and-Submissions_R-70121-1": {
      "title": "Senior Manager, Research Grants and Submissions",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nFull time\n\nR esearch Office This is a full-time continuing position with salary in the range $140,766 - $148,862 (HEW 9), plus a generous super allowance of up to 17%. Currently based at our Toowong office on High Street, with occasional travel to other UQ campuses. (This role may potentially be relocated to our UQ St Lucia campus). About This Opportunity  Help researchers secure the funding that turns ambitious ideas into research with real-world impact. At UQ, our researchers compete for some of the world's most competitive research funding. Internally known as the Senior Manager, Pre-Award, join us in a senior leadership position to oversee major funding rounds, guide proposal excellence, and lead a high-performing team supporting applications to funding agencies such as ARC, NHMRC, MRFF and international applications. Working closely with researchers, academic leaders and research teams, you will help transform ambitious ideas into competitive, high quality funding proposals while leading institutional approaches to research funding, proposal development and submission excellence. Key responsibilities will include:  Support and develop the Pre-Award team through active mentoring and training, fostering a culture of expertise and service excellence across the Research Office. Oversee end to end competitive funding submissions and major grant rounds, including ARC, NHMRC and MRFF, ensuring compliance with university policies and sponsor rules while recruiting and supervising casual pre-award staff during major rounds. Contribute to strategic plans, policies, procedures and operational protocols, while developing high quality guidance materials to support researchers in preparing competitive funding proposals. Build influential relationships with senior academic leaders, including Heads of School and Centre Directors, and manage the effective review of funding agreements in coordination with Legal Services. Drive continuous improvement of the MyResearch platform and internal workflows, using data and feedback to establish long term priorities and enhance service delivery. About You   You understand what it takes to deliver successful grant applications in a complex and highly competitive funding environment. Whether your background is in research management, grants administration, research support, academia or a funding agency, you bring a strong understanding of the research funding landscape and a genuine passion for helping researchers secure funding and achieve impact. You thrive in fast paced environments where priorities shift, deadlines matter and quality is non-negotiable. You enjoy leading people, building trusted relationships and finding practical solutions to complex challenges with the ability to pivot quickly and strategically. You will bring: Postgraduate qualifications in a relevant field, or an equivalent combination of extensive relevant experience, management expertise and/or education and training. Significant experience supporting competitive research funding applications and grant submission processes. Proven management experience, with the ability to motivate, mentor and develop high-performing teams. Strong organisational and project management skills, with the ability to manage multiple priorities and demanding funding cycles. Excellent communication and stakeholder engagement skills, with the confidence to work alongside researchers, academic leaders and professional staff. A strong understanding of major research funding schemes and the factors that contribute to successful funding outcomes. Sound judgement, attention to detail and a commitment to service excellence. About UQ At The University of Queensland, we're creating change together. Ranked among the world's leading universities, UQ is a place where talented people come together to solve complex challenges, make meaningful contributions and shape the future.   Join a community where contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available. The broader benefits of joining UQ are extensive, including 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program and salary packaging. Want to Apply?   If you thrive on helping researchers secure funding, enjoy leading people through complex and fast paced grant environments, and want to make a meaningful contribution to world class research, we would love to hear from you. All applicants must upload the following documents for their application to be considered: Resume. Cover letter addressing how your skills, experience and achievements align with the requirements of the role. For more information about this opportunity, please contact Dr Jodi Clyde-Smith, Executive Director, Research Office at executivedirector.management@research.uq.edu.au . For application queries, please contact talent@uq.edu.au stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.  When you apply, please upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns with the 'About You' section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, education and criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity and inclusion. We recognise that career pathways differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate transferable skills, relevant experience and their potential to succeed in the role. We're also happy to support accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process.   Applications close Monday 16 November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-70121). Interviews will be tentatively scheduled for 30 November 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Student-and-Academic-Administration-Officer_R-69867-1": {
      "title": "Student and Academic Administration Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nFull time\n\nFaculty of Science Full-time, fixed-term position up to 24 December 2027  Base salary will be in the range $85,451.97 - $94,983.84 + 17% super (HEW Level 5)   Based at our St Lucia location        About This Opportunity   Join UQ as a Student and Academic Administration Officer and help deliver a positive experience for students throughout their university journey.  As a Student and Academic Administration Officer at UQ, you'll be at the heart of delivering services that support student success and academic excellence. Working within a truly collaborative team, you'll provide expert administrative support, solve complex problems, engage with a diverse range of stakeholders, and contribute to processes that help students thrive throughout their university journey.  Within the Faculty of Science’s School of Mathematics and Physics, you’ll support a broad range of student and academic administration activities, from enrolment and progression through to examinations and grades. You’ll join a supportive team where strong customer service, attention to detail and practical problem-solving will help keep the student experience running smoothly. Key responsibilities will include:    Deliver exceptional service and expert advice to students, staff and external stakeholders through a range of communication channels, helping to create a positive and seamless university experience. Coordinate a diverse range of student and academic administration activities, assessing applications, processing documentation and responding to enquiries in line with policies and procedures. Maintain accurate, secure and confidential student records, ensuring the integrity of information across key academic and administrative processes. Process student administration matters relating to admission, progression, examinations, class sign-on, course enrolment, grades and other stages of the student journey. Support academic administration activities, including course profile and Blackboard updates, timetabling, examination paper preparation, and grade uploads and amendments .    Join a vibrant professional community dedicated to supporting student success. As your experience grows, you'll have opportunities to expand your knowledge across a variety of specialist student administration functions, broadening your skills, developing your career, and discovering new opportunities within one of Australia's leading universities.    About You       A relevant undergraduate qualification, or an equivalent combination of experience, education and training that demonstrates your capability in a similar environment. The ability to confidently manage competing priorities, maintain accuracy and deliver results in a fast-paced administrative environment. Strong written, verbal and interpersonal communication skills, with the ability to provide clear advice and build positive relationships with a diverse range of stakeholders. A genuine commitment to delivering exceptional customer service, complemented by sound judgement, attention to detail and a proactive approach to problem-solving. Confidence in learning and navigating complex systems, databases and digital tools, with the ability to quickly adapt to new technologies and processes. An understanding of student and academic administration processes, or the ability to quickly develop this knowledge and apply relevant policies and procedures. The ability to work effectively both independently and collaboratively, including across teams and organisational areas, to support a positive experience for students from diverse backgrounds. High level organisational skills and attention to detail. We welcome diverse experience and encourage you to highlight any relevant experience in tertiary education or a large, complex organisation, or with systems such as SI-net, Business Objects or CRM systems.   About UQ   As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.    Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Hayley Wilson hayley.wilson@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.    When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.       Other Information     Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia and criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.    You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.    Applications close Sunday 8 November at 11.00pm AEST (R-69867). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for mid November."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Research-Fellow---Asia-Pacific-Heavy-Industry-Transitions_R-69578-1": {
      "title": "Research Fellow - Asia Pacific Heavy Industry Transitions",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nFull time\n\nUQ Business School within the Faculty of Business, Economics and Law (BEL) Full-time or part-time, fixed-term position until 31 December 2027 Base salary will be in the range $85,372.89 to $113,659.72 + 17% super (Academic Level A). Visa sponsorship is not available for this role Based at our vibrant St Lucia Campus .   About This Opportunity Join a visionary research initiative helping shape the future of green and sustainable finance across the Asia-Pacific. As a Research Fellow in Asia-Pacific Heavy Industry Transitions, you will work in a collaborative research environment to deliver applied, policy-relevant research on transition finance and pathways for heavy industry, particularly the iron and steel sector. Your work will explore financing and investments in emerging energy technologies including hydrogen and ammonia, alongside carbon pricing, trade and policy developments across Australia, China, Korea, Japan, India and other economies in the region.   Key responsibilities will include: Research: Contribute to the leadership and delivery of research projects examining heavy industry transition finance, and transition pathways, including quantitative analysis of transition technology finance and investment (e.g., hydrogen, ammonia), carbon pricing, international trade, and related policy and technology data, qualitative interviews, scenario analysis and publication in high-quality peer-reviewed journals. Research translation and impact: Translate research findings into accessible policy notes and briefs for government, industry and broader audiences, and present research at academic conferences and policy forums. Supervision and Researcher Development: Contribute to a collaborative research culture by sharing specialist knowledge, supporting researcher development and applying rigorous research practices across multidisciplinary projects. Citizenship and Service: Engage in inclusive team activities, contribute to internal service roles and committees, and help build productive partnerships with academic, industry, government and community stakeholders.   This is a research focused position . Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance.   About You A Master’s degree with approximately three to five years of relevant professional experience, or completion of a PhD in a related discipline. Demonstrated experience relating to iron and steel investment and green transition in Australia, China or another relevant country. Excellent investment, trade and policy data analysis skills, with demonstrated experience collecting, cleaning and analysing data using advanced statistical methods. A developing public research and thought-leadership profile demonstrated through high-quality publications or equivalent industry or professional outputs. Proficiency in reading and writing Chinese is highly regarded, supporting access to and analysis of primary-source materials and data. Knowledge of, or a strong interest in, industrial decarbonisation, energy transition, finance or climate policy. Experience contributing to research projects and/or research funding applications, together with the ability to collaborate effectively in multidisciplinary teams and build productive relationships with academic, industry and government stakeholders. Excellent written and verbal communication, organisational and prioritisation skills, with the ability to communicate complex ideas clearly to technical and non-technical audiences and deliver high-quality outcomes.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.   The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Professor Christoph Nedopil via email at c.nedopil@uq.edu.au . For application enquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume, cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia and qualifications. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We’re dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Monday 9 November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69578). Please note applications will be reviewed as received. Candidates may be interviewed prior to the job closing date. We encourage candidates to apply as soon as possible."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Research-Fellow---Professional-Development-Education-and-Training-Program-Leader----Minerals-Industry-Safety-and-Health-Centre_R-69807-1": {
      "title": "Research Fellow - Professional Development Education and Training Program Leader -  Minerals Industry Safety and Health Centre",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nFull time\n\nFaculty of Engineering, Architecture and Information Technology / Sustainable Minerals Institute Full-time fixed-term position thought to October 2028 Base salary will be in the range $119,462.94 - $141,545.09 + 17% super (Academic Level B) Based at our St Lucia Campus   About This Opportunity  This is an exciting opportunity for a Research Fellow to lead the Professional Development Education and Training Program for the Minerals Industry Safety and Health Centre (MISHC). Working within a collaborative, multidisciplinary environment, you will provide strategic direction for MISHC’s education and training portfolio, strengthening its position as a centre of expertise in capacity building for the resources and other high-risk industries across occupational health and safety, risk management, safety engineering and human factors. Alongside this leadership role, you will develop your research profile and work with industry, government and other partners to turn expertise into meaningful impact.   Key responsibilities will include: Teaching: Lead the strategic direction, design, delivery and evaluation of high-quality professional education and training programs, including contemporary delivery modes for industry and government professionals. Research: Develop an independent and/or collaborative research program, produce quality research outputs, contribute to competitive funding applications and work towards national recognition and impact in your research area. Supervision and Researcher Development: Provide effective supervision and researcher development through feedback, coaching and professional development, while contributing to a supportive and collaborative environment. Citizenship and Service: Build purposeful partnerships with industry, government, professional bodies and the wider community, contribute effectively to internal service and administration, mentor and collaborate with colleagues, and demonstrate UQ values.   This is a research focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance .     About You  A postgraduate qualification, progress towards postgraduate qualifications in a relevant discipline with extensive relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and training. Demonstrated industry experience relevant to risk management and health and safety in high-risk work, preferably within mining or quarrying. Knowledge of contemporary pedagogical approaches, including remote learning methodologies, and their application to professionals working across corporate and government environments. Demonstrated experience designing and delivering professional education and training for industry and/or government professionals and managers, together with an understanding of industry professional development needs. An understanding of the challenges involved in delivering training across varied cultural and organisational settings, supported by highly developed written, oral and interpersonal communication skills. Capacity to think strategically, identify and pursue new opportunities, and manage projects effectively while balancing priorities and deadlines within a multidisciplinary environment. Demonstrated ability to build productive relationships with resources industry personnel, government, civil society organisations and other stakeholders in professional development and training. Willingness and availability to travel regularly, including to support the delivery of MISHC courses internationally. Desirable: Experience in academic research. A Certificate IV in Training and Assessment, or equivalent trainer and assessor qualification. We particularly welcome applications from women, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people with disability, members of the LGBTQIA+ community, and others who may be underrepresented in this discipline. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.   The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Professor Maureen Hassall at m.hassall1@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.    When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, and Blue Card checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   Relocating from interstate or overseas? We may support you with obtaining employer-sponsored work rights and a relocation support package, however upfront costs will still be expected to be covered. You can find out more about life in Australia’s Sunshine State here .   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Tuesday November 24th 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69807). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for the week commencing November 30th 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Principal-Digital-Modelling-Officer_R-69251-2": {
      "title": "Principal Digital Modelling Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nFull time\n\nProperty and Facilities Full-time, continuing position. Base salary will be in the range $120,317 to $134,893 + 17% super (HEW Level 8). Based at our St Lucia campus   About This Opportunity Lead the digital blueprint of The University of Queensland. UQ’s campuses bring together heritage buildings, contemporary teaching spaces, world-class research facilities and complex infrastructure. Behind every project, asset decision and operational plan is a critical layer of digital information that must be accurate, accessible and built for the future. As Principal Digital Modelling Officer , you will lead UQ’s Digital Modelling Office and shape how built-environment information is created, governed, connected and used across our diverse property portfolio. This is a rare opportunity to combine hands-on BIM and digital engineering expertise with strategic influence and people leadership. You will set the direction for UQ’s digital modelling capability, lead a collaborative specialist team and work closely with senior leaders, project teams, consultants and contractors to improve the quality and value of our property information. Your work will directly support capital projects, campus operations, asset management and long-term planning, helping UQ make smarter, more confident decisions about its built environment. Key responsibilities will include: Lead the University’s digital modelling and built-environment information management function, establishing priorities, service plans and resource strategies that respond to current and future organisational needs. Develop and continuously improve standards, policies, procedures and governance arrangements for BIM, CAD, mapping, drafting, project information handover and associated digital workflows. Oversee the quality assurance, validation, integration and maintenance of drawings, floor plans, maps, surveys, reality-capture information and digital models, ensuring information remains accurate, accessible and fit for operational use. Provide specialist and strategic advice to senior leaders, project teams, consultants and contractors, while leading staff, technology initiatives, process improvements and service transformation. About You You are an experienced BIM, digital engineering or built-environment information leader who can move confidently between detailed technical work and strategic organisational conversations. You understand that digital models are more than project deliverables. They are valuable operational assets that support better planning, safer delivery and more informed decision-making throughout the asset lifecycle. Degree qualifications in a relevant discipline and extensive subsequent experience, or an equivalent combination of education, training and substantial management expertise. Demonstrated experience leading a significant digital modelling, built-environment information management, property, infrastructure or comparable specialist function in a complex organisation. Advanced knowledge and practical experience in Autodesk Revit and AutoCAD, supported by substantial expertise in contemporary BIM, CAD, digital engineering and structured asset information. Demonstrated experience developing and maintaining BIM and CAD standards, digital handover processes, policies, governance frameworks and information-management practices. Experience undertaking quality assurance, model validation and the integration of built-environment information with operational or asset management systems. Demonstrated ability to lead specialist technical staff, set priorities, manage workloads and resources, develop capability and guide teams through operational or technological change. Experience leading projects, procurements, technology implementations, automation, process improvement or service-transformation initiatives. Exceptional leadership, communication, negotiation, stakeholder engagement and problem-solving skills, with the ability to provide strategic advice, influence senior stakeholders and make complex technical and service-delivery decisions. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a visionary and inclusive community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available. Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on-campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Jake Williams at jake.williams@uq.edu.au . For application enquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns with the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We’re dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Sunday 15 November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69251)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Casual-Academic---School-of-Mathematics-and-Physics--Mathematics-and-Statistics-_R-70239": {
      "title": "Casual Academic - School of Mathematics and Physics (Mathematics and Statistics)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nCasual\n\nAbout This Opportunity   The School of Mathematics and Physics is looking for casual academics who are enthusiastic about sharing their discipline knowledge, who can convey concepts to students with clarity, who are able to provide feedback in a timely fashion, and who are interested in developing and applying teaching strategies to support student learning in a range of different settings. Casual Academics are expected to foster caring, collaborative, and inclusive classrooms and laboratory environments. The School of Mathematics and Physics values diversity and inclusion and seeks to ensure that all members of mathematics and physics communities at UQ feel empowered to apply to participate in such opportunities. We particularly encourage people who bring diversity to UQ – for example, gender identity, LGBTQIA+, cultural and/or linguistic, Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander peoples, or people with a disability - to apply for casual academic work. Responsibilities    Casual Academic responsibilities may include (but are not limited to):    Delivering practical and applied teaching sessions  Marking   Teaching Support  Background    Applicants should possess a strong academic background in Mathematics or Statistics, and normally be achieving a GPA of at least 5.5 and have completed at least 1 year of undergraduate study. Prospective MATH casual academics must have completed MATH2001 (or equivalent). Preference will be given to applicants who have completed at least 4 units of Level 2 MATH courses in total. Prospective STAT casual academics must have completed STAT2004 (or equivalent). Preference will be given to applicants who have completed at least 4 units of Level 2 STAT courses in total. Honours, Masters by Research and PhD candidates are encouraged to apply. Postgraduate coursework students with completion of at least one semester’s work of full-time study are also eligible to apply. Applicants who have completed higher-level MATH or STAT courses at UQ (or equivalent institutions) will be given preference. Postgraduate professional experience in STEM or teaching will also be regarded favourably.   What We Can Offer Limited positions for new Mathematics and Statistics Casual Academics.   These positions are casual academic appointments. As such, there is no firm advance commitment to ongoing or indefinite work. The casual rate will vary depending on the type of work completed. Please view our various casual academic pay scales.    Further details are available at the UQ Casual Academic website and the SMP Casual Academic Staff Opportunities website .   For further information about UQ’s benefits, please visit Why Work at UQ and review  The University of Queensland Enterprise Agreement 2021-2026 (Enterprise Agreement). Questions?  To discuss this role please contact Dr Samuel Kault ( s.kault@uq.edu.au ).  For application queries, please contact  casualrecruit@uq.edu.au  stating the job reference number in the subject line.   Want to Apply?  Expressions of Interest can be submitted via the Apply Now link. Please provide your details, a resume and complete the questionnaire in full.  If you are a current employee of the University (including casual staff), or hold a HDR Scholarship with the University, please login to your staff Workday account and visit the internal careers board to apply for this opportunity. Please do NOT apply via the external job board.     The submission of your EOI is appreciated as soon as possible however is due no later than 11.59 pm on Sunday, 15th November 2026.    Tips    If you want, and are qualified, to deliver sessions for specialist second or third year subjects you should mention this in your preferences.   New casual academics should be prepared to take on and should request 3 hours of paid work a week. This will include any preparation, delivery and marking.   International students should ensure they have a visa that permits this type of casual employment before applying for casual academic work.  Experienced casual academics can also apply for a lead casual academic position.   If you are able and willing to be a casual academic in advanced courses, please indicate these in your preferences as well.  If you are shortlisted, you will be contacted and need to attend an interview. These are usually short (5 mins). Details of interview times will be shared within a week of the job ad closing."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Herston-Campus/Senior-Research-Assistant--Gynaecological-Cancer-Research-_R-69390-1": {
      "title": "Senior Research Assistant (Gynaecological Cancer Research)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nPart time\n\nFrazer Institute Part-time (0.8FTE) fixed-term position until December 2027 Base salary will be in the range $97,268 - $104,348 + 17% super (HEW Level 6)  Based at our  Herston Campus   About This Opportunity    Join a purpose-driven research team at the forefront of improving outcomes for women affected by gynaecological cancer. Based within the Queensland Centre for Gynaecological Cancer Research at the Frazer Institute, this exciting opportunity will see you contribute to impactful clinical and translational research that advances scientific knowledge and supports meaningful change in patient care. Working alongside leading researchers, clinicians and collaborators, you will play a pivotal role in transforming complex research data into high-quality scientific outputs, helping to drive discoveries from the laboratory and clinical trials to real-world clinical impact. As part of UQ's collaborative and inclusive research community, you will be challenged, inspired and rewarded while working with talented colleagues committed to research excellence and improving lives.  In this role, you will: Collect, curate and manage complex laboratory, clinical and translational research datasets, ensuring the highest standards of data integrity and quality. Analyse and interpret laboratory, clinical and translational research data and liaise with researchers, clinicians and external collaborators to support project deliverables. Draft and contribute substantially to scientific manuscripts for publication in peer-reviewed journals, working closely with investigators to prepare and revise manuscripts through the publication process. Contribute to literature reviews, grant applications, ethics submissions and research reporting activities that support the success and growth of a dynamic research program.   About You     A tertiary qualification in health sciences, biomedical sciences, public health, or a related discipline, or an equivalent combination of experience and education. Demonstrated experience in research data management, analysis and interpretation within a clinical, biomedical or health research environment. Proven ability to contribute to, draft and prepare high-quality scientific manuscripts for publication in peer-reviewed journals. Strong analytical skills with experience transforming complex datasets into meaningful insights that support impactful research outcomes. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to collaborate effectively with researchers, clinicians and multidisciplinary stakeholders. Experience using statistical software such as R, SPSS, STATA, SAS or Python, and confidence working with laboratory and/or clinical trial datasets.  About UQ   As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of  benefits and rewards  are available.   Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, , and salary packaging.    Interested?   For more information about this opportunity, please contact Dr Eva Baxter – e.baxter@uq.edu.au For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at  talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.     When you apply, please ensure you upload a  resume  and  cover letter  summarizing how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section.  Other Information    Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, criminal history checks and mandatory immunisations. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.    You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to  equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing  talent@uq.edu.au  or calling +61 7 3365 2623.    Applications close Tuesday 10 November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69390).  Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for the 17 November 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Casual-Academic---School-of-Mathematics-and-Physics--Physics-_R-70245-1": {
      "title": "Casual Academic - School of Mathematics and Physics (Physics)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-23\nCasual\n\nAbout This Opportunity The School of Mathematics and Physics is looking for casual academics who are enthusiastic about sharing their discipline knowledge, who can convey concepts to students with clarity, who are able to provide feedback in a timely fashion, and who are interested in developing and applying teaching strategies to support student learning in a range of different settings. Casual Academics are expected to foster caring, collaborative, and inclusive classrooms and laboratory environments.  The School of Mathematics and Physics values diversity and inclusion and seeks to ensure that all members of mathematics and physics communities at UQ feel empowered to apply to participate in such opportunities. We particularly encourage people who bring diversity to UQ – for example, gender identity, LGBTQIA+, cultural and/or linguistic, Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander peoples, or people with a disability - to apply for casual academic work.  Responsibilities Casual Academic responsibilities may include (but are not limited to):    Delivering practical and applied teaching sessions  Marking   Teaching Support  Background    Applicants should possess a strong academic background in Physics, normally have achieved a GPA of at least 5.5 and have completed at least 1 year of undergraduate study. Prospective Casual Academics must have completed PHYS1001/1171 and PHYS1002 (or equivalent).   Honours, Masters by Research and PhD candidates are encouraged to apply.  Postgraduate coursework students with completion of at least one semester’s full-time study are also eligible to apply.  What We Can Offer     Limited positions for new Physics Casual Academics.  These positions are casual academic appointments. As such, there is no firm advance commitment to ongoing or indefinite work. The casual rate will vary depending on the type of work completed. Please view our various casual academic pay scales.    Further details are available at the UQ Casual Academic website and the SMP Casual Academic Staff Opportunities website .   For further information about UQ’s benefits, please visit Why Work at UQ and review  The University of Queensland Enterprise Agreement 2021-2026 (Enterprise Agreement).  Questions?     To discuss this role please contact Dr Sarah Sweet ( s.sweet@uq.edu.au ).  For application queries, please contact  casualrecruit@uq.edu.au stating the job reference number in the subject line.   Want to Apply?     Expressions of Interest can be submitted via the Apply Now link. Please provide your details and complete the questionnaire in full where applicable.  If you are a current employee of the University (including casual staff), or hold a HDR Scholarship with the University, please login to your staff Workday account and visit the internal careers board to apply for this opportunity. Please do NOT apply via the external job board.     The submission of your EOI is due no later than 11.59 pm on Sunday 15th November 2026 .   Tips    If you have not previously applied for casual academic work in Physics, you will be required to upload a short video (2 min max) of you explaining your choice of topic from PHYS1171/1001/1002 (to YouTube or some other site) and paste the link in the application form. You might like to imagine that a student has asked you to clarify a question they are struggling with, or perhaps explain a new concept to them.  New Casual Academics should request 3-5 hours of paid work per week. This includes the 1-3 hours contact and preparation/marking/meeting time for one applied or practical laboratory respectively.  International students should ensure they have a visa that permits this type of casual employment before applying for casual academic work.  If you are able and willing to be a casual academic in specialist second or third year, please indicate these in your preferences as well.  Experienced casual academics can also apply for a lead casual academic position.   If you are shortlisted and have not previously applied to be a casual academic in Physics, you will be contacted and need to attend a 5-minute online interview in the week of 23rd November 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Lecturer---Senior-Lecturer-in-Mechanical-Engineering_R-69939-2": {
      "title": "Lecturer / Senior Lecturer in Mechanical Engineering",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-23\nFull time\n\nSchool of Mechanical and Mining Engineering – Faculty of EAIT Full-time (Part-time negotiable ), Permanent position Academic Level B (Lecturer) or C (Senior Lecturer) Level B base salary range $119,462.94 - $141,545.09 + 17% super Level C base salary range $145,961.51 - $168,043.68 + 17% super Visa Sponsorship may be available for this appointment Based at our St Lucia Campus - Brisbane   About This Opportunity  Join us as a Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Mechanical Engineering and play an important role in shaping the next generation of engineers. To be successful, you will need to possess a solid foundation in the discipline of Mechanical Engineering. In this role, it will also be important bringing expertise in delivering and coordination of undergraduate and postgraduate courses, supervise Higher Degree by Research students, pursue impactful research, contribute to the School community, and build strong partnerships with industry.   Key responsibilities will include:    Teaching: Coordinate and deliver undergraduate and postgraduate teaching in mechanical engineering, including lectures, tutorials, assessment and supervision. Applicants are encouraged to review the course profiles for MECH2300, MECH2305, MECH2100, MECH2210, MECH3100, MECH3200, MECH3400, MECH3610, MECH3780, AERO4100, AERO4200, AERO4300 and ENGG4552, and identify courses aligned with their teaching expertise.   Research: Develop and deliver high-quality independent and collaborative research, secure external funding, publish in leading academic and professional journals, and contribute to interdisciplinary projects aligned with the School’s strategic priorities. Conduct research ethically using best-practice methodologies, while preparing publications and reports and actively contributing to project meetings and outcomes.   Supervision and Researcher Development: Contribute to the supervision of Honours and Higher Degree by Research students, while supporting staff recruitment, onboarding, performance management, employee development and workplace matters in accordance with University policies and procedures.   Citizenship and Service: Develop strong partnerships with colleagues, industry, government, professional bodies and the wider community to support the School’s strategic objectives, while contributing to School administration, decision-making and committees in alignment with UQ values.   Additional to the duties above, Academic level C will include :  Provide principal supervision to Higher Degree by Research students, lead strategic research collaborations nationally and internationally, secure and manage external funding, and produce high-quality research outputs. Contribute to contemporary teaching programs, School and industry activities, and the translation and commercialisation of research outcomes.   This is a teaching & research position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance .   For further information, please click here to view the information booklet.   About You  Completed PhD in mechanical engineering and a record of research in mechanical engineering. A growing research profile in the field, with work of high quality and the potential for impact. A developing reputation and track record of high-quality publications in reputed refereed journals and presenting at conferences. Evidence of successfully seeking, obtaining, and managing external research funding. A growing record of supervision of undergraduate, postgraduate coursework, and HDR students to successful completion. A growing profile in teaching mechanical engineering content. Evidence of a high level of quality teaching in mechanical engineering at undergraduate and postgraduate level across a variety of settings, including small and large groups. Ability and willingness to teach large mechanical engineering courses. Willingness to coordinate mechanical engineering courses and contribute to the development and continuous innovation of new programs, curriculum design and course material in the discipline area. Experience in meaningful internal service roles in conjunction with active contributions to external activities. Well-developed communication, interpersonal and consultative skills, and the ability to work collaboratively with colleagues in teaching and learning.   Academic Level C (Senior Lecturer) Selection criteria as listed above, in addition to the following:   National recognition in the area of expertise, with an internationally developing profile and an established record of high-quality publications in reputed refereed journals and conference presentations. A solid record of supervision of undergraduate, postgraduate coursework, and HDR students as principal supervisor. A strong track record of coordinating mechanical engineering courses and participating in the development and continuous innovation of new programs, curriculum design and course material in the discipline area. A growing record of meaningful internal service roles that have delivered outcomes in conjunction with a strong contribution to external activities.   We particularly welcome applications from women, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people with disability, members of the LGBTQIA+ community, and others who may be underrepresented in this discipline.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.   The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Professor Ross McAree via hos@mechmining.uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.    When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter ( clearly note if you are applying for Academic Level B or C ) and responses referring to the relevant ‘ Key Responsibilities’ and ‘ About You’ sections. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia and qualifications checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   Relocating from interstate or overseas? We may support you with obtaining employer-sponsored work rights and a relocation support package, however upfront costs will still be expected to be covered. You can find out more about life in Australia’s Sunshine State here .   We are dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We are also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close on Sunday, 22 November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69939). Please note that interviews will likely be scheduled for early December 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Casual-Academics---School-of-Psychology_R-70472": {
      "title": "Casual Academics - School of Psychology",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-23\nCasual\n\nApplications for Tutoring in Semester 1 2027 will close on 18 th January 2027.   Casual Academic staff provide a rich and important contribution to the University’s academic, teaching, outreach and clinical endeavours through the provision of a range of academically focused services and activities. Casual Academic staff may be engaged to fulfil a wide range of duties, including (but not limited to) tutoring; marking; demonstrating; teaching; clinical education; student outreach and engagement; moderating/facilitating and/or student advising; across multiple academic disciplines.  Casual Academic staff are remunerated according to the category of their duties, as detailed in UQ’s Enterprise Agreement and reiterated in this job profile.   If you are a current employee of the University, including current casual staff and HDR scholars, please login to your staff Workday account and visit the internal careers board to apply for this opportunity. Please do NOT apply via the external job board. Before applying, please familiarise yourself with the Semester 1 timetable to ensure you will be available to tutor for what has been scheduled.   Please click here for a list of Semester 1 2027 Course Offerings .   Please note that these opportunities are subject to The University’s Code of Conduct .    School of Psychology Requirements   Each course is delivered differently. Please  familiarise yourself with the course  before preferencing.  We strongly recommend reaching out to the Course Coordinator to discuss course details before applying.  If you have skills that you believe are relevant to a particular course (e.g., quantitative/technical skills or in-depth content knowledge) please make note of this when applying.  All new casual academics are required to attend a casual academic orientation on Friday 12th February from 10.00am to 12.00pm. New casual academics who do not attend will not be given an appointment.  Casual academics need to be available for at least half of the tutorials on offer for their preferred courses otherwise we may not be able to offer you a position.  To be undertake tutoring you  MUST  be available for the entire semester, from Week 1 through to Grade Release.  New casual academics are also expected to attend Faculty casual academics Training, this comprises a number of sessions which will take place in O Week. More information will be provided following the release of appointments.  Course Ranking and Allocation   We use a rating system to assist us in allocating casual academics to courses. When rating the courses that are on offer, please remember that the more courses you give a rating to (1 to 4 stars) the greater your chance of being allocated to a course.   4 – I would love to teach this subject; 3 – I would be happy to teach this subject; 2 – I would not mind teaching this subject; 1 – I would rather not teach this subject; 0 – I would rather not tutor than teach this subject.  The minimum allocation load for most courses is 2 tutorials. Tutorials are either 1 or 2 hours long.   Casual academics will be paid as per the casual academic salary scale via a predetermined schedule. Payments will vary week to week depending on the course schedule.  What We Can Offer  These are casual appointments. As such, there is no firm advance commitment to ongoing or indefinite work. The casual rate will vary as per the work completed. Please click here to view our various casual academic pay scales.  For further information about UQ’s benefits, please visit Why Work at UQ and review  Operational UQ Enterprise Agreement_2021-2026.pdf   Questions?  For application queries, please contact casualrecruit@uq.edu.au stating the job reference number in the subject line.  How to Apply All applicants must supply the following documents:    Resume    To satisfy pre-requisite questions and ensure your application can be considered in full, all candidates must apply via the UQ Careers portal by the job closing deadline. Applications received via other channels, including direct email, will not be accepted.     Other Information    You must have work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.    If you are based within our psychology clinics, please note that you will be required to provide evidence against certain vaccine preventable diseases, which may include the COVID-19 vaccine.     At UQ we know that our greatest strengths come from our diverse mix of colleagues, this is reflected in our ongoing commitment to creating an environment focused on equity, diversity and inclusion .  We ensure that we are always attracting, retaining and promoting colleagues who are representative of the diversity in the broader community, whether that be  gender identity ,  LGBTQIA+ ,  cultural and/or linguistic ,  Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander peoples , or  people with a disability . Accessibility requirements and/or adjustments can be directed to  recruitment@uq.edu.au"
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Casual-Academic---UQ-School-of-Agriculture-and-Food-Sustainability_R-70438": {
      "title": "Casual Academic - UQ School of Agriculture and Food Sustainability",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-23\nCasual\n\nSchool of Agriculture and Food Sustainability Casual Position     Applications Close on 04 December 2026 at 11:59PM AEST     About this opportunity  The successful appointees will support the Course Coordinator in the delivery of course aims and learning outcomes, in first year, second year, third year and postgraduate courses in the School of Agriculture and Food Sustainability.      Casual academic positions are based at both the Gatton and St Lucia Campuses and successful appointees must be available to attend on campus.      Casual Academics are remunerated according to the category of their duties, as detailed in The University of Queensland Enterprise Agreement 2021-2026 (Enterprise Agreement) a nd reiterated in this job profile.         Responsibilities as a Casual Academic may include (but are not limited to):    Demonstrator support for the conduct of practical classes, demonstrations, student field excursions  Marking  Exam invigilation  Preparation time  facilitation of a class activity not requiring the development of materials  consultation with students  attendance at induction sessions  undertaking of training as required  moderating a discussion forum, being available for student consultation online or in person, being available for consultation/ assistance on a specific activity such as a simulation and where no preparation or follow-up is required outside of paid time; and  attendance at, or involvement in, other academic activities as directed by their Supervisor.     Compliance    All Casual academics are required to comply with legislation and University policy relevant to the duties undertaken, including but not exclusive to:    The University’s Code of Conduct .   Requirements of the Queensland occupational health and safety (OH&S) legislation and related OH&S responsibilities and procedures developed by the University or Institute/School.   The adoption of sustainable practices in all work activities and compliance with associated legislation and related University sustainability responsibilities and procedures .   Requirements of the Education Services for Overseas Students Act 2000, the National Code 2007 and associated legislation, and related responsibilities and procedures developed by the University.       About the School of Agriculture and Food Sustainability      The School of Agriculture and Food Sustainability brings together expertise in agriculture, agribusiness, plant and crop sciences, animal science, soil science, food science, and international and rural development.    While the School itself is new, it draws on a rich history from predecessor Schools — the School of Agriculture and Food Sciences, the School of Biological Sciences and the School of Earth and Environmental Sciences.    Officially established on 30 June 2023, the School brings into sharper focus the need to address the  United Nations Sustainable Development Goals  relating to zero hunger, life on land and life under water.    Together, our work will help address complex and unrelenting global issues such as climate change, food and water insecurity, and biosecurity.    With world class teaching and research facilities, the School provides a flagship learning environment for addressing food security and sustainability on a global scale, while training and graduating a workforce capable of tackling these challenges. Academic support staff play a vital role in teaching and learning within the School of Agriculture and Food Sustainability (AGFS), assisting teaching staff in the delivery of undergraduate and postgraduate courses.    About You    We welcome expressions of interest from research assistants, postdoctoral fellows, honours, undergraduate/postgraduate and Higher Degree Research students, and applicants who have previously taught in their field at a significant level who want to return to the workforce.    What we can offer      This is a casual position. As such, there is no firm advance commitment to ongoing or indefinite work. The casual hourly rate can be viewed here .   Most casual academics rates will be within the AC30 and AC50 pay scales.    For further information about UQ’s benefits, please visit  Why Work at UQ  and review Operational UQ Enterprise Agreement_2021-2026.pdf .     Questions?      To discuss this role please contact AGFS CAHP team: agfs.cahp@uq.edu.au    For application queries, please contact casualrecruit@uq.edu.au stating the job reference number in the subject line.        Want to apply?      All applicants must provide the following documents:     Resume      To ensure your application is considered, you must apply via the UQ Careers portal by the job closing deadline. Applications received via other channels, including direct email, will not be accepted.     If you are a current employee of the University, including current casual staff and HDR scholars, please login to your staff Workday account and visit the internal careers board to apply for this opportunity. Please do NOT apply via the external job board."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Gatton-Campus/Casual-Animal-Attendant_R-70323-1": {
      "title": "Casual Animal Attendant",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-23\nCasual\n\nFaculty of Science . Casual position with an hourly rate in the range of $41 to $43 per hour, plus superannuation of 12% (HEW Level 1). Weekend and public holiday shifts are required. Join our Gatton Campus community and take a virtual tour .   About This Opportunity We are seeking a reliable and motivated Casual Farm Animal Attendant to support the care and welfare of animals at UQ’s Gatton Campus. This entry-level opportunity offers on-the-job training within a purposeful and collaborative rural environment, providing a practical starting point for a career in animal attendance, farm operations or veterinary assistance.   Key responsibilities will include, but not limited to: Perform milking and calf feeding and assist with feeding dairy stock. Maintain safe, clean, hygienic and secure animal facilities, stalls and work areas. Assist with the collection, loading, restraint and movement of animals and livestock. Undertake basic farmhand duties, grounds and facility maintenance, and driving of University vehicles as required.   About You You will bring a practical, safety-conscious approach and genuine care for production animals. You will also demonstrate: Completion of Year 10. A current Queensland open driver licence, Class C. The physical capacity to safely perform the inherent requirements of the role. The ability to work effectively and collaboratively within a team environment. The ability to communicate respectfully with staff, students, clients and external suppliers. An understanding of the importance of providing excellent client service. Care, patience and compassion when working with production animals. Availability to work weekend and public holiday shifts. Basic computer skills and previous experience within a production animal system will be highly regarded.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world.   Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available, such as access to exclusive internal-only vacancies and free parking at the Gatton Campus.   UQ’s commitment to agricultural and food innovation extends from practical animal care to emerging dairy technologies. Discover how UQ dairy technology is moving from the laboratory to industry , supporting new collaboration and innovation across the dairy sector.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Dustin Dowrick at d.dowrick@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns with the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, mandatory immunisations. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Sunday 22 November at 11.00pm AEST (R-70323).   Please note applications will be reviewed as received. Candidates may be interviewed before the job closing date. We encourage candidates to apply as soon as possible. The University of Queensland reserves the right to close this application process early."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Associate-Director--Global-Student-Insights-and-Intelligence_R-70445-1": {
      "title": "Associate Director, Global Student Insights and Intelligence",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nFull time\n\nFuture Students and Admissions Full-time (100%) fixed-term position for an initial period of up to 5 years   Based at our picturesque St Lucia Campus   About This Opportunity  The University of Queensland is seeking an exceptional strategic leader to join Future Students and Admissions as Associate Director, Global Student Insights and Intelligence. Reporting to the Director, Future Students and Admissions, this senior leadership role is responsible for leading the University's student insights and intelligence capability and providing strategic advice on student demand, market conditions, recruitment performance, forecasting and emerging opportunities and risks.   Working across the University, you will translate complex data and market intelligence into actionable recommendations that inform recruitment strategy, enrolment planning, scholarship investment and long-term growth objectives. You will also lead a specialist team and partner with senior stakeholders to strengthen evidence-based decision-making and support UQ's strategic priorities in a rapidly evolving global higher education environment    For more information about this opportunity, please view the appointment booklet HERE   About You You are an accomplished leader with a strong track record of using data, intelligence and strategic insight to inform high-impact decision-making within a complex organisational environment. You bring significant experience providing executive-level advice, translating complex evidence into practical recommendations, and influencing senior stakeholders on matters of strategic importance. You will possess advanced analytical capability, experience leading forecasting and intelligence functions, and a demonstrated ability to navigate evolving policy, regulatory and market environments. Equally important is your ability to build trusted relationships across diverse stakeholder groups, lead and develop high-performing teams, and drive continuous improvement in analytical practices, reporting and governance frameworks. Experience within the higher education sector and expertise in market intelligence, student demand analysis, planning or business intelligence will be highly regarded.    About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.   Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, affordable parking, and salary packaging.   Interested? If you’d like to have a confidential discussion, please contact Maddy Flaherty (Principal Consultant, Executive Recruitment) via executiverecruitment@uq.edu.au . When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity.   We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing executiverecruitment@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3443 6272.   Applications close Sunday 8th November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-70445)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Herston-Campus/Research-Fellow---Indigenous-Oral-Health_R-70102-1": {
      "title": "Research Fellow – Indigenous Oral Health",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nPart time\n\nSchool of Dentistry Part-time (40%), fixed-term position for up to 2 years Base salary will be in the range $119,463 – $141,545 + 17% super (Academic Level B) Based at our Herston campus About This Opportunity This is an exciting opportunity to contribute to a collaborative and impactful program of Indigenous oral health research through the Deadly Dental Home Project. Working alongside Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities, health service partners and multidisciplinary researchers, you will use mixed-methods, co-design and implementation research to generate evidence that informs policy, strengthens services and improves oral health outcomes. You will join a purposeful and inclusive research environment where community partnerships, culturally safe practice and meaningful translation are central to creating change together. Key responsibilities will include: Research: Contribute to the design, implementation and evaluation of the Deadly Dental Home Project, including qualitative, quantitative and implementation research, data collection and analysis, co-design workshops, stakeholder engagement, reporting, publication and the translation of findings into practical recommendations. Research governance and partnerships: Navigate data governance, data access and data-sharing processes in collaboration with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities, health services, organisational partners and researchers, ensuring compliance with ethical, legal and culturally safe research practices. Supervision and Researcher Development: Supervise Honours and Higher Degree by Research students, support the development of researcher capability and skills, and facilitate meaningful engagement opportunities for supervisees. Citizenship and Service: Demonstrate UQ values through mentoring, collaboration and active participation in unit priorities, while developing partnerships with communities, government, industry, professional bodies and the wider community.   This is a research focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance .   About You Completion of a PhD in dental public health or health services research. A growing research profile in a relevant discipline area, supported by high-quality scholarly contributions. Experience in mixed-methods health services research, including qualitative, quantitative and implementation research approaches. Demonstrated capability in qualitative data analysis, with experience using established methods such as thematic analysis. A developing record of publications in reputable peer-reviewed journals and presentations at academic or professional conferences. Evidence of successfully seeking, obtaining and managing external research funding. Demonstrated ability to plan and coordinate research activities, including data collection, ethics documentation, evaluation, reporting and dissemination. Excellent communication and relationship-building skills, with the ability to work respectfully and effectively with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities, organisations and stakeholders in culturally safe ways. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development. UQ research is helping demonstrate how population-level approaches can reduce inequalities in children’s oral health. https://news.uq.edu.au/2025-07-fluoride-has-greater-benefits-vulnerable-children. The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Dr Nicole Stormon at n.stormon@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia and qualifications. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Thursday 5th November at 11.00pm AEST (R-70102)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Gardener-Groundsperson_R-70139-1": {
      "title": "Gardener/Groundsperson",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nFull time\n\nProperty and Facilities Full-time, permanent position. Base salary will be in the range $70,319 to $77,400 + 17% super (HEW Level 3). Based at our St Lucia Campus . About This Opportunity Make your mark on one of Australia’s most beautiful university campuses. If you’re someone who takes genuine pride in a well-kept garden, a perfectly finished lawn and seeing the results of a good day’s work, this is an opportunity to put your horticultural skills to work in an environment unlike your typical grounds role. As part of UQ’s Grounds team, you’ll help care for the landscaped gardens, lawns and outdoor spaces that make our St Lucia Campus such a distinctive place to study, work and visit. With more than 100 hectares of grounds and a diverse mix of established gardens, heritage landscapes, sporting areas and contemporary campus spaces, there is plenty of variety to keep the work interesting. You’ll take responsibility for a designated area of campus, giving you the opportunity to really know your patch, take ownership of its presentation and see the impact of your work over time . One day might involve turf maintenance and garden care, while the next could see you tackling irrigation repairs, rejuvenating a garden bed or landscaping around a new development. This is a practical, hands-on role for someone who enjoys working outdoors, being part of a supportive team and putting their horticultural knowledge into practice. Most importantly, you’ll be able to see the results of your work every day in the spaces enjoyed by thousands of students, staff and visitors across the campus. Key responsibilities will include: Maintain gardens, lawns, pathways and surrounding areas to a consistently high standard through mowing, edging, brush cutting, weeding, fertilising, watering, pruning and general grounds care. Restore and enhance outdoor spaces by repairing turf, aerating and top dressing lawns, controlling pests, refurbishing gardens and undertaking landscaping around new and existing buildings. Install, replace and repair irrigation components, drainage, mulch, tree guards and other landscape infrastructure. Maintain a safe work environment by caring for grounds maintenance equipment, completing required records, contributing to risk assessments and following safe operating procedures. About You You will take pride in creating high-quality outdoor environments and enjoy contributing to a supportive team whose work is visible across the campus every day. You will bring: Qualifications and training equivalent to a Certificate III in Horticulture, or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and education or training. A current open Queensland Driver Licence. A current commercial operator’s licence for chemical application, together with knowledge of equipment calibration and the safe application of chemicals. Substantial experience in maintenance gardening, with a genuine interest in plant identification, plant health and landscape presentation. Practical experience maintaining, fault-finding and repairing grounds maintenance equipment. The ability to work safely and productively with minimal supervision while contributing positively to a collaborative team. Strong communication skills and the ability to liaise respectfully with students, staff, contractors, consultants and visitors. Sound judgement, professionalism and the ability to respond constructively in challenging situations. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a purposeful and inclusive community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available. The presentation and care of our campuses play an important role in creating an environment where people can connect, learn and thrive. At St Lucia, you’ll work across a unique campus landscape where established gardens, open green spaces and contemporary facilities come together along the Brisbane River. As part of the Grounds team, your work will directly contribute to maintaining these spaces for the thousands of students, staff and visitors who enjoy the campus every day. Read about UQ research helping apartment dwellers grow fresh produce within their homes . Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible start and finish times where operationally suitable, purchased leave, a condensed fortnight, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on-campus childcare, affordable parking and salary packaging. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Michael Gower, Supervisor, Grounds, at m.gower1@uq.edu.au . For application enquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns with the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, professional registration, criminal history checks, mandatory immunisations, First Aid and/or CPR certification, and Blue Card checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We’re dedicated to equity, diversity and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Sunday 8th November at 11.00pm AEST (R-70139)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Royal-Brisbane--Womens-Hospital---Herston/Senior-Lecturer---Principal-Specialty-Supervisor--Medicine-_R-69369": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer & Principal Specialty Supervisor (Medicine)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nPart time\n\nGreater Brisbane Clinical School Part-time (0.2FTE), fixed-term position until October 2028   Base salary will be in the range $145,961- $168,043 + 17% super (Academic Level C)  Based at the Royal Brisbane & Women’s Hospital - Herston   About This Opportunity    Join a visionary and collaborative academic community dedicated to shaping the next generation of medical practitioners. As Principal Specialty Supervisor, you will play a pivotal leadership role within UQ’s Doctor of Medicine program, providing specialty-specific educational leadership across clinical learning environments while fostering excellence in teaching, mentorship, and clinical engagement. Working closely with the Mayne Academy, MD Curriculum & Assessment team, and a network of hospital and community-based clinicians, you will help create meaningful learning experiences that inspire, challenge and support future doctors to thrive in an increasingly complex healthcare landscape. This is an exceptional opportunity for an accomplished clinician-educator to influence medical education at scale while contributing to a culture of collaboration, innovation and positive change.  Key responsibilities will include:     Teaching: Provide specialty-specific leadership in clinical education across the MD program, deliver high-quality learning experiences and assessments, support clinical supervisors and colleagues, mentor students, act as an educational advisor, and contribute to curriculum enhancement and continuous improvement of teaching and assessment practices. Research: Maintain a strong clinical research profile through high-quality publications and scholarly outputs, contribute to competitive research funding applications, lead or support research projects, and apply best-practice research methodologies to advance clinical knowledge and practice. Supervision and Researcher Development: Supervise Higher Degree by Research students and specialty trainees, foster researcher capability and professional growth, provide coaching and performance feedback, lead responsible research practices, facilitate engagement opportunities, and effectively manage staff throughout the employee lifecycle. Citizenship and Service: Demonstrate leadership through mentoring and collaboration, support staff wellbeing, contribute to committees and administrative activities, build strategic partnerships with healthcare, industry and professional stakeholders, strengthen community engagement, and uphold UQ’s values and standards.   This is a teaching focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s  Criteria for Academic Performance .    About You    Medical qualification with eligibility for registration as a medical practitioner in Australia and a specialist qualification recognised by an Australian specialist college. PhD or equivalent in a relevant discipline, or an equivalent combination of specialist clinical expertise, academic achievement and professional standing. Demonstrated excellence in clinical teaching, with evidence of significant contributions to undergraduate, postgraduate and/or professional-entry medical education. Established expertise within your specialty area, including experience leading innovation, educational improvement and the adoption of contemporary clinical practices. Strong track record of scholarly activity and/or clinical research, including publications, conference presentations and engagement with evidence-based practice. Proven ability to mentor and supervise students, trainees and clinical colleagues, with a collaborative approach to leadership, stakeholder engagement and building professional partnerships.   About UQ   As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.   The greater  benefits  of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a  Group of Eight  university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process.     Interested?   For more information about this opportunity, please contact Associate Professor Carl Lisec at c.lisec@uq.edu.au For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at  talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a  resume ,  cover letter , and  responses to the ‘About You’ section.        Other Information    Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, professional registration (e.g. AHPRA), criminal history checks and mandatory immunisations. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.    We're dedicated to  equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing  talent@uq.edu.au  or calling +61 7 3365 2623.    Applications close 4th of November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69369).  Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for the 9th of November 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Gatton-Campus/Urgent-Care-Veterinary-Nurse_R-69969-1": {
      "title": "Urgent Care Veterinary Nurse",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nFull time\n\nUQ VETS . Full-time, permanent position. Base salary will be in the range $79,096 to $83,863 + 17% super (HEW Level 4). Join our Gatton Campus community and take a virtual tour .   About This Opportunity Join the 24/7 UQ VETS Small Animal Hospital as an Urgent Care Veterinary Nurse, providing skilful, compassionate nursing support to injured animals across emergency, intensive care, isolation and hospital wards. Working alongside veterinarians, veterinary specialists, support staff and students, you will contribute to a collaborative clinical environment where excellent patient care, purposeful teamwork and the education of future veterinary professionals come together.   Key responsibilities will include, but not limited to: Assess, monitor and treat patients in intensive care, isolation and hospital wards, maintaining excellent standards of nursing care and animal husbandry. Support veterinarians and veterinary specialists during emergency procedures, diagnostic clinical pathology and advanced diagnostic imaging, including radiography, computed tomography, scintigraphy and magnetic resonance imaging. Care for, clean and maintain technical and specialised instruments and equipment, while following and contributing to the review of nursing standard operating procedures. Provide clinical teaching support for veterinary and veterinary technology students, deliver a high level of service to clients and support the hospital’s clinical administration, reception, pharmacy inventory and practice management requirements.   As a 24-hour operation, the roster consists of rotating morning, afternoon and evening shifts that provide exposure to a broad range of cases and opportunities to develop your skills across multiple specialty areas. Your take-home salary will be dependent on your specific roster, including loadings of 50% for weekend shifts, 25% for night shifts, and 15% for afternoon shifts.   About UQ VETS A career at UQ VETS means making a difference to not only the lives of the people and animals in your care, but also the next generation of vets and vet technicians. At UQ VETS, we offer the opportunity to build a dynamic career through structured development programs and access to industry leaders.   Instead of solely concentrating on meeting caseload benchmarks or revenue targets, your priorities lie in creating an impact, establishing a lasting impression on the field, and conducting work that brings about positive changes in the lives of animals.   The Veterinary Teaching Hospital is an organisational unit within the School that includes all of the School’s outstanding clinical facilities. UQ VETS Equine Hospital is largely referral-based hospital with advanced facilities and offers a 24-hour emergency medical and surgical service. UQ VETS Small Animal Hospital boasts the latest in veterinary medical and surgical diagnostic and treatment options for small animals, birds, exotic pets and wildlife, as well as a 7-day urgent and emergency Care service. UQ staff also supervise clinical placements at the RSPCA’s Brisbane Animal Care Campus, Wacol.    In particular, the UQ VETS Small Animal Hospital values every animal and offers unrivalled medical, surgical and diagnostic services for dogs, cats, birds, reptiles, small mammals and fish at our world-class veterinary hospital in Gatton. You can take a virtual tour of our Small Animal Hospital here .   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a collaborative and inclusive community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available, such as:   Free parking and an intercampus bus . On-campus childcare , a medical clinic and a post office. Up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave or paid primary carer’s leave. 17% super contributions and 17.5% annual leave loading. Health and wellness discounts, including Fitness Passport access, free annual flu vaccinations, discounted health insurance and access to our Employee Assistance Program for staff and their immediate family. Career development opportunities, including access to exclusive internal-only vacancies and our Study for Staff program. A 25% discount on eligible UQ VETS services. Access to our $4.1 million UQ Sport facility .   About You A Certificate IV in Veterinary Nursing, Bachelor of Applied Science in Veterinary Technology or Bachelor of Veterinary Technology, together with at least two years of veterinary nursing experience, or an equivalent combination of relevant education, training and experience. Demonstrated ability to deliver and maintain high standards of professional veterinary nursing, including the assessment, monitoring and care of hospitalised and critically ill patients. Strong organisational skills and the ability to use initiative, exercise appropriate autonomy and work effectively in a fast-paced clinical environment. Effective written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to build professional and operational relationships with colleagues, clients and students. Knowledge of the ethical requirements, principles and practices relevant to veterinary nursing, together with relevant workplace health and safety, equity and anti-discrimination obligations. A collaborative approach and demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships within a multidisciplinary team. A commitment to ongoing professional development and excellence in veterinary nursing, with an interest in supporting the clinical education of veterinary and veterinary technology students. A current radiation licence, or the ability to apply for one, and the flexibility to participate in a rotating roster that includes evenings, nights, weekends and public holidays.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Sophie Alonso at s.alonso@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns with the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia and mandatory immunisations. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   We’re dedicated to equity, diversity and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Sunday 15 November at 11.00pm AEST (R-69969)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Employability-Program-Advisor_R-70084-1": {
      "title": "Senior Employability Program Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nFull time\n\nFaculty of Engineering, Architecture and Information Technology (EAIT) Full-time (100%), Permanent position Base salary will be in the range $97,268.85 - $104,348.98 + 17% super (HEW Level 6) Based at our St Lucia Campus - Brisbane   About This Opportunity  Make a meaningful impact on the student experience as a Senior Employability Program Officer within the EAIT Employability team. In this highly student-facing role, you will bring innovative employability programs to life, creating valuable opportunities for students to connect with industry and alumni, explore future career pathways and gain exposure to the world of work.   You will be part of a collaborative team of 7 colleagues and will oversee the planning, coordination and delivery of a diverse suite of extra-curricular programs, ensuring an engaging and high-quality participant experience. Through effective program management, stakeholder engagement and data-informed evaluation, you will help continually enhance our programs and strengthen employability outcomes for students.   Key responsibilities will include:   Employability Program Development and Delivery : Coordinate the end-to-end delivery of EAIT’s co-curricular employability programs, creating opportunities for students to connect to industry and explore career pathways. Programs and activities include on campus panel discussions, networking sessions, Industry Site Visits and the Industry Mentoring Program. Manage program logistics, communications, resources and participant experiences, while collaborating with colleagues, industry partners and alumni to maximise student engagement and program impact. Monitor participation trends and emerging student needs to support the ongoing improvement and development of employability initiatives.   Industry and Stakeholder Engagement: Coordinate involvement of industry partners, alumni and academics in employability programs and activities. Work collaboratively with stakeholders to shape content, identify relevant topics that help students develop professional skills, industry awareness and professional networks.   Information Management : Collect and analyse program participation data, feedback and outcomes to evaluate the effectiveness of employability initiatives, identify opportunities for improvement and support ongoing program development.   Systems and Processes : Contribute to the continuous improvement of team systems and processes by identifying opportunities for increased efficiency, effectiveness, and student experience.   About You   Relevant tertiary qualifications and/or demonstrated experience supporting student engagement programs, ideally in a tertiary education environment. Proven experience coordinating multiple programs or initiatives simultaneously, exercising judgement to manage competing priorities and deliver outcomes to timelines. Strong stakeholder engagement skills, with the ability to collaborate across teams and build effective partnerships. Highly organised, with advanced administrative skills and confidence using digital systems for program delivery, data capture, communications and reporting. Experience planning and delivering events or activities, ensuring smooth logistics and positive participant experiences. Demonstrated ability to analyse program data and feedback to inform service improvements.   We particularly welcome applications from women, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people with disability, members of the LGBTQIA+ community, and others who may be underrepresented in this discipline.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.   Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Madelynn Jenkins via madelynn.jenkins@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.    When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia and Blue Card checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We are dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We are also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close on Sunday, 8 November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-70084)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Principal-Events-Officer_R-70291": {
      "title": "Principal Events Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nFull time\n\nAlumni and Community Events team based in the Office of the Vice President (Advancement and Community Engagement) Full-time (100%), permanent position with a collaborative and professional team Base salary will be in the range $106,709 to $116,199 + 17% super (HEW Level 7) Based at our vibrant and picturesque St Lucia Campus   About This Opportunity  We are seeking an experienced Principal Events Officer to help lead the UQ Events team and deliver the University's most significant and high-profile events. Working alongside another Principal Events Officer and reporting to the Senior Manager, Community Engagement and Events, you will guide a small skilled, professional team of Senior Events Officers and will provide operational leadership contributing to a diverse portfolio of events including graduations, building openings, staff events and award ceremonies.   You will work closely with the Vice-Chancellor's Office to coordinate the attendance of the Chancellor and Vice-Chancellor at events and support the hosting of government representatives, dignitaries and other VIP guests. Success in this role requires strong event management, leadership and protocol expertise, with a focus on delivering exceptional polished, welcoming experiences that enhance engagement and uphold the University's reputation.      Key responsibilities will include but are not limited to:  Plan and oversee strategically significant events that engage key University stakeholders, support institutional objectives and demonstrate UQ’s commitment to excellence and hospitality. Lead and support a collaborative, high-performing team by coordinating workloads and event allocations, providing coaching and guidance, and maintaining a strong focus on service excellence. Provide expert advice on event planning and communications, event formats, Orders of Proceedings, protocol and the hosting of dignitaries, official guests and other distinguished visitors. Build effective relationships with University clients, senior leaders, external suppliers and key stakeholders, while overseeing event documentation, budgets, financial processes, risk controls and continuous improvement initiatives.   About You   You will have: A degree in event management, marketing, public relations or a related discipline plus substantial relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education, training and experience. Demonstrated experience planning and delivering complex events, including budgeting, risk management and stakeholder engagement, with the ability to manage competing priorities in a fast-paced environment while maintaining accuracy and attention to detail. Excellent knowledge of protocol and demonstrated experience supporting VIPs, dignitaries, official guests and distinguished visitors, with a proven ability to exercise sound judgement, tact, diplomacy, discretion and confidentiality. Demonstrated experience providing advice on event formats, Orders of Proceedings and stakeholder experience for high-profile events, creating engaging event experiences that align with event objectives, audience needs and institutional expectations. Demonstrated leadership experience, including supervising, mentoring and developing staff, managing workloads and fostering a collaborative, high-performing team culture. Exceptional relationship management skills, including experience working with senior executives, dignitaries, VIPs, clients, suppliers and external stakeholders. Exceptional written and verbal communication skills, including the preparation, review and quality assurance of event communications, invitations, briefings and materials for senior leaders and VIP stakeholders. Proficiency in Microsoft Office applications. Experience with EventsAir and CRM platforms such as Raiser's Edge or Salesforce is highly regarded.   About Advancement and Community Engagement   Throughout its history, The University of Queensland has benefited from the support of its alumni and community through engagement, volunteering and gifts. We are committed to positioning UQ as a worthy destination for philanthropy, ensuring future generations have access to educational opportunity and that we continue to deliver world leading research and innovation. To learn more about philanthropy at UQ visit  https://alumni.uq.edu.au/giving/ . To learn more about Advancement’s alumni engagement activities, visit  https://alumni.uq.edu.au/ .    About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.   Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Cathy James, Senior Manager Community Engagement and Events - cathy.james@uq.edu.au For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.     When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close 2 November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-70291).   #LI-DNI"
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Rockhampton-Hospital/Senior-Community-Engagement-Officer--Identified-s25-_R-69996-2": {
      "title": "Senior Community Engagement Officer (Identified s25)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-21\nFull time\n\nUQ Poche Centre for Indigenous Health. Full-time or part-time (80% to 100%), fixed-term position for up to 2 years. Base salary will be in the range $97,269 to $104,349 + 17% super (HEW Level 6). This position is open to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander candidates only, as a genuine occupational requirement given the role's focus on building trusted relationships with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities and supporting culturally informed research activities. Based at our Rockhampton location.   Acknowledgement of Country  The University of Queensland (UQ) acknowledges the Traditional Owners and their custodianship of the lands on which UQ operates. We pay our respects to their Ancestors and their descendants, who continue cultural and spiritual connections to Country. We recognise their valuable contributions to Australian and global society.    About This Opportunity The Senior Community Engagement Officer will provide essential communications and community engagement support to the RISE research program, which is applying a precision public health approach to help eliminate curable sexually transmitted infections and improve STI management in Central Queensland. Working within a close-knit and supportive team across Rockhampton, Brisbane and Cairns, you will collaborate with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities, Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Organisations, health services and community partners to strengthen research impact and create purposeful, community-led change. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff and students within the UQ Poche Centre meet monthly to connect, share information and opportunities, and progress work that supports unity, cultural leadership and empowerment. The group helps embed cultural integrity across the Centre’s operations and supports research conducted in accordance with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander ethical research principles. Key responsibilities will include: Coordinate and facilitate the co-design and implementation of interventions with Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Organisations and community coalitions in alignment with the RISE research protocol. Build trusted relationships with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities, community organisations, healthcare workers, research partners and other stakeholders across Central Queensland. Develop accessible written and visual content for websites, brochures, newsletters, social media and other community-facing publications, translating complex research evidence into clear and relevant health messages. Support stakeholder and consumer events, respond to requests for information, and help maintain strategic relationships with organisations involved in Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander health and STI communication. For further information, please click here to view the information booklet. About You The University of Queensland considers that to perform this role, it is essential that the person is an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander person. It is therefore a genuine occupational requirement under section 25 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 (Qld). The successful candidate will be required to provide evidence to confirm that they are an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person. Demonstrated experience in research translation or stakeholder engagement, together with experience in healthcare delivery or a health research services environment. Successfully delivered communication outcomes across online services, publications and stakeholder engagement activities. The capacity to undertake significant travel between community locations, which may involve travel for two to three days each week, as well as regular travel to the Brisbane office. A vehicle will be provided for approved community travel. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will work alongside bright minds from around the world, united by a shared commitment to creating a better world. Excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued, and staff are supported through a wide range of benefits, including up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% superannuation, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, salary packaging, and cultural leave.   UQ is committed to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples’ learning, discovery and engagement, and to playing a leading role in reconciliation in Australia. Guided by our Strategic Plan, Stretch Reconciliation Action Plan and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Employment Strategy, we work in partnership with communities to embed reconciliation across our operations. You will be supported to connect with vibrant staff networks, including the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Staff Network, and your cultural identity will be respected, valued and embraced.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Dr Julie-Anne Carroll . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns with the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, criminal history checks and mandatory immunisations. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We’re dedicated to equity, diversity and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Sunday 15th November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69996). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for Wednesday 18th November 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Toowong/Administration-Officer--Identified-s25-_R-69957-2": {
      "title": "Administration Officer (Identified s25)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-21\nPart time\n\nUQ Poche Centre for Indigenous Health. Part-time (60% to 80%), fixed-term position for up to 2 years. Base salary will be in the range $85,452 to $94,984 + 17% super (HEW Level 5). This position is open to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander candidates only, as a genuine occupational requirement, due to the role's contribution to culturally safe practices and support of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander-led initiatives. Based at our Toowong location   Acknowledgement of Country  The University of Queensland (UQ) acknowledges the Traditional Owners and their custodianship of the lands on which UQ operates. We pay our respects to their Ancestors and their descendants, who continue cultural and spiritual connections to Country. We recognise their valuable contributions to Australian and global society.  About This Opportunity Join the close-knit and supportive team at the UQ Poche Centre for Indigenous Health, where your administrative expertise will help keep purposeful work moving. As the Administration Officer, you will provide responsive office coordination, executive and committee support, financial administration, event assistance and stakeholder service. You will be embedded within the Centre’s operations team and work closely with colleagues who value collaboration, cultural integrity and creating meaningful change together. You'll be part of the Centre's Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Staff and Student Group, which meets monthly to connect, share knowledge and opportunities, strengthen cultural leadership, and contribute to initiatives that support the work of the Centre. Key responsibilities will include:  Provide high-level administrative support and office coordination, acting as an initial point of contact and responding to routine and non-routine enquiries. Coordinate calendars, meetings, committees, travel and office financial processes, including meeting papers, minutes, reimbursements, expenses and travel reconciliations. Support projects, workshops, seminars, conferences and other Centre activities, including documentation, communications, purchasing, hospitality and RSVP coordination. Build productive relationships with internal and external stakeholders while maintaining efficient procedures, information systems and document management processes. For further information, please click here to view the information booklet. About You   The University of Queensland considers that to perform this role, it is essential that the person is an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander person. It is therefore a genuine occupational requirement under section 25 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 (Qld). The successful candidate will be required to provide evidence to confirm that they are an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person. Experience in administration or customer service, or transferable skills gained through study, community, cultural, volunteer or professional experiences. Strong communication and interpersonal skills. Good organisational skills and the ability to manage day-to-day priorities. The ability to work collaboratively and contribute positively to a team environment. A proactive and adaptable approach to work. A commitment to providing high-quality support and service. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will work alongside bright minds from around the world, united by a shared commitment to creating a better world. Excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued, and staff are supported through a wide range of benefits, including up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% superannuation, 17.5% leave loading, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, salary packaging, and cultural leave.   UQ is committed to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples’ learning, discovery and engagement, and to playing a leading role in reconciliation in Australia. Guided by our Strategic Plan, Stretch Reconciliation Action Plan and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Employment Strategy, we work in partnership with communities to embed reconciliation across our operations. You will be supported to connect with vibrant staff networks, including the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Staff Network, and your cultural identity will be respected, valued and embraced.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Heather Young . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.    When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Sunday 8th November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69957). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for Thursday 12th November 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Toowong/Operations-Manager--Identified-s25-_R-69963-1": {
      "title": "Operations Manager (Identified s25)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-21\nPart time\n\nUQ Poche Centre for Indigenous Health Part-time, 80% FTE, fixed-term position through to November 2028 Base salary will be in the range $120,317 to $134,893 + 17% super at HEW Level 8 This position is open to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander candidates only, as a genuine occupational requirement, due to the need for lived cultural knowledge and experience to support the Centre’s work with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities Based at our Toowong location.   Acknowledgement of Country  The University of Queensland (UQ) acknowledges the Traditional Owners and their custodianship of the lands on which UQ operates. We pay our respects to their Ancestors and their descendants, who continue cultural and spiritual connections to Country. We recognise their valuable contributions to Australian and global society.  About This Opportunity Join the UQ Poche Centre for Indigenous Health in a purposeful leadership role supporting work that seeks to transform Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples’ experiences of injustice and inequity in health and wellbeing. As Operations Manager, you will lead the Centre’s day-to-day operations, providing high-level operational, administrative and project support within a collaborative environment that brings together Indigenous and health expertise, community organisations and health providers. Through culturally safe leadership, operational excellence and continuous improvement, you will help create the conditions for the Centre’s people, partnerships and programs to thrive. Key responsibilities will include: Lead the Centre’s day-to-day operations, ensuring responsive, efficient and high-quality administrative and business support aligned with strategic priorities. Develop and continuously improve systems, processes and workflows that promote best-practice service delivery, compliance and operational effectiveness. Foster a culturally safe, respectful and inclusive workplace aligned with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander ways of Knowing, Being and Doing. Coordinate operational planning and delivery across governance, finance, human resources, facilities, events, projects and Higher Degree by Research student administration. About You The University of Queensland considers that to perform this role, it is essential that the person is an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander person. It is therefore a genuine occupational requirement under section 25 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 (Qld). The successful candidate will be required to provide evidence to confirm that they are an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person. You will hold relevant qualifications in business, management, administration or a related discipline, together with substantial relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of education, training and experience. You will have essential experience working collaboratively with and for Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander peoples, communities or organisations. You will demonstrate experience managing operational functions and implementing systems, administrative processes and service improvements within a complex organisation. You will bring experience in financial administration, budget coordination and resource management. You will have experience supporting human resources processes, including recruitment, onboarding and workforce administration. You will demonstrate the ability to promote culturally safe workplace practices and support capability development in culturally responsive ways. You will bring strong project coordination, event management, organisational, communication and stakeholder management skills, together with the initiative to solve problems and manage competing priorities. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will work alongside bright minds from around the world, united by a shared commitment to creating a better world. Excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued, and staff are supported through a wide range of benefits, including up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% superannuation, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, salary packaging, and cultural leave. UQ is committed to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples’ learning, discovery and engagement, and to playing a leading role in reconciliation in Australia. Guided by our Strategic Plan, Stretch Reconciliation Action Plan and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Employment Strategy, we work in partnership with communities to embed reconciliation across our operations. You will be supported to connect with vibrant staff networks, including the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Staff Network, and your cultural identity will be respected, valued and embraced. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Heather Young . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.  When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Sunday 1st November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69963). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for Thursday 5th November. #LIDNI"
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/UQ-Campus---Herston/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow---First-Nations-Health_R-70064-3": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow - First Nations Health",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-21\nFull time\n\nFirst Nations Cancer and Wellbeing Research team within the School of Public Health Part-time (0.8 FTE minimum) or full-time (1.0 FTE), fixed-term position for up to 2 years. Base salary will be in the range $85,373 to $113,660 + 17% super (Academic Level A). Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people are strongly encouraged to apply. Based at our Herston Campus   About This Opportunity This is an exciting opportunity for a highly motivated, research-focused qualitative postdoctoral Research Fellow to join the First Nations Cancer and Wellbeing Research team. Working within a collaborative, supportive and community-driven research environment, you will contribute to innovative research that seeks to achieve equity in cancer outcomes and strengthen wellbeing across the life course for First Nations peoples. Working on an NHMRC–CIHR Healthy Cities Implementation Science Grant project, you will help establish a new research initiative, build meaningful stakeholder relationships, and contribute to research with real-world impact. Key responsibilities will include: Conduct qualitative and participatory research activities, including participant recruitment and retention, interviews, longitudinal data collection, ethics and governance processes, research data management, qualitative analysis, and interpretation of findings to support project outcomes. Prepare research reports, scholarly publications, conference presentations, dissemination materials, and contribute to competitive research funding applications. Strong interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to engage respectfully and effectively with researchers, research partners, services and communities. Proven organisational and project management skills, including the ability to work independently and collaboratively, manage competing priorities and meet project deadlines in a dynamic research environment. This is a research focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance. About You Completion or near completion of a PhD in a relevant discipline, which may include public health, psychology, statistics or another field that supports the objectives of the research program. Demonstrated experience in qualitative research design, participant recruitment, data collection and qualitative data analysis. Experience in participatory, co-design or community-engaged research approaches, or the demonstrated capacity to develop this experience. Evidence of contributing to research outputs, such as peer-reviewed publications, conference presentations, reports or other forms of scholarly dissemination. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development. The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Professor Gail Garvey . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia and qualifications. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Tuesday 10th November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-70064). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for Wednesday 18th November 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow-in-Human-Rights-Law_R-69658-1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow in Human Rights Law",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-20\nPart time\n\nTC Beirne School of Law within the Business, Economics and Law Faculty . Part-time (40%), fixed-term position until May 2029. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment Base salary will be in the range $85,373 to $113,660 + 17% super (Academic Level A). Based at our St Lucia campus in Brisbane.   About This Opportunity Join a visionary international research project examining how human rights agencies navigate some of the most consequential questions facing contemporary societies. Working within a collaborative and inclusive team of academic experts, research assistants and project leaders, you will contribute to the Benchmarking Human Rights Agencies: How Should They Balance Freedom Rights with Equality Rights? project. Your work will explore how agencies at international, regional, national and subnational levels manage tensions between freedom of speech, association and religion, and the rights to equality and non-discrimination. Alongside building your emerging research profile, you will help coordinate the project, engage with agencies and stakeholders, and translate research into practical benchmarks and implementation guidance.   Key responsibilities will include: Research: Conduct high-quality legal and interdisciplinary research into the frameworks, procedures and methodologies used by human rights agencies to address competing rights claims, producing sole-authored and collaborative publications for reputable outlets. Project leadership and coordination: Support the leadership team to coordinate an international network of researchers, oversee project activities and milestones, and help deliver conferences, workshops, publications and other research outputs. Supervision and Researcher Development: Support and supervise research assistants and, where appropriate, contribute to the supervision or associate supervision of undergraduate and higher degree research students. Citizenship and Service: Build purposeful relationships with human rights agencies, government, professional bodies and other stakeholders, while contributing collaboratively to the School and demonstrating behaviours aligned with UQ values.   This is a research focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance.   About You Completion, or near completion, of a PhD in law or a closely related discipline is preferred. Outstanding recent LLB graduates with strong research skills will also be positively considered. Demonstrated research knowledge in one or more relevant areas, including human rights law, constitutional law, comparative law, competing rights, procedural fairness, statutory bodies or independent commissions. An emerging research profile, demonstrated through publications in reputable refereed journals, conference presentations or other relevant scholarly outputs is desirable. Experience in quantitative or qualitative data management and analysis, or the demonstrated capacity to learn appropriate data management systems and research methods. The capacity to develop project management and leadership skills while supporting milestones, publications, events and the timely delivery of project outcomes. Strong written, interpersonal and organisational skills, with the ability to collaborate effectively across a diverse international research team. The ability to engage professionally and constructively with human rights agencies, researchers, government representatives and other stakeholders. Experience contributing to internal service, external engagement or collaborative activities within a university or comparable research environment is desirable.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.   The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Professor Nicholas Aroney at n.aroney@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia and qualifications. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We’re dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close 2nd November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69658). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for mid November."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Herston-Campus/Senior-Community-Engagement-Officer--Identified-s25-_R-69438-2": {
      "title": "Senior Community Engagement Officer (Identified s25)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-20\nFull time\n\nFirst Nations Cancer and Wellbeing Research team within the School of Public Health Full-time (100%), fixed-term position for up to 2 years Base salary will be in the range $97,269 to $104,349 + 17% super (HEW Level 6). This position is open to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander candidates only, as a genuine occupational requirement due to the role's focus on engaging and working in partnership with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities Based at our Herston Campus   Acknowledgement of Country  The University of Queensland (UQ) acknowledges the Traditional Owners and their custodianship of the lands on which UQ operates. We pay our respects to their Ancestors and their descendants, who continue cultural and spiritual connections to Country. We recognise their valuable contributions to Australian and global society.    About This Opportunity  Join the First Nations Cancer and Wellbeing Research team and contribute to innovative, community-driven research that supports and empowers Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples and communities to achieve optimal health and wellbeing. Working within a purposeful, collaborative and inclusive team, you will provide community engagement and communications support across research projects, build respectful stakeholder relationships and help translate research evidence into meaningful impact. Key responsibilities will include: Build and maintain respectful relationships with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, communities, community organisations, healthcare workers and other stakeholders. Maintain and support the Cancer Ambassador program and contribute to the planning and delivery of stakeholder and consumer events. Work with research teams and external organisations to strengthen strategic research partnerships and engagement Develop communication and knowledge translation strategies, and produce clear and accessible written and visual content for websites, brochures, newsletters, social media and other publications. For further information, please click here to view the information booklet. About You   The University of Queensland considers that to perform this role, it is essential that the person is an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander person. It is therefore a genuine occupational requirement under section 25 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 (Qld). The successful candidate will be required to provide evidence to confirm that they are an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person. Strong interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to build positive relationships and engage respectfully with people from diverse cultural, community and professional backgrounds. Experience working with, or a genuine commitment to engaging with, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, communities and organisations Experience in stakeholder engagement, partnership development or community engagement, ideally within healthcare, research, community, government or a related environment. Well-developed written communication skills, including the ability to translate complex information into clear, accessible and engaging content for a range of audiences. The ability to coordinate activities, manage competing priorities and work collaboratively with colleagues and stakeholders to achieve shared goals. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will work alongside bright minds from around the world, united by a shared commitment to creating a better world. Excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued, and staff are supported through a wide range of benefits, including up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% superannuation, 17.5% leave loading, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, salary packaging, and cultural leave.   UQ is committed to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples’ learning, discovery and engagement, and to playing a leading role in reconciliation in Australia. Guided by our Strategic Plan, Stretch Reconciliation Action Plan and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Employment Strategy, we work in partnership with communities to embed reconciliation across our operations. You will be supported to connect with vibrant staff networks, including the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Staff Network, and your cultural identity will be respected, valued and embraced.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Dr Sara Gollschewski   . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.    When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia,  and criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Tuesday 17 November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69438). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for Wednesday 25 November 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Bundaberg-Hospital/Head-Bundaberg-Regional-Clinical-Unit_R-69805-1": {
      "title": "Head Bundaberg Regional Clinical Unit",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-19\nPart time\n\nRural Clinical School Part-time (0.5FTE), fixed-term position until December 2028   Base salary will be in the range $145,961 - $193,070 + 17% super (Academic Level C to D)  Based at the Bundaberg Hospital     About This Opportunity    Play a pivotal role in shaping the future of rural medical education and strengthening the medical workforce across regional and rural Queensland. As Head, Bundaberg Regional Clinical Unit , you will lead the delivery of UQ’s end-to-end Doctor of Medicine (MD) program in Bundaberg, providing strategic academic and operational leadership within the Rural Clinical School’s Wide Bay Learning Community. At an exciting time of growth for Wide Bay and rural Queensland, you will join an enthusiastic and supportive team of clinicians, academics, professional staff and healthcare partners. Together, you will create an exceptional learning environment where future doctors can thrive, develop their professional identity and build strong connections with regional medicine. This is a unique opportunity for an experienced GP or non-GP Specialist to shape a growing regional medical pathway, strengthen clinical and community partnerships, and inspire the next generation of doctors to build rewarding careers in rural and regional Queensland. Key responsibilities will include:    Teaching: Provide academic leadership for the MD program within the Bundaberg Regional Clinical Unit, foster excellence in teaching and learning, contribute to curriculum delivery and assessment, and support students through academic guidance, career advice and pastoral care. Research: Maintain academic standing through scholarship and research activities, foster a positive research culture, and support collaborative research initiatives that strengthen the Rural Clinical School and regional medical pathway. Supervision and Researcher Development: Mentor and develop students and emerging clinicians, encourage the responsible conduct of research, and support professional growth through coaching, feedback and scholarly engagement. Citizenship and Service: Build strong partnerships with healthcare providers, general practice and community stakeholders, contribute to the governance and leadership of the Rural Clinical School, champion regional medical careers, and promote a culture of collaboration, wellbeing and excellence.   This is a teaching focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s  Criteria for Academic Performance .   For further information, please click here to view the information booklet.     About You    You will be an experienced clinician with a passion for rural and regional medicine and an interest in extending your impact through medical leadership, education and academia. This opportunity will particularly appeal to someone seeking a rewarding portfolio career that combines clinical practice in the local community with academic leadership. Whether you bring an established research profile or are looking to build your research capability, you will join an enthusiastic and supportive Rural Clinical School community committed to helping medical education, research and regional healthcare thrive. You will also have: Medical qualification with Specialist Registration (GP or non-GP Specialist) and eligibility for registration as a medical practitioner in Australia with AHPRA. Demonstrated experience in clinical teaching and a strong commitment to delivering high-quality medical education in undergraduate, postgraduate and/or professional settings. Proven ability to provide academic leadership, build collaborative relationships, and work effectively across complex healthcare and university environments. Experience in, or a commitment to, scholarship and research, with the ability to foster a positive culture of academic enquiry and continuous improvement. Exceptional communication and interpersonal skills, with the ability to engage and influence students, clinicians, academic colleagues, health service partners and community stakeholders. A genuine commitment to rural and regional health, with an understanding of the opportunities and challenges associated with living, learning and working in regional communities. About the Rural Clinical School Located within the Medical School at The University of Queensland, the Rural Clinical School (RCS) is the largest rural clinical school hosted by a single university. It works to foster and sustain a skilled and dynamic medical workforce to meet the needs of rural, regional, and remote Queensland. The Rural Clinical School provides students with rigorous clinical training built upon hands-on experiences in hospitals and community-based practices. Above all, we give our students the unique opportunity to live and work in some of Queensland’s amazing rural, regional, and remote locations. Our classrooms are the public and private hospitals, specialist consulting rooms, general practices and community healthcare settings throughout rural, regional and remote Queensland. Our teachers are dedicated and passionate rural clinicians. Our students get the opportunity to learn in a range of clinical contexts across the public, private and community sectors. The greater  benefits  of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a  Group of Eight  university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process.   Interested?   For more information about this opportunity, please contact Professor Riitta Partanen | Director, Rural Clinical School at r.partanen@uq.edu.au For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at  talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a  resume  and  cover letter  summarizing how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section.  Other Information    Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, professional registration (e.g. AHPRA), criminal history checks and mandatory immunisations. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to  equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing  talent@uq.edu.au  or calling +61 7 3365 2623.    Applications close Friday 8 January 2027 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69805).  Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for late January 2027."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Research-Fellow---Communication-and-Hearing-Health-Research_R-68796": {
      "title": "Research Fellow - Communication and Hearing Health Research",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-19\nPart time\n\nSchool of Health and Rehabilitation Sciences Part-time (80% FTE), fixed term position for ~4.5 years (through to May 2031) Base salary (at 1FTE) will range from $119,462 - $141,545 (Academic Level B) + 17% Super Based at our vibrant St Lucia campus   About This Opportunity Join a collaborative, inclusive and internationally recognised research environment focused on improving outcomes for individuals with communication disability. As a Research Fellow , you will contribute to an established program of impactful research spanning hearing health, communication disability, person- and family-centred care, co-design, implementation and evaluation. Working closely with the Head of School and an interdisciplinary network of research collaborators, you will contribute to existing and emerging projects while developing your own complementary research profile, building meaningful partnerships, securing competitive funding, and creating positive change through research translation and impact.   Key responsibilities will include: Research: Develop an independent and/or team research program in hearing health and communication disability research, publish high-quality research outputs, contribute to competitive funding applications, and progress research translation and impact. Teaching: Contribute to undergraduate and postgraduate teaching activities where required, maintaining high standards of teaching quality and student outcomes. Supervision and Researcher Development: Supervise Honours and Higher Degree by Research students, support researcher capability development, facilitate engagement opportunities, and provide effective mentoring and supervision. Citizenship and Service: Demonstrate UQ values through collaboration, committee participation, partnership development, and active contribution to School, University and community priorities. This is a research focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance .   About You Completion of a PhD in a relevant discipline, such as speech pathology, audiology or a related field. Demonstrated research expertise in communication disability, hearing health, health services research, implementation science, co-design, and person- and family-centred care, including experience in qualitative, quantitative, and/or mixed-methods research.  Experience conducting research across the lifespan is highly desirable. Evidence of a growing research profile, including designing, coordinating, and delivering research projects, publications in high-quality peer-reviewed journals and presenting at conferences. Strong collaboration, stakeholder engagement and partnership-building skills. Experience contributing to competitive research funding applications and research project delivery. Emerging track record of supervision and mentoring of Honours and/or Higher Degree by Research students. Active contributions to internal and external services roles and activities. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.   The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Professor Nerina Scarinci, Head of School, at n.scarinci@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number R-68796.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter including responses to the ‘About You’ selection criteria above. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information Pre-employment checks will include verification of your right to work in Australia and your qualifications. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion. We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Friday 6 November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (Job Reference Number: R-68796).   #LI-DNI"
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Casual-Academics---School-of-Communication-and-Arts_R-70682": {
      "title": "Casual Academics - School of Communication and Arts",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-29\nCasual\n\nThe University of Queensland Communication and Arts School     The University of Queensland Communication and Arts School is a large, research-intensive unit with an international reputation for outstanding research and teaching in Communication, Strategic Communication, Communication for Social Change, Digital Media, Journalism, Media and Digital Cultures, Professional and Creative Writing and Literature, Drama, Film and Television Studies and Art History. Our academics are widely published internationally and have extensive research backgrounds. The school is based at the St Lucia campus as part of the Faculty of Humanities, Arts and Social Sciences.    About This Expression of Interest     The University of Queensland Communication and Arts School is seeking expressions of interest from casual academics and facilitators to be engaged on a casual basis to teach into our school courses.    Casual Academics at The University of Queensland Communication and Arts School play a significant part in the overall student experience. As such, our successful candidates will participate in the education of undergraduate and/or postgraduate students within the school by preparing and delivering lectures/seminars/tutorials, marking assessment items, conducting student consultations and fostering a positive learning environment. Facilitators are also being sought to assist in activity-based seminars, tutorials and workshops.  These positions are located at our St Lucia campus , renowned as one of Australia’s most attractive university campuses, and located just 7km from Brisbane’s city centre. Bounded by the Brisbane River on three sides, and with outstanding public transport connections, our 114-hectare site provides a perfect work environment – you can enjoy the best of both worlds: a vibrant campus with the tradition of an established university.    Our Ideal Candidate     As a casual academic, you'll have a minimum of an undergraduate degree, with a minimum GPA of 5.0, enrolment in a relevant postgraduate program (coursework or HDR), and/or relevant Industry experience ideally within a business or related discipline. Your skills in teaching will be demonstrable and effective, with teaching evaluations (or other evidence) to support your claims. You'll be proficient in using eLearning systems, such as Blackboard, or are capable of swiftly learning new software. Experience in public speaking and leading small groups will be highly regarded, plus a proven ability to work autonomously and meet deadlines.    You must have ongoing unrestricted work rights in Australia to apply for this opportunity.    We value diversity and inclusion and actively encourage applications from those who bring diversity to the University. Our Diversity and Inclusion webpage contains further information if you require additional support. Accessibility requirements and/or adjustments can be directed to recruitment@uq.edu.au .    What We Can Offer     This is a casual position. As such, there is no firm advance commitment to ongoing or indefinite work. The casual hourly rate can be viewed here .    We invest in the development of our Casual Academics through training sessions, plus a host of other University wide development initiatives.    For further information about UQ’s benefits, please visit Why Work at UQ and review  The University of Queensland Enterprise Agreement 2021-2026 (Enterprise Agreement).    Questions?     If you have any questions regarding this expression of interest, please contact The University of Queensland Communication and Art School's Casual Support team at sca.timetable@uq.edu.au .         Want to Submit an Expression of Interest as a casual academic teaching in our school?     Please hit the APPLY button at the top of the page and follow the prompts. Expressions of interest must be submitted by Tuesday, 1 December 2026 at 11:59pm (AEST) for consideration for semester 1 in 2027. If you submit an expression of interest after this date, you may be considered for vacancies that arise throughout the year or the year following.             If you are a current UQ Employee, unpaid staff member or scholar please login to your staff Workday account and visit the internal careers board to apply for this opportunity via our Internal Job Board.          To satisfy pre-requisite questions and ensure your application can be considered in full, all candidates must apply via the UQ Workday portal . Applications received via other channels including direct email will not be accepted.          Applications close:  Tuesday, 1 December 2026 at 11:59pm (AEST)"
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow---Research-Officer-in-severe-thunderstorm-observations-and-analysis_R-69059": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow / Research Officer in severe thunderstorm observations and analysis",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-28\nFull time\n\nSchool of Civil Engineering – Faculty of EAIT Full-time (100%), fixed-term position through to 11 February 2028 Base salary will be in the range $85,372.89 - $113,659.72 + 17% super (Academic Level A) Visa sponsorship is not available for this position Based at our St Lucia Campus - Brisbane   About This Opportunity  Take your severe weather research into the field as a Postdoctoral Research Fellow / Research Officer specialising in severe thunderstorm observations and analysis. Working alongside researchers from the School of Civil Engineering, School of the Environment and Bureau of Meteorology, you will carry out fieldwork to capture critical measurements using mobile radar and LiDAR systems during severe thunderstorm events across Southeast Queensland. You will also advance techniques for analysing near-surface hail and wind observations during these events, contributing to a deeper understanding of severe storms and their impacts.   Key responsibilities will include:    Research: Contribute to cutting-edge severe thunderstorm research by planning and undertaking fieldwork across Southeast Queensland, operating radar, LiDAR and weather monitoring systems, advancing methods to analyse near-surface wind and hail, and producing high-quality research outputs for publication and industry partners.   Supervision and Researcher Development: Contribute to the supervision and development of Honours and Higher Degree by Research students, providing effective guidance, feedback, coaching and professional development to support their research progress and performance.   Citizenship and Service: Contribute to a collaborative academic environment through School activities, team support and external engagement.   This is a research focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance .   About You  Completion or near completion of a PhD in a discipline of Engineering or Science with a focus on measurement, analysis or modelling of atmospheric flows. The ideal candidate will have focussed on severe weather, but this focus is not essential. Applicants with an MPhil (or equivalent) having undertaken research in these areas will also be considered. Demonstrated competencies in: Analysis of large observational or numerical data sets of atmospheric variables. Mathematical programming and analysis in Python, R or equivalent Desired (but not essential) competencies in: Experience in collection and analysis of observations using radar, LiDAR or surface-based weather stations. Experience undertaking or managing fieldwork campaigns, especially during severe weather. Expertise in the theory and practical implementation of optimisation or inverse problems in earth sciences or geophysics. Experience modelling complex fluid flows (e.g. computational fluid dynamics). Evidence of publications in reputed refereed journals and presenting at conferences.   We particularly welcome applications from women, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people with disability, members of the LGBTQIA+ community, and others who may be underrepresented in this discipline.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.   The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact A/Prof Matthew Mason via matthew.mason@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.    When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia and qualifications, checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We are dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We are also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Wednesday, 11 November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69059)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Research-Project-Officer---Student-Experience---AI-in-Teaching_R-69840-1": {
      "title": "Research Project Officer - Student Experience & AI in Teaching",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-28\nPart time\n\nSchool of Psychology Part-time (60% FTE), fixed-term position through to 31 December 2027 Full-time equivalent base salary will range from $97,268 - $104,348 + 17% Superannuation (HEW Level 6) Based at our vibrant St Lucia campus About This Opportunity We are seeking a highly organised and proactive Research Project Officer to provide high-level administrative and research support for the Student Experience Suite, an innovative teaching and research project funded by a UQ Teaching Innovation Grant and centred on enhancing the student experience. Within this project, a new platform will be piloted with students across nine courses and student-facing units in five faculties. Working collaboratively with academic staff, students and external partners, you will support the project from planning through to evaluation and dissemination, helping turn evidence, diverse perspectives and practical insights into meaningful outcomes.   Key responsibilities will include: Coordinate project planning, implementation, monitoring and evaluation, tracking milestones and deliverables and preparing high-quality project reports. Support project-specific research and analysis, including environmental scanning, data collection and analysis, and contribute to briefs, reports and evidence-informed recommendations. Run the student focus groups and interviews, and act as one of the reviewers in the project’s welfare process on a duty roster, reading student messages flagged for welfare or crisis concerns before they are referred to UQ support services. Build collaborative relationships with students, academic and professional staff and external partners, communicating project information clearly and supporting meaningful stakeholder participation. Provide high-level project and administrative support to the Program Lead and project groups, including correspondence, meetings, committee support, documentation, records and project-related events. About You A degree in psychology, education, social science or a related field, or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and training. Experience supporting human research administration, including consent processes and ethics compliance. Demonstrated project coordination experience spanning planning, implementation, monitoring, reporting and the coordination of activities and deliverables. Strong organisational and prioritisation skills, with the ability to work independently, manage competing demands and meet deadlines in a dynamic project environment. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with experience preparing high-quality reports, meeting documentation and communication materials for varied audiences. Demonstrated ability to develop effective, collaborative working relationships with diverse stakeholders, including students, academic and professional staff and external partners. Strong problem-solving skills, with the initiative, sound judgement and discretion required to handle sensitive personal information appropriately, and the interpersonal skills to work with distressed or reluctant participants. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.   Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on-campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Professor Jason Tangen at j.tangen@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number R-69840.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ selection criteria above. Please note that applications received via email cannot be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks will include verification of your right to work in Australia. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Friday 6 November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (Job Reference Number: R-69840). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for Wednesday 11 November."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Scientific-Officer--Fermentation_R-69816-1": {
      "title": "Scientific Officer, Fermentation",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-29\nFull time\n\nAustralian Institute for Bioengineering and Nanotechnology (AIBN) Full-time (100%), fixed-term position for up 12 months Base salary will be in the range $85,451.97 - $94,983.84 + 17% super (HEW Level 5) Based at our St Lucia Campus About This Opportunity  Join IDEA Bio and work in an experienced and collaborative research team, where you will provide technical research support and specialist expertise across the bioprocess development lifecycle. Primarily laboratory based, you will work closely with a senior fermentation engineer to assist in the implementation of fermentation strategies to better characterised microbial strains and support the development of fermentation workflows, to advance sustainable biomanufacturing.   Key responsibilities will include:   Coordinating bioreactor preparation and operations, including setting up equipment, formulating growth media and using specialist instrumentation for the growth and propagation of microorganisms. Supporting the design and delivery of complex experiments and fermentation strategies for IDEA Bio framework projects and collaborative programs. Troubleshooting, maintaining and supporting the safe, efficient operation of scientific equipment, laboratory facilities, methodologies and protocols. Undertaking literature reviews, preparing reports, graphs, figures and presentations, and supporting client engagement and the day-to-day administration of the facility.   About You   Completion of a degree-level qualification in biotechnology, chemical engineering or biochemical engineering, completion of a relevant diploma with subsequent experience, or an equivalent combination of education, training and experience. Excellent practical research skills and experience applying relevant research methodologies and laboratory techniques. Experience using instrumented bioreactors and advanced bioprocess equipment. Demonstrated experience cultivating microorganisms, including bacteria and yeast, using screening methods, media selection and standard growth kinetics characterisation. Demonstrated laboratory skills, including preparing media, buffers and samples, implementing experimental protocols and using volumetric equipment. The ability to work both collaboratively with colleagues, clients, collaborators and students in an inclusive, multidisciplinary environment, and independently on routine procedures while remaining organised, methodical and self-motivated. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with previous experience in a similar role considered favourably.   About IDEA Bio IDEA Bio has emerged as a state-of-the-art fermentation biofoundry, dedicated to providing high-quality services to both academic and industry partners. Our mission is to develop sustainable bioprocesses that contribute to the growth of Queensland’s bioeconomy and enhance Australia’s position in the global biotechnology landscape. IDEA Bio has fostered a robust network across key nodes funded by the NCRIS BioFoundry program. Our collaborations extend to the BPA Metabolomics and Proteomics Queensland and the Australian Genome Foundry nodes. These partnerships facilitate knowledge exchange, resource sharing, and collaborative projects. We have successfully delivered a robust platform for strain characterization and design through the implementation of analysis, bioprocess characterization, machine learning and artificial intelligence to accelerate cell factory engineering for advanced biomanufacturing.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.   Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Dr Axayacatl Gonzalez at r.gonzalezgarcia@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.     When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Wednesday 4 November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69816).   Please note applications will be reviewed as received. Candidates may be contacted prior to the job closing date. We encourage candidates to apply as soon as possible. The University of Queensland reserves the right to close this application process early."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Senior-Business-Analyst---Data-Governance_R-65385-1": {
      "title": "Senior Business Analyst - Data Governance",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-29\nFull time\n\nInformation Technology Services Full-time (100%), permanent position Base salary will be in the range $120,317 – $134,892 + 17% super (HEW Level 8) Based at our St Lucia campus About This Opportunity Join a collaborative and forward-thinking team where your expertise will help shape the University’s data governance capability. As a Senior Business Analyst within Data Strategy and Governance, you will play a critical role in enabling strong data practices, working with the Solution Architect and diverse stakeholders to develop UQ's conceptual data models and key data governance artefacts. As a Senior Business Analyst, you will have the opportunity to showcase your expertise in identifying and defining business requirements and risks, providing data governance advice and representing business processes and information in models of varying complexity. Key responsibilities will include: Partner with stakeholders across the University to elicit, analyse and define risks and business requirements, providing complex data governance advice, direction or recommendations Prepare key documentation to support business analysis, such as business cases, risk reviews, conceptual data models and issue papers Develop and maintain enterprise data artefacts such as the information entity catalogue and business glossary Facilitate workshops with stakeholders and work with the Solution Architect to develop conceptual data models, define key information entities and business terms, and help identify data-related issues Support the Solution Architect to develop and implement UQ's data architecture framework Drive the successful delivery of data initiatives by managing changes to conceptual data models, business glossary definitions and supporting the development of more detailed models where needed Supervise the Data Governance Officer to meet their operational objectives, ensuring all aspects of human resource management are conducted effectively in accordance with University policies and procedures About You Postgraduate qualifications in Information Technology or a related discipline, or an equivalent combination of education and relevant experience Demonstrated significant experience in business analysis, including requirements elicitation, risk management, analysis, and documentation Proven experience in data governance, information management, enterprise architecture, or data services environments Demonstrated ability to develop business process models, conceptual data models or other enterprise architecture Exceptional analytical and problem-solving skills with the ability to interpret complex information and develop practical solutions Strong interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to influence, negotiate, and collaborate with diverse stakeholders Strong written communication skills with experience producing high-quality reports and artefacts Ability to work independently, manage competing priorities, and deliver outcomes within agreed timeframes Desirable qualifications include : Experience working in a regulated environment with knowledge of Queensland's regulatory environment regarding data, information and records Experience modelling using the ArchiMate modelling language and using architecture or modelling tools (E.g. Avolution, Lean IX, Visio, Erwin) Experience working in the higher education sector or in a large IT department Knowledge of information technology services provided in a large organisation About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available. Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on-campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Ellee McClymont, Senior Manager, Data Strategy & Governance at e.mcclymont@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, and criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion. We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au  or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Thursday 12 November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-65385)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Advanced-Laboratory-Technician_R-70248-1": {
      "title": "Advanced Laboratory Technician",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-29\nFull time\n\nAustralian Institute for Bioengineering and Nanotechnology (AIBN) Full-time (100%), fixed-term position through to December 2027 Base salary will be in the range $97,268.85 - $104,348.98 + 17% super (HEW Level 6) Based at our St Lucia Campus   About This Opportunity  Join the collaborative Chappell research group and support an industry-linked vaccine development program and the broader work of the group. As the Advanced Laboratory Technician, you will provide specialist technical expertise in mammalian cell culture, protein production and aseptic laboratory practice. You will help researchers deliver focused, high-quality work while maintaining the equipment, systems and standards needed for safe, reliable and compliant laboratory operations.   Key responsibilities will include:  Provide technical laboratory support across a range of laboratory methods and equipment, including troubleshooting experimental and equipment issues, maintaining specialist knowledge of laboratory techniques, and assisting with the development and improvement of testing procedures and methodologies. Manage CHO cell culture activities, including coordination with researchers to perform transfections, maintenance of CHO cell line banks, and support for laboratory experiments and reporting. Maintain laboratory operations and compliance through equipment servicing, repair and calibration, quality assurance processes, risk assessments, waste management, housekeeping, stock and chemical inventory control, sample storage, and accurate record management. Support laboratory and field-based research activities by assisting with preparation for external site work and ensuring laboratories are operational, safe, and research-ready. Safety Ensure the safe and compliant operation of laboratory and facility activities by managing occupational health and safety systems, risk assessments and mitigation strategies, inductions, supervision, training records, inventories, and the reporting and investigation of incidents, injuries, and hazards. Monitor and maintain compliance with University, regulatory, and quarantine requirements through laboratory audits, compliance inspections, and the application of safe design principles to laboratory and facility plant, equipment, and systems. Operational and Administration Coordinate laboratory operations and resources, including equipment requests, consumables, inventory management, procurement activities, maintenance scheduling, and contribution to laboratory budget planning for equipment, facilities, and service contracts. Support the effective delivery of laboratory services through liaison with internal and external stakeholders and assistance with the supervision of casual laboratory support staff.   Education In conjunction with discipline-based academic staff, develop and maintain undergraduate laboratory experiments to satisfy learning objectives for undergraduate courses, including: determining requirements for the preparation/conduct of classes; the development and testing of new practicals and participation in tutor training Assist with distribution, handling and administration of teaching related materials such as practical notes and examination papers Assist with preparations for and participate in the outreach activities, information sessions and workshops for schools and other external organizations   About You   You will bring: A degree in microbiology, molecular biology, biotechnology or a related discipline, together with relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of education, training and professional experience. Demonstrated expertise in mammalian cell culture, including CHO cell methodologies and effective aseptic techniques. Strong practical knowledge of molecular biology or vaccine-development laboratory methods and equipment. Advanced capability in cleaning, maintaining, servicing, troubleshooting and calibrating laboratory equipment. Detailed knowledge of laboratory safety requirements and operating procedures, including risk assessment, quarantine, waste disposal, chemical labelling and incident reporting. Experience maintaining laboratory records, sample storage systems, inventories, consumables and high standards of laboratory housekeeping. The ability to analyse technical problems, improve testing procedures and methodologies and communicate findings through clear reports. Strong interpersonal and organisational skills, with the ability to collaborate effectively with researchers, professional staff and internal and external clients.     About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.   Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Prof Keith Chappell - k.chappell@uq.edu.au .   For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.     When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, mandatory immunisations. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close 11 November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-70248)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Senior-Laboratory-Coordinator---Chemical-Engineering_R-69096-1": {
      "title": "Senior Laboratory Coordinator - Chemical Engineering",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-29\nFull time\n\nSchool of Chemical Engineering – Faculty of EAIT Part-time or full-time (60% - 100%), Permanent position Base salary will be in the range $97,268.85 - $104,348.98 + 17% super (HEW Level 6) Visa sponsorship is not available Based at our St Lucia Campus   About This Opportunity  This is an excellent opportunity to contribute to undergraduate and postgraduate coursework laboratory teaching and learning across chemical engineering programs, including chemical, biological, environmental and metallurgical engineering.   Working as part of a team within the School of Chemical Engineering, you will help develop and maintain engineering practice facilities in teaching laboratories and pilot hall. In this role, you will also contribute to practical classes, and hands-on learning experiences that enhance student experience within a School recognised internationally as a leader in chemical engineering, with a strong and collaborative culture.   Key responsibilities will include:   Provide advice on practical teaching capability and investment, aligned with the School’s teaching and learning objectives and actively promote the School’s practical teaching capabilities. Collaborate with academic and course teams to develop, evaluate and improve innovative laboratory experiments, practicals, and learning resources that meet course objectives, enhance student learning and support an engaging experience across undergraduate and postgraduate coursework programs. Liaise proactively with course coordinators to identify and coordinate the overall requirements for laboratory classes, including scheduling, equipment, materials, technical support and resourcing, to support safe, efficient and high-quality practical teaching delivery. Maintain a strong client-service focus by liaising and communicating proactively with academic staff to ensure practical teaching objectives are achieved and continuously improved. Manage the day-to-day operations of practical teaching laboratories, ensuring full compliance with University HSW requirements and maintaining facilities to a high standard of organisation, tidiness and safety. Prepare, set up, pack down and safely store teaching materials, chemicals and equipment; procure and manage consumables and inventories; and coordinate installation, maintenance, servicing and troubleshooting of equipment to ensure reliable and uninterrupted practical teaching. Maintain current knowledge of laboratory methodologies, equipment and practical teaching requirements to support high-quality teaching delivery. Conduct laboratory and facility inductions, maintain accurate induction records, and provide training and instruction to students, tutors and academic staff in the safe and effective use of laboratory equipment, instruments and chemicals. Develop, review and maintain practical teaching documentation—including risk assessments, SOPs and induction records — ensuring all documents are accurate, current and compliant with University and legislative requirements. Contribute to a collegial, collaborative and high-performance culture by engaging in School activities, meetings and communities of practice, and ensuring effective teamwork, role overlap and backup coverage within the practical teaching team. Participate in outreach activities, information sessions and workshops for schools and external organisations as required. Any other duties as reasonably directed by your supervisor.   About You   Tertiary qualifications in science or engineering, a chemical engineering degree would be particularly desirable. Demonstrated experience working in teaching or research laboratories, including chemical preparation, sampling and chemical analysis techniques. Sound knowledge of laboratory equipment, methodologies, experimental facilities and practical teaching processes, including an understanding of pedagogical principles for laboratory-based learning. Demonstrated knowledge and application of laboratory health, safety and compliance requirements, including the ability to conduct risk assessments and instruct students, tutors and academic staff in safe laboratory practices and the use of equipment, chemicals and facilities. Demonstrated ability to explain and demonstrate practical activities and provide clear technical guidance to students, tutors and academic staff to support effective laboratory-based learning. Ability to plan, organise and manage laboratory resources, respond to fluctuating workloads and work both autonomously and under limited supervision. Strong teamwork skills, supported by positive communication and a demonstrated ability to contribute to a constructive and inclusive workplace culture. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to engage constructively with academic staff, students, contractors and other stakeholders. Technical proficiency in digital control systems, data-logging applications and laboratory software, with experience using LabVIEW or the ability to rapidly acquire proficiency. Experience using university systems such as UniFi, UQ Safe and ChemWatch, or the ability to rapidly acquire proficiency. Proficiency in the Microsoft Office suite.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.   Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Melanie Gunn via sm@chemeng.uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.    When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, criminal history checks, mandatory immunisations and Blue Card checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We are dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We are also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close on Thursday 19 November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69096). Interviews will likely be held on either the 7th or 8th of December 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow---Medicinal-Chemist_R-70549-1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow - Medicinal Chemist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-29\nFull time\n\nSchool of Chemistry and Molecular Biosciences Full-time, fixed term position until December 2028 Full-time equivalent base salary will be in the range $85,372 - $113,659 + 17% Superannuation (Academic Level A)  Based at our  St Lucia Campus About This Opportunity   Join a world-class research environment and advance your career in medicinal chemistry, drug discovery and development. This is an exciting opportunity to build your research profile within a collaborative, high-performing team tackling real-world health challenges. Key responsibilities will include:   Research: Develop a coherent research program in medicinal chemistry and drug discovery, publish in high-quality outlets, contribute to competitive funding applications, collaborate on research projects, and support translation and commercialisation of research outcomes. Supervision and Researcher Development: Contribute to the supervision of undergraduate, Honours, Masters and HDR students, support researcher development through coaching and feedback, and assist in managing research staff and resolving issues in line with University policies. Citizenship and Service: Contribute to internal service roles and committees, collaborate with colleagues and external partners, support unit activities, and demonstrate behaviours aligned with University values. This is a research focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s  Criteria for Academic Performance .    About You  Completion or near completion of a PhD in organic/bio-organic chemistry with experience of medicinal chemistry hit-to-lead and/or lead optimisation. Theoretical and practical knowledge of synthetic organic chemistry techniques to synthesise, isolate and characterise small molecules and peptides is essential. Skills in operating and maintaining analytical and purification equipment. Methodical approach to problem solving and a meticulous attitude to laboratory performance, record keeping, electronic data storage and organisation. Excellent communication and organisational skills, including the ability to prioritise own workload, exercise initiative and judgment as appropriate, and adapt to a flexible and dynamic environment with competing priorities to meet deadlines. Ability to work collaboratively and collegially in a multicultural, multidisciplinary team. An emerging profile in research in drug discovery. Evidence of publications in reputed refereed journals and presenting at conferences. Some experience in meaningful internal service roles and contributions towards external activities. Assay development skills would be highly valued. About UQ  As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.  The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process.  Interested?   For more information about this opportunity or application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.  When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section . Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information   Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia and qualifications. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.  Applications close Sunday 15 November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-70549).  Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for 24 November 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Library-IT-Officer_R-70286-1": {
      "title": "Library IT Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-30\nFull time\n\nUQ Library Full-time (100%), permanent position Base salary will be in the range of $85,452 - $94,984 + 17% super (HEW Level 5). Based at our St Lucia location .   About This Opportunity Join UQ Library in a practical, client-focused technology role where your expertise will help staff and students make the most of the digital tools they rely on every day. As a Library Information Technology Officer, you will provide responsive technical support across hardware, UQ systems, services and personal devices while contributing to service improvement and collaborative projects. Working within the student-facing Student Digital Capability Support team you will support students when they experience difficulties with UQ systems and their personal devices by analysing complex scenarios, troubleshooting, identifying problems, taking appropriate actions to resolve, and developing students’ digital skills. Key responsibilities will include: Assess client needs, troubleshoot technical issues and deliver effective solutions, advice and support in person and through remote assistance channels. Investigate complex technology issues, develop and maintain support documentation, and identify process improvements that strengthen the client experience and team efficiency. Contribute to Library initiatives and projects, including evaluating and implementing new technologies and collaborating with teams across UQ. Build digital capability by supporting students, sharing knowledge with colleagues, maintaining accurate service records and continually developing your own professional and technical skills. Identifying process improvements to enhance the client experience and team efficiencies, including supporting training and education sessions for other Library staff. Research and identify optimal solutions for technology issues encountered by students and other Library clients. Liaise with Library Technology Service (LTS), UQ Information Technology Service (ITS) and other stakeholders to resolve student and library client issues.   About You A bachelor’s degree with a significant computing component, or an equivalent combination of relevant experience, education and/or training. Experience providing technical support across several PC operating system environments, such as Windows, Unix/Linux, Macintosh and/or mobile operating systems. Strong interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to engage effectively with clients, colleagues and stakeholders across the University. Demonstrated customer service and people skills in an IT environment, including the ability to remain calm and responsive when working under pressure or managing frequent disruption. Demonstrated problem-solving capability across complex and multi-source technical issues, supported by an organised, methodical approach and strong attention to detail. Demonstrated ability to work independently, collaboratively within a team and as part of projects, with well-developed prioritisation and workload management skills.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available. Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on-campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging. Interested? For more information about this opportunity Please contact Brylee Brazel on b.brazel@library.uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia and criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Sunday 15 November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-70286)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Principal-Research-Technician_R-70193-1": {
      "title": "Principal Research Technician",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-30\nFull time\n\nInstitute for Molecular Bioscience Full-time, fixed-term position for up to 2 years Full-time equivalent base salary will be in the range $106,709.01 - $116,198.52 + 17% super (HEW Level 7) Based at our St Lucia Campus   About This Opportunity  Join a purposeful and collaborative senior research team supporting Professor Yengo’s Snow Fellowship Research Program, where you will apply specialist statistical genetics and bioinformatics expertise to research using large-scale genomic and biobank datasets from genetically diverse populations. As Principal Research Technician, you will develop scalable analysis pipelines, curate complex datasets and provide technical leadership that enables rigorous, high-impact research and helps translate genomic data into meaningful scientific discovery. Key responsibilities will include: Develop, test and apply scalable and generalisable statistical genetics and bioinformatics pipelines for large-scale genomic and biobank datasets. Provide specialist scientific and technical expertise, conduct complex analyses and contribute to the design, implementation and refinement of research methodologies and protocols. Curate research datasets and manage their responsible use, including compliance with Data Access Agreements, Data Transfer Agreements, ethics approvals and research integrity requirements. Provide technical leadership by supervising, training and developing junior research engineers, scientists and technicians, while contributing to research outputs, publications and collaborative initiatives. About You Postgraduate qualifications in Genetics or Genomics, with subsequent experience in statistical genetics and biobank data analysis, or an equivalent combination of relevant education, training and experience. Demonstrated expertise across the research cycle, including data quality control, software benchmarking, scientific analysis, reporting, publication preparation and oral presentation. Specialist expertise in methods for detecting identity-by-descent in biobanks containing relatives, together with methods for detecting and characterising local ancestry. Advanced programming capability in R or another high-level programming language, with experience developing robust bioinformatics or statistical genetics workflows. Advanced technical expertise in genome-wide association studies and downstream applications. Significant experience collaborating with analysis consortia or multidisciplinary teams within the statistical genetics community, including national and international research collaborations. Demonstrated leadership capability, including the ability to supervise, train and develop junior staff and foster a safe, respectful and collaborative research environment. Strong interpersonal and scientific communication skills, together with a commitment to research integrity, ethics, workplace health and safety, openness, respect and integrity.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.   Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Professor Loic Yengo at l.yengo@imb.uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.    When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Sunday November 8th 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-70193)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Coordinator--Research-Centre_R-69903-2": {
      "title": "Coordinator, Research Centre",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-30\nPart time\n\nInstitute for Molecular Bioscience Part-time (29 hours), fixed-term position through to October 2030 Full-time equivalent base salary will be in the range $97,268.85 - $104,348.98 + 17% super (HEW Level 6) Based at our St Lucia Campus   About This Opportunity  Join the NHMRC Centre of Research Excellence in Venom Science and play a pivotal role in enabling a nationally significant, multidisciplinary research program. As Centre Coordinator, you will provide high-level administrative, organisational and communication support, connecting academic, clinical and Indigenous partners across Australia and helping a diverse, collaborative research community translate its work into meaningful health and community impact. Key responsibilities will include: Coordinate the Centre’s day-to-day administration, including support for the Centre Director, Steering Committee and Workstream Leads, meetings, workshops, retreats, advisory board activities, documentation, timelines and follow-up actions. Serve as a central point of contact across investigators, trainees, Indigenous partners and external stakeholders, while supporting newsletters, website content, presentations and other communications that share the Centre’s research, training and engagement activities. Coordinate logistics for training programs, community engagement activities, the Venoms to Drugs conference, travel, workshops, field activities, and student and EMCR mentoring initiatives. Support effective Centre governance through NHMRC and partner reporting, budget tracking, purchasing, reimbursements, financial reporting, and ethics, governance and compliance documentation. About You Completion of a relevant degree in an appropriate discipline area. Experience in pharmacology, biochemistry, biomedical science or a related discipline, together with relevant experience in a research environment, would be highly desirable. Strong organisational and time-management skills, with the ability to coordinate complex activities and balance multiple priorities and deadlines. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to synthesise complex scientific information into research summaries, reports, presentations and content for specialist and broader audiences. Demonstrated understanding of pharmacology and venom science, including venom-derived bioactive molecules and their potential therapeutic applications, with the ability to apply this knowledge to research coordination and communication. Demonstrated experience coordinating scientific meetings, seminar programs, conferences or research training activities, including liaison with researchers, speakers and external stakeholders collaborative and professional approach, with the ability to build productive relationships across multidisciplinary teams, institutions and external partner organisations. Experience supporting research students and early-career researchers, including mentoring and coordinating research activities would be highly desirable. High levels of discretion, initiative and attention to detail, with the ability to maintain accurate records, documentation and project timelines. Familiarity with financial and reporting systems, including supporting budget monitoring, purchasing, reimbursements and reporting activities. A proactive and culturally respectful approach to working with diverse communities and partners, together with a commitment to supporting Indigenous engagement and research with meaningful health impact.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.   Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Professor Irina Vetter at i.vetter@imb.uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.    When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Sunday November 9th 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69903)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/School-of-Social-Science--Casual-Academic-Opportunities_R-70717": {
      "title": "School of Social Science: Casual Academic Opportunities",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-30\nCasual\n\nAbout This Opportunity  The School of Social Science is seeking Casual Academics for Semester 1 2027. Casual Academics make a rich and important contribution to the School’s academic, teaching and research endeavours through the provision of a range of academically focused services and activities. Casual Academics may be engaged to fulfil a wide range of duties, including (but not limited to) lecturing; tutoring; marking; teaching; moderating/facilitating and/or student advising; across multiple academic areas.  Casual Academics are paid according to the category of their duties, as detailed in UQ’s Enterprise Agreement.   Casual Academic responsibilities may include (but are not limited to):  Lecturing/Teaching  Tutoring and Coordinating Tutoring  Assisting Course Coordinators  Marking  What We Can Offer  Current School of Social Science HDR students can apply to undertake casual academic positions for the courses listed on the School of Social Science casual academics webpage .  These are casual academic appointments. As such, there is no firm advance commitment to ongoing or indefinite work. The casual rate will vary depending on the type of work completed. Please view our various casual academic pay scales.    The marking rate for standard essays is 3,500 words per hour and the pay rate code is AC30A or AC30B.  For example, if marking 2,000 words for 18 students, then 2,000 x 18 / 3500 = 10 hours 17 mins. Initial marking hours are based upon expected enrolment figures, which often fluctuate in the first few weeks of semester. After census date, all marking is reassessed using census enrolment figures, which means that your allocated hours for marking may increase or decrease depending on student numbers.  For further information about UQ’s benefits, please visit Why Work at UQ and review  The University of Queensland Enterprise Agreement 2021-2026 (Enterprise Agreement).  Eligibility In the School of Social Science preference is given to our current HDR students and HDR students enrolled in the ISSR for employment opportunities. You must have work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.  We value diversity and inclusion, and actively encourage applications from those who bring diversity to the University. UQ’s Diversity and Inclusion webpage contains further information if you require additional support. Accessibility requirements and/or adjustments can be directed to recruitment@uq.edu.au . Want to Apply? All applicants will be asked to nominate their course and/or general subject area preference/s in the application form. In addition, all applicants must upload the following documents for an application to be considered: A copy of your current CV/resume A brief statement addressing: experience in tutoring/facilitating/marking undergraduate or postgraduate coursework; experience using online tools (Blackboard, and other digital tools, e.g., Zoom, Kaltura, or lecture recording software); and knowledge/expertise in the academic content of the course/s. Please note that you will be asked to add all documents into the one upload box during the application form. The submission of your application is appreciated as soon as possible however is due no later than 11.00 pm AEST on Sunday 10 January 2027. If you are a current employee of the University (including casual staff), or hold a HDR Scholarship with the University, please login to your staff Workday account and visit the internal careers board to apply for this opportunity. Please do NOT apply via the external job board."
    }
  },
